roves closure of regular languages under union and begins concatenation.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends Sipser's free lectures as the companion to the standard theory of computation textbook and a path to understanding how computers fundamentally work.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"theory of computation\",\"categories\":[\"Computability & complexity theory\",\"University lectures & courses\"],\"tags\":[\"finite automata\",\"regular expressions\",\"regular languages\",\"michael sipser\",\"mit ocw\"],\"quote\":\"Sipser also has corresponding lectures that are available for free on YouTube here\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"A0VYsiMtrNE\",\"title\":\"電磁祭囃子 in NEO TOKYO 🏮\",\"channel\":\"ELECTRONICOS FANTASTICOS!\",\"summary\":\"A short music video by the Japanese group Electronicos Fantasticos, led by Ei Wada, playing festival-style music on repurposed old electronics. Instruments include CRT TVs, a barcode reader, an electric fan harp and TV taiko drums.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN users share it as a delightful example of people making real music from obsolete electronics and self-built instruments.\",\"content_type\":\"music\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"electronic music from obsolete devices\",\"categories\":[\"Music performance & songs\",\"Embedded systems & DIY electronics\",\"Music theory, synthesis & production\"],\"tags\":[\"ei wada\",\"crt\",\"japanese music\",\"homemade instruments\",\"retro electronics\"],\"quote\":\"It's this band from Japan who make incredible music with instruments they made themselves.\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"ACkmg3Y64_s\",\"title\":\"Miniatur Wunderland *** official video *** largest model railway / railroad of the world\",\"channel\":\"Miniatur Wunderland\",\"summary\":\"The official tour video of Miniatur Wunderland in Hamburg, the world's largest model railway. It shows the 1,300 square meter layout with trains, aircraft, cars and ships moving around.\