    ",{\"vid\":\"CM0aohBfUTc\",\"title\":\"Veritasium: A Story of YouTube Propaganda\",\"channel\":\"Tom Nicholas\",\"summary\":\"Tom Nicholas critiques Veritasium's sponsored video on driverless cars, using it to examine YouTube sponsorships, science-channel credibility and how statistics and misinformation can be used in branded content.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN cites it in discussions of sponsored science content and Veritasium's credibility, usually as the go-to critique.\",\"content_type\":\"commentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"youtube sponsorship influence\",\"categories\":[\"Media, journalism & propaganda\",\"Internet culture & memes\",\"Self-driving & autonomous vehicles\"],\"tags\":[\"veritasium\",\"sponsored content\",\"self-driving cars\",\"science youtube\",\"misinformation\"],\"quote\":\"Tom Nicholas did a good job breaking down the problems with it\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"CUjt36SD3h8\",\"title\":\"Why does light slow down in water?\",\"channel\":\"Fermilab\",\"summary\":\"Fermilab's Don Lincoln explains why light travels slower in water and glass. He knocks down common wrong explanations such as absorption and re-emission, then gives the wave-mechanics reason tied to refractive index.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters link it to correct the widespread misconception about why light slows in a medium.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"refractive index\",\"categories\":[\"Fundamental physics\",\"Everyday physics & science demos\"],\"tags\":[\"speed of light\",\"optics\",\"physics misconceptions\",\"wave mechanics\",\"refraction\"],\"quote\":\"See this video for an explanation of why light travels slower through a medium with an index of refraction greater than one.\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"CXv1j3GbgLk\",\"title\":\"DEFCON 20: Hacker + Airplanes = No Good Can Come Of This\",\"channel\":\"Christiaan008\",\"summary\":\"A DEF CON 20 talk by RenderMan on the security weaknesses of ADS-B, the unencrypted, unauthenticated aircraft tracking broadcast. It shows how easily the data can be spoofed and what that means for security designed in too late.\",\"why_hn\":\"Cited whenever aviation or vehicle communication security comes up, as the standard demonstration that ADS-B is naively designed and trivially spoofable.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"ads-b aviation security\",\"categories\":[\"Security & hacking\",\"Aviation & aerospace engineering\"],\"tags\":[\"ads-b\",\"defcon\",\"spoofing\",\"aircraft tracking\",\"wireless security\"],\"quote\":\"Good watch if you wanna see how naively designed the whole thing is.\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"CaasbfdJdJg\",\"title\":\"Infinite fractions and the most irrational number\",\"channel\":\"Mathologer\",\"summary\":\"Mathologer uses continued fractions to show why the golden ratio is the most irrational number, comparing it with root 2, e and pi and pointing to where this shows up in nature.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as a clear, visual explanation of continued fractions and why phi is hardest to approximate by fractions.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"continued fractions\",\"categories\":[\"Recreational & visual math\",\"Pure mathematics\"],\"tags\":[\"golden ratio\",\"irrational numbers\",\"continued fractions\",\"mathologer\",\"number theory\"],\"quote\":\"Pretty good Mathologer video about continued fractions\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"CbHjcwIoTiY\",\"title\":\"WLW's 500,000 Watt Transmitter\",\"channel\":\"K7AGE\",\"summary\":\"A tour of the historic WLW transmitter facility in Mason, Ohio, recorded at Hamvention 2013. It shows the surviving equipment from the 500,000 watt AM era, when WLW was the only US station at that power, and explains the history.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as the definitive visual walkthrough of the WLW 500 kW transmitter, with detailed equipment views and lots of history.\",\"content_type\":\"documentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"am radio transmitters\",\"categories\":[\"Electronics & circuit design\",\"Audio & video engineering\"],\"tags\":[\"wlw\",\"high power broadcasting\",\"radio history\",\"ham radio\",\"transmitter tour\"],\"quote\":\"You get to see the high power equipment in detail accompanied by lots of history about the facility.\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"DYvhC_RdIwQ\",\"title\":\"I Have Delivered Value... But At What Cost?\",\"channel\":\"KRAZAM\",\"summary\":\"A short KRAZAM comedy sketch satirizing corporate tech life, where a worker reckons with having delivered value at the cost of his soul, complete with Slack chimes.\",\"why_hn\":\"Praised as painfully accurate office satire, with the channel's sketches called genius.\",\"content_type\":\"comedy\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"tech workplace satire\",\"categories\":[\"Satire & parody\",\"Software (other)\"],\"tags\":[\"krazam\",\"sketch\",\"office culture\",\"slack\",\"satire\"],\"quote\":\"The videos on that channel are so good that they're painful to watch\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"DlTq8DbRs4k\",\"title\":\"The UK's Failed Experiment in Rail Privatization\",\"channel\":\"Wendover Productions\",\"summary\":\"Wendover Productions explains how UK rail privatization worked and why it failed. It covers the fragmented operator and infrastructure structure and why market forces apply poorly to rail.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended in privatization and rail threads as a clear, well-sourced explanation of the UK rail mess.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"uk rail privatization\",\"categories\":[\"Transit, cities & urban planning\",\"Tech industry & business models\"],\"tags\":[\"uk rail\",\"privatization\",\"public transport\",\"wendover\",\"economics\"],\"quote\":\"the people on the Wendover YouTube channel did a video on this back in March which explained the issue really well.\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"DlkMs4ZHHr8\",\"title\":\"Infinite Mario AI - Long Level\",\"channel\":\"Robin Baumgarten\",\"summary\":\"A bot built by Robin Baumgarten plays Infinite Mario using A* path-finding, shown as a red planned path that updates as new obstacles appear. It won the Mario AI competitions and uses only on-screen information.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN points to it as a vivid demo that plain A* search can beat fancier machine-learning approaches in game AI.\",\"content_type\":\"demo\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"game ai pathfinding\",\"categories\":[\"Chess, board games & puzzles\",\"Algorithms & data structures\",\"Machine learning & deep learning\"],\"tags\":[\"a-star\",\"mario ai\",\"game ai\",\"path planning\",\"competition\"],\"quote\":\"It's pretty interesting, a lot of people wrote/are writing high falutin' machine learning algorithms\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"DwKPFT-RioU\",\"title\":\"The Fallen of World War II\",\"channel\":\"Neil Halloran\",\"summary\":\"Neil Halloran's animated, data-driven documentary visualizes the human cost of World War II and compares it with other wars and the trend toward fewer war deaths in recent decades.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN cites it for a sense of the scale of WWII casualties by country and the long decline in violence.\",\"content_type\":\"documentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"world war ii casualties\",\"categories\":[\"Data visualization & information design\",\"Military history & warfare\",\"World history\"],\"tags\":[\"data visualization\",\"war deaths\",\"wwii\",\"history\",\"decline of violence\"],\"quote\":\"does a great job of giving a sense of what the proportions are of relative tragedies in terms of loss of life.\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"EGqwXt90ZqA\",\"title\":\"Bret Victor - Inventing on Principle\",\"channel\":\"Colin McDonnell\",\"summary\":\"Bret Victor's talk from Dropbox's DBX conference about living by a guiding principle, in his case that creators need an immediate connection to what they are making. He shows live-coding and time-manipulation demos that let ideas take form at the speed of thought.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters cite it as the best demonstration of why instant feedback matters in programming tools.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"programming tools and live feedback\",\"categories\":[\"GUI, HCI & interface history\",\"Testing, debugging & developer tools\",\"Tech pioneers & interviews\"],\"tags\":[\"bret victor\",\"live programming\",\"instant feedback\",\"developer tools\",\"time travel debugging\"],\"quote\":\"Bret Victor's Inventing on Principle is probably the best demonstration of why this matters\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"EMKLeS-Uq_8\",\"title\":\"Hyperbolica: A Non-Euclidean Adventure [Old Trailer]\",\"channel\":\"CodeParade\",\"summary\":\"The old announcement trailer for Hyperbolica, CodeParade's Unity game set in hyperbolic and spherical space. The creator has since posted devlog videos on building it.\",\"why_hn\":\"Pointed to when non-Euclidean geometry or games in hyperbolic space come up, along with the creator's well-liked devlog.\",\"content_type\":\"demo\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"hyperbolic geometry games\",\"categories\":[\"Indie game development\",\"Pure mathematics\",\"Computer graphics & rendering\"],\"tags\":[\"hyperbolica\",\"non-euclidean\",\"game dev\",\"codeparade\",\"unity\"],\"quote\":\"CodeParade has been working on hyperbolica for a while now and doing fantastic videos on the process.\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"ESXOAJRdcwQ\",\"title\":\"Jailbreaking the Simulation with George Hotz | SXSW 2019\",\"channel\":\"SXSW\",\"summary\":\"George Hotz gives a SXSW 2019 talk on the idea that we live in a simulation and asks what actions would be available from inside it. He takes a hacker's view of whether it could be escaped.\",\"why_hn\":\"Shared in simulation-argument threads as an entertaining, hacker-minded take, though some commenters criticize Hotz himself.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"simulation hypothesis\",\"categories\":[\"Philosophy & ethics\",\"Security & hacking\",\"Tech pioneers & interviews\"],\"tags\":[\"george hotz\",\"simulation argument\",\"philosophy\",\"hacking\",\"sxsw\"],\"quote\":\"He's taking the hacker's perspective and exploring if the simulation would be hackable from inside.\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"F8sZRBdmqc0\",\"title\":\"Bryan Cantrill on ARC: A Self-Tuning, Low Overhead Replacement Cache [PWL SF] 10/2017\",\"channel\":\"PapersWeLove\",\"summary\":\"Bryan Cantrill presents the ARC (Adaptive Replacement Cache) paper by Megiddo and Modha at Papers We Love. He opens with a survey of caching strategies before digging into the self-tuning LRU/MFU design.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended in cache-algorithm threads as a fun, informative walk through caching strategies and ARC.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"cache replacement algorithms\",\"categories\":[\"Algorithms & data structures\",\"Databases & storage\",\"Software performance & optimization\"],\"tags\":[\"arc\",\"lru\",\"caching\",\"zfs\",\"bryan cantrill\",\"papers we love\"],\"quote\":\"Bryan Cantrill does a pretty good survey of caching strategies in the intro\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"FKTxC9pl-WM\",\"title\":\"Making Badass Developers - Kathy Sierra (Serious Pony) keynote\",\"channel\":\"O'Reilly\",\"summary\":\"Kathy Sierra's Fluent 2015 keynote on the one metaskill for developers: the ability to come up to speed quickly and stay there. She covers how people learn, cognitive resources versus willpower, and building skill.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters recommend it when asked how to learn better or keep improving.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"ge