 Read's 1958 essay I, Pencil, showing how no one person knows how to make a pencil and how many people cooperate without central planning.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as a quick video version of the classic essay on specialization and economic interdependence.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"specialization and supply chains\",\"categories\":[\"Microeconomics & behavioral economics\",\"Business & economics (other)\"],\"tags\":[\"i, pencil\",\"free markets\",\"interdependence\",\"economics\",\"leonard read\"],\"quote\":\"It's really about specialization and the interdependency of everything in the economy\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"Ie-gqwSHQr0\",\"title\":\"\\\"Building a Toolkit for Design\\\" by Matthew Griffith\",\"channel\":\"elm-conf\",\"summary\":\"Matthew Griffith, creator of elm-ui, presents a toolkit for designing layouts in Elm as an alternative to CSS. He explains the opinionated approach to layout and styling.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters recommend it as a fresh perspective on layout and styling in CSS discussions.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"elm-ui layout\",\"categories\":[\"Web development\",\"Functional programming\",\"UX & UI design\"],\"tags\":[\"elm\",\"elm-ui\",\"css alternative\",\"layout\",\"front-end\"],\"quote\":\"For those who want a fresh perspective on CSS, layout and styling, check out this talk by Matthew Griffith\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"Ijst4g5KFN0\",\"title\":\"26. Chernobyl — How It Happened\",\"channel\":\"MIT OpenCourseWare\",\"summary\":\"MIT 22.01 lecture by Michael Short explaining the Chernobyl accident technically, including RBMK reactor positive feedback, xenon poisoning and the cascade of human errors during the test.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as the detailed technical account of exactly what happened at Chernobyl.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"chernobyl accident\",\"categories\":[\"Nuclear power & fusion\",\"University lectures & courses\"],\"tags\":[\"chernobyl\",\"nuclear engineering\",\"rbmk reactor\",\"mit\",\"xenon poisoning\"],\"quote\":\"This is a presentation to students by an MIT professor that goes over exactly what happened, the sequence of events, mistakes made, and so on.\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"Iq9DzN6mvYA\",\"title\":\"Jake Vanderplas - Statistics for Hackers - PyCon 2016.mp4\",\"channel\":\"PyCon 2016\",\"summary\":\"Jake Vanderplas's PyCon 2016 talk shows how sampling-based methods such as simulation, shuffling and bootstrapping make statistics intuitive. His pitch is that if you can write a for-loop, you can do statistics.\",\"why_hn\":\"It is recommended to programmers who want to learn statistics through simulation rather than memorized formulas.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"statistics for programmers\",\"categories\":[\"Data science & statistics\",\"Python, Ruby & scripting languages\"],\"tags\":[\"bootstrapping\",\"resampling\",\"python\",\"simulation\",\"hypothesis testing\"],\"quote\":\"The presenters advocate using simulation/bootstrapping/shuffling methods instead of memorizing formulas.\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"JAcR7kqOb3o\",\"title\":\"Orbital Data Centers Yes or No\",\"channel\":\"Eager Space\",\"summary\":\"Eager Space examines the practicality of putting data centers in orbit, walking through three scenarios with cost calculations and the launch cost reductions that would be required.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended in orbital data center threads as a thorough, numbers-based assessment.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"orbital data centers\",\"categories\":[\"Space exploration & spaceflight\",\"Cloud, DevOps & reliability\"],\"tags\":[\"space data centers\",\"launch costs\",\"spaceflight economics\",\"thermal management\"],\"quote\":\"Goes into great detail with extensive calculations (for a YT video at least).\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"JFaOYYSxSEA\",\"title\":\"Classic Game Postmortem - Another World\",\"channel\":\"GDC Festival of Gaming\",\"summary\":\"Eric Chahi's GDC 2011 postmortem of Another World (Out of This World), describing how he built the game. He shows his polygon art approach, rotoscoping and animation tools.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN points to it whenever Another World comes up, as one of the better GDC talks by the game's creator.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"game development postmortem\",\"categories\":[\"Game history & retro gaming\",\"Game design\",\"Animation & visual effects\"],\"tags\":[\"another world\",\"eric chahi\",\"gdc\",\"rotoscoping\",\"retro games\",\"polygon graphics\"],\"quote\":\"it's one of the better GDC talks\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"JdxkVQy7QLM\",\"title\":\"Pachelbel Rant\",\"channel\":\"RobPRocks\",\"summary\":\"Comedian and cellist Rob Paravonian rants about having to play Pachelbel's Canon in D, and shows how many pop songs reuse its chord progression. It was recorded live at Penn State.\",\"why_hn\":\"Brought up in threads about chord progressions and musical copyright as the funny canonical version of the idea.\",\"content_type\":\"comedy\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"pachelbel chord progression\",\"categories\":[\"Stand-up & sketch comedy\",\"Music theory, synthesis & production\",\"Music performance & songs\"],\"tags\":[\"pachelbel canon\",\"stand-up\",\"cello\",\"chord progressions\",\"pop music\"],\"quote\":\"There was a relevant and entertaining stand-up bit about this\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"Jkiij9dJfcw\",\"title\":\"Denmark's Forest Kindergartens\",\"channel\":\"SBS Dateline\",\"summary\":\"An SBS Dateline short documentary on Danish forest kindergartens, where children play freely outdoors, climb trees and use knives with little adult intervention.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as a concise look at the culture behind outdoor kindergartens in threads on parenting and education.\",\"content_type\":\"documentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"outdoor kindergartens\",\"categories\":[\"Education & learning\",\"Social issues & culture\"],\"tags\":[\"forest school\",\"denmark\",\"early education\",\"parenting\",\"free play\"],\"quote\":\"This is a very concise (11:32) video that goes over some of the culture and thinking around outdoor kindergartens.\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"JmwbYl6f11c\",\"title\":\"How to Survive in Gamedev for Eleven Years Without a Hit\",\"channel\":\"GDC Festival of Gaming\",\"summary\":\"Jake Birkett of Grey Alien Games explains at GDC 2016 how an indie developer survived eleven years without a hit by building a long tail of games and redefining success.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended in indie and solo game development threads as a grounded account of making a living in games.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"indie game business\",\"categories\":[\"Indie game development\",\"Game design\"],\"tags\":[\"indie games\",\"gdc\",\"long tail\",\"game sales\",\"solo developer\"],\"quote\":\"you can redefine your idea of \\\"success\\\" a little and make a comfortable living\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"JsVtHqICeKE\",\"title\":\"DEFCON 19: Steal Everything, Kill Everyone, Cause Total Financial Ruin! (w speaker)\",\"channel\":\"Christiaan008\",\"summary\":\"Jayson E. Street's DEF CON 19 talk shows photos from real security engagements where he was already inside client facilities, illustrating how social engineering and physical intrusion can cause huge losses. For each attack he also explains what defenses would have stopped him.\",\"why_hn\":\"It is a repeat pick in 'favorite tech talk' threads as a funny, eye-opening look at how easily physical security and people can be bypassed.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"social engineering and physical security\",\"categories\":[\"Security & hacking\",\"Privacy, surveillance & cybercrime\"],\"tags\":[\"def con\",\"social engineering\",\"red team\",\"penetration testing\",\"security awareness\"],\"quote\":\"The absolute funniest tech talk I've seen\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"JurplDfPi3U\",\"title\":\"Former fusion scientist on why we won't have fusion power by 2040\",\"channel\":\"Improbable Matter\",\"summary\":\"A former fusion scientist argues why fusion power by 2040 is unrealistic, covering the physics challenges, neutron issues and optimistic company claims, with a brief note of good news.\",\"why_hn\":\"Praised as the best crash course on fusion reactor design nuances from an insider, used to temper fusion hype.\",\"content_type\":\"commentary\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"nuclear fusion feasibility\",\"categories\":[\"Nuclear power & fusion\"],\"tags\":[\"fusion power\",\"tokamak\",\"neutrons\",\"energy\",\"reactor design\"],\"quote\":\"I find it's the best crash course on the nuances of reactor design that I've seen.\",\"recommenders\":1,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"JxI3Eu5DPwE\",\"title\":\"Bob Nystrom - Is There More to Game Architecture than ECS?\",\"channel\":\"Roguelike Celebration\",\"summary\":\"Bob Nystrom, author of Game Programming Patterns, gives a Roguelike Celebration 2018 talk questioning whether entity component systems are the only way to structure a game. He walks through alternative architectures drawn from roguelike development.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters cite it as a level-headed counterpoint to ECS zealotry and a good intro to game architecture beyond ECS.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"game architecture\",\"categories\":[\"Game programming & engines\",\"Software architecture & design\"],\"tags\":[\"ecs\",\"roguelike\",\"game programming patterns\",\"software design\",\"entity systems\"],\"quote\":\"Bob Nystrom has an excellent talk on roguelike architecture [0]\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"KDpNQQqdSh8\",\"title\":\"The Secret Life of Machines - Vacuum Cleaners  1/2\",\"channel\":\"Buddy Runyan\",\"summary\":\"Part 1 of an episode of the British educational TV series The Secret Life of Machines, about how vacuum cleaners work and their history. It mixes humor with explanation of household technology.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends the series as an entertaining way to learn how everyday machines work, enough to start debugging them.\",\"content_type\":\"documentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"household appliances\",\"categories\":[\"Mechanical & precision engineering\",\"Home, DIY & practical skills\",\"Engineering & making (other)\"],\"tags\":[\"vacuum cleaners\",\"how machines work\",\"tim hunkin\",\"british tv\",\"engineering history\"],\"quote\":\"These overviews give you just enough knowledge to start debugging a broken household machine.\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"KEoGrbKAyKE\",\"title\":\"Who Killed Captain Alex: Uganda's First Action Movie (English Subtitles & Video Joker) - Wakaliwood\",\"channel\":\"Official Wakaliwood\",\"summary\":\"Who Killed Captain Alex is Uganda's first action movie, made for under \$200 by Nabwana IGG in Wakaliga. This version has English subtitles and a video joker (VJ Emmie) providing running commentary.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as a fun, surprisingly entertaining low-budget film made better by the live-style commentary.\",\"content_type\":\"other\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"low-budget filmmaking\",\"categories\":[\"Filmmaking & video production\",\"Documentaries & storytelling\"],\"tags\":[\"wakaliwood\",\"uganda\",\"action movie\",\"video joker\",\"indie film\"],\"quote\":\"Despite the very low budget, the movie is surprisingly entertaining, and the commentary makes it even better.\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"KINIAgRpkDA\",\"title\":\"GopherCon 2016: The Design of the Go Assembler - Rob Pike\",\"channel\":\"Gopher Academy\",\"summary\":\"Rob Pike's GopherCon 2016 talk on the design of the Go assembler, explaining why Go uses a common assembly language translated to each architecture.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN points to it for background on how the Go toolchain targets many architectures and why its assembler is designed that way.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"advanced\",\"primary_topic\":\"go assembler design\",\"categories\":[\"C, C++, Rust, Go & systems languages\",\"Compilers, interpreters & program analysis\"],\"tags\":[\"go\",\"assembler\",\"compilers\",\"rob pike\",\"toolchain portability\"],\"quote\":\"Its Rob Pike talking about the go assembler and he answers a few of your questions.\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"KJPdbp1An2s\",\"title\":\"Sam Aaron live coding a DJ set with Sonic Pi\",\"channel\":\"Sam Aaron\",\"summary\":\"A long live-coded DJ set in which Sam Aaron creates all the music in real time by writing code in his Sonic Pi system. It demonstrates algorithmic electronic music performance.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN people recommend it as the most impressive demonstration of live coding music, especially to those curious about programmable music.\",\"content_type\":\"demo\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"live coding music\",\"categories\":[\"Music theory, synthesis & production\",\"Creative coding & generative art\",\"Music performance & songs\"],\"tags\":[\"sonic pi\",\"live coding\",\"algorave\",\"electronic music\",\"ruby\"],\"quote\":\"The most impressive thing you can do with it is live coding\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"KNWh6Kw3ejQ\",\"title\":\"Adam Ruins Everything - The Truth About the McDonald's Coffee Lawsuit\",\"channel\":\"truTV\",\"summary\":\"An Adam Ruins Everything segment on the real facts of the McDonald's hot coffee lawsuit, covering the severity of the burns and the corporate PR campaign that painted it as frivolous.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN cites it as a quick, entertaining explainer to correct the myth of the frivolous coffee lawsuit.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"mcdonald's coffee lawsuit\",\"categories\":[\"Law, policing & justice\",\"Media, journalism & propaganda\"],\"tags\":[\"tort reform\",\"legal myths\",\"corporate pr\",\"adam conover\",\"product liability\"],\"quote\":null,\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"K_nnUbX7uuQ\",\"title\":\"The Secrets Of Cornwall - Part 1 - Communications\",\"channel\":\"Mark Thomas\",\"summary\":\"A documentary on how Cornwall became a hub for the world's communications, from the first transatlantic radio signal to modern undersea fibre cables. It describes how cable landing stations and the systems are put together.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as a rare look at the unmarked buildings and infrastructure where undersea cables come ashore.\",\"content_type\":\"documentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"undersea cable infrastructure\",\"categories\":[\"Networking & internet infrastructure\",\"Documentaries & storytelling\"],\"tags\":[\"cornwall\",\"submarine cables\",\"fibre optic\",\"landing stations\",\"telecom\"],\"quote\":\"Documentary about the cable landing stations: The Secrets of Cornwall\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"K_oET45GzMI\",\"title\":\"Black Wednesday (BBC 1997)\",\"channel\":\"batrachious\",\"summary\":\"A 1997 BBC documentary on Black Wednesday in 1992, when sterling was forced out of the Exchange Rate Mechanism. It covers the crisis from the British government side, including George Soros's trade and political mistakes.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as a detailed documentary on the Soros-versus-Bank-of-England episode.\",\"content_type\":\"documentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"black wednesday currency crisis\",\"categories\":[\"Macroeconomics & financial crises\",\"Documentaries & storytelling\",\"Investing & personal finance\"],\"tags\":[\"george soros\",\"pound sterling\",\"exchange rate mechanism\",\"bank of england\",\"bbc\"],\"quote\":\"This wonderful BBC documentary explains everything on detail.\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"KdnGPQaICjk\",\"title\":\"Computers for Cynics 0 - The Myth of Technology\",\"channel\":\"TheTedNelson\",\"summary\":\"The opening episode of Ted Nelson's Computers for Cynics series, in which he casts doubt on conventional accounts of computer basics. He argues that conventions and packaging are mistaken for technology.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN likes it for Nelson's habit of reframing computing history so that past decisions look arbitrary.\",\"content_type\":\"commentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"computing history\",\"categories\":[\"History of computing\",\"Tech pioneers & interviews\",\"GUI, HCI & interface history\"],\"tags\":[\"ted nelson\",\"history of computing\",\"user interfaces\",\"hypertext\",\"critique of technology\"],\"quote\":\"Ted has a knack for reframing things in ways that make the arbitrariness of certain historical decisions clear\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0}",
    ",{\"vid\":\"KeLBd2EJLOU\",\"title\":\"C++ and Beyond 2012: Herb Sutter - atomic Weapons 2 of 2\",\"channel\":\"alexsmail\",\"summary\":\"Part two of Herb Sutter's C++ and Beyond 2012 session on atomic weapons, a deep dive into the C++ memory model, atomics, memory ordering, and hardware behavior. It ends with the modern meaning of volatile.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as an in-depth, practical introduction to atomics and weak versus strong memory ordering, relevant to ARM and Rust as well as C++.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"advanced\",\"primary_topic\":\"c++ memory model\",\"categories\":[\"C, C++, Rust, Go & systems languages\",\"Computer architecture & CPUs\"],\"tags\":[\"atomics\",\"memory ordering\",\"acquire release\",\"concurrency\",\"herb sutter\"],\"quote\":\"For a more in depth introduction to the topic with a lot of practical code examples, I highly recommend\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"L3LMbpZIKhQ\",\"title\":\"Lec 1 | MIT 6.042J Mathematics for Computer Science, Fall 2010\",\"channel\":\"MIT OpenCourseWare\",\"summary\":\"The first lecture of MIT 6.042J Mathematics for Computer Science, taught by Tom Leighton, introducing mathematical proofs. It covers propositions, implication and basic proof methods.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN users recommend it as a good discrete math course for programmers, praising Leighton's relatable stories that motivate new concepts.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"mathematical proofs\",\"categories\":[\"University lectures & courses\",\"Pure mathematics\",\"Logic & formal methods\"],\"tags\":[\"discrete math\",\"proofs\",\"mit ocw\",\"tom leighton\",\"computer science\"],\"quote\":\"the professor tells relatable stories to motivate the new concepts\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"LNidsMesxSE\",\"title\":\"Animation Bootcamp: An Indie Approach to Procedural Animation\",\"channel\":\"GDC Festival of Gaming\",\"summary\":\"David Rosen of Wolfire Games explains how simple procedural techniques can produce fluid, interactive character animation from very few keyframes. Examples come from Overgrowth, Receiver and Black Shades, from his 2014 GDC session.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN points to it as a concrete case of procedural animation that keeps characters responsive, often in threads about Overgrowth and game feel.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"procedural animation\",\"categories\":[\"Game programming & engines\",\"Indie game development\",\"Animation & visual effects\"],\"tags\":[\"procedural animation\",\"inverse kinematics\",\"indie games\",\"overgrowth\",\"game feel\"],\"quote\":\"Here's a video I've enjoyed of David Rosen giving a presentation at Game Developers Conference 2014\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"LUFp6sjKbkE\",\"title\":\"50TH ANNIVERSARY OF THE COMPUTER SCIENCE DEPARTMENT AT THE UNIVERSITY OF UTAH\",\"channel\":\"University of Utah\",\"summary\":\"A full-day recording of the University of Utah computer science department's 50th anniversary event with panels, keynotes and talks by alumni and pioneers. It includes Ivan Sutherland's presentation on single flux quantum electronics.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN points to specific segments, especially Sutherland's SFQ talk and graphics pioneers, rather than the full seven hours.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"computer science history\",\"categories\":[\"History of computing\",\"Computer graphics & rendering\"],\"tags\":[\"university of utah\",\"ivan sutherland\",\"single flux quantum\",\"computer graphics\",\"panel\"],\"quote\":\"It's a very interesting presentation, from the history to the mechanics of how it works, to the possibilities.\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"LZgyVadkgmI\",\"title\":\"Lichess founder Thibault Duplessis lectures on Lichess (2017)\",\"channel\":\"Lichess\",\"summary\":\"Lichess founder Thibault Duplessis talks at Big Techday 10 in Munich about how Lichess works and how he built it. He covers the project's origins and technical challenges including cheating detection.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters point to it as a good talk on how one person built a major free, open-source chess server with few resources, and on the cheating problem.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"building lichess\",\"categories\":[\"Chess, board games & puzzles\",\"Software practice, open source & careers\",\"Software architecture & design\"],\"tags\":[\"lichess\",\"chess\",\"open source\",\"cheat detection\",\"scala\",\"solo developer\"],\"quote\":\"He has a good talk about how he did it here\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"LfascZSTU4o\",\"title\":\"8/28/2006 Peter Schiff On Kudlow & Co: Predicts The US Economic Collapse\",\"channel\":\"PhilDeCarolis\",\"summary\":\"A 2006 television appearance in which Peter Schiff warns on Kudlow & Co about the housing bubble and an approaching US economic collapse.\",\"why_hn\":\"Offered as an example of someone who publicly predicted the financial crisis ahead of time, in arguments about economic forecasting.\",\"content_type\":\"interview\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"2008 financial crisis prediction\",\"categories\":[\"Macroeconomics & financial crises\",\"Media, journalism & propaganda\"],\"tags\":[\"peter schiff\",\"housing bubble\",\"financial crisis\",\"economic forecasting\"],\"quote\":\"There have been people like Peter Schiff warning about the recession and specifically saying when it will happen and how long it will last\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"Lq-Y7crQo44\",\"title\":\"Algorithmic Redistricting: Elections made-to-order\",\"channel\":\"AlphaPhoenix\",\"summary\":\"AlphaPhoenix uses Monte Carlo simulation and annealing-style methods to draw North Carolina congressional district maps, both gerrymandered and fair. He focuses on the fascinating computational problem rather than the politics.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as an entertaining demonstration of how powerful algorithmic gerrymandering can be.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"algorithmic gerrymandering\",\"categories\":[\"US politics & elections\",\"Algorithms & data structures\"],\"tags\":[\"gerrymandering\",\"monte carlo\",\"simulation\",\"redistricting\",\"elections\"],\"quote\":\"For a great (and entertaining!) look into how you can use simulations to generate these types of maps\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"LruaD7XhQ50\",\"title\":\"A Third of America Belongs to America\",\"channel\":\"CGP Grey\",\"summary\":\"CGP Grey explains what it means that a third of the United States is federally owned land. It describes the agencies that manage it and why so much of it is in the western states.\",\"why_hn\":\"Offered as the quick, clear explainer on US federal land ownership whenever land use in the West comes up.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"us federal land\",\"categories\":[\"Politics & history (other)\",\"Environment, agriculture & food systems\"],\"tags\":[\"federal land\",\"blm\",\"national parks\",\"land ownership\",\"united states\"],\"quote\":\"You might enjoy CGP Grey's \\\"What is federal land?\\\" [0].\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"LsGZ_2RuJ2A\",\"title\":\"Salad Mug - DYNAMO DREAM, Ep1\",\"channel\":\"IanHubert\",\"summary\":\"The first episode of Ian Hubert's Dynamo Dream series, a one-person sci-fi short made in Blender with a gritty futuristic city setting. It was made with help from friends and Patreon supporters over about three years.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN holds it up as an impressive example of what a tiny team can achieve with Blender.\",\"content_type\":\"demo\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"blender short film\",\"categories\":[\"Animation & visual effects\",\"Filmmaking & video production\"],\"tags\":[\"blender\",\"ian hubert\",\"3d animation\",\"indie film\",\"vfx\"],\"quote\":\"I always post this link when Blender is discussed because it's so impressive.\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"LwicN2u6Dro\",\"title\":\"\\\"Liberating the Smalltalk lurking in C and Unix\\\" by Stephen Kell\",\"channel\":\"Strange Loop Conference\",\"summary\":\"Stephen Kell's Strange Loop talk argues that C and Unix already contain Smalltalk-like dynamism and introspection that can be exposed. He presents his liballocs research on bridging dynamic and compiled languages.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters recommend it in Smalltalk and Unix threads as a demonstration of runtime dynamism hiding in native binaries and shared libraries.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"advanced\",\"primary_topic\":\"dynamic features in c and unix\",\"categories\":[\"Programming language design\",\"Operating systems & kernels\",\"C, C++, Rust, Go & systems languages\"],\"tags\":[\"stephen kell\",\"smalltalk\",\"liballocs\",\"c\",\"unix\",\"ffi\"],\"quote\":\"there's a great video of one of his talks\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"MCVj5RZOqwY\",\"title\":\"Avi Bryant demos Dabble db\",\"channel\":\"Tavis Rudd\",\"summary\":\"Avi Bryant demos Dabble DB, a web-based spreadsheet-like database for building apps without code. It shows how users can model and relate data through a smooth interface.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN points to it as a lost example of intuitive low-code tooling, and as inspiration for newer products.\",\"content_type\":\"demo\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"low-code databases\",\"categories\":[\"Databases & storage\",\"GUI, HCI & interface history\",\"UX & UI design\"],\"tags\":[\"dabble db\",\"smalltalk\",\"web apps\",\"user interface\",\"data modeling\"],\"quote\":\"The UI was very intuitive, and it did a better job with things like table relations than it's competitors.\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"MEUWxNifJJ8\",\"title\":\"Yanis Varoufakis: The Global Minotaur: America, Europe and the Future of the Global Economy\",\"channel\":\"SkriptaTV\",\"summary\":\"Yanis Varoufakis talks at a festival in Zagreb about his book The Global Minotaur. He argues the postwar global economy and the 2008 crisis stem from deeper structural causes than greed or weak regulation.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as a narrative of a century of political economy and the geopolitics behind the 2008 crash.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"political economy\",\"categories\":[\"Macroeconomics & financial crises\"],\"tags\":[\"varoufakis\",\"2008 financial crisis\",\"global economy\",\"macroeconomics\",\"europe\"],\"quote\":\"a nice narrative of the last 100 years of the political economy\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"MHTizZ_XcUM\",\"title\":\"Automatic Bullseye, MOVING Dartboard\",\"channel\":\"Mark Rober\",\"summary\":\"Mark Rober shows an automatic bullseye dartboard that tracks a thrown dart with motion capture and moves in real time so it always hits the center. He spent over three years on the project.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN calls it a great, impressive engineering project involving real-time trajectory calculation.\",\"content_type\":\"demo\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"engineering demo\",\"categories\":[\"Mechanical & precision engineering\",\"Robotics\"],\"tags\":[\"darts\",\"motion capture\",\"mark rober\",\"real-time control\",\"trajectory\"],\"quote\":\"Another great video is his automatic bulls eye dartboard [2].\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"MShbP3OpASA\",\"title\":\"Aalto Talk with Linus Torvalds [Full-length]\",\"channel\":\"aaltouniversityace\",\"summary\":\"Linus Torvalds is interviewed at Aalto University in 2012, followed by an audience Q&A. He talks about Linux, kernel development and the desktop, and about Nvidia support. The Q&A has a well-known moment where he is blunt about Nvidia.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN links it for individual clips: the Nvidia moment, his views on C and his blunt communication style. Some commenters say the whole talk is worth watching.\",\"content_type\":\"interview\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"linus torvalds on linux\",\"categories\":[\"Tech pioneers & interviews\",\"Linux & open-source ecosystems\"],\"tags\":[\"linus torvalds\",\"linux kernel\",\"nvidia\",\"c language\",\"open source\"],\"quote\":\"the whole talk by itself is very recommendable\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"M_VuXVErD6E\",\"title\":\"Design Your Own Keyboard!\",\"channel\":\"Ben Vallack\",\"summary\":\"Ben Vallack walks through designing a custom keyboard PCB from scratch using Ergogen and KiCad, ending with a gerber file ready to order from a PCB fabricator. The example is a split keyboard.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as a thorough, general walkthrough of the steps in designing your own keyboard.\",\"content_type\":\"tutorial\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"custom keyboard pcb design\",\"categories\":[\"Electronics & circuit design\",\"Embedded systems & DIY electronics\",\"Consumer devices & gadgets\"],\"tags\":[\"ergogen\",\"kicad\",\"split keyboard\",\"pcb\",\"mechanical keyboards\"],\"quote\":\"It's targeting a split keyboard, but I think it details the general steps involved pretty well.\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"MgrO5za0lSY\",\"title\":\"Francis Bacon: A Brush with Violence (2017)\",\"channel\":\"Wei Yu\",\"summary\":\"A BBC documentary on Francis Bacon, the loud, hard-drinking British painter, drawing on people who knew him. It explores the haunted man behind the Roaring Boy persona.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters recommend it as a well-made, interesting and sad documentary on Bacon's troubled life.\",\"content_type\":\"documentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"francis bacon painter\",\"categories\":[\"Visual arts, photography & architecture\",\"Documentaries & storytelling\"],\"tags\":[\"francis bacon\",\"art documentary\",\"british art\",\"biography\",\"painting\"],\"quote\":\"Pretty interesting and sad at the same time. It's worth a watch, IMO.\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"MlhAkNWC1qo\",\"title\":\"David Heinemeier Hansson-Unlearn Your MBA (Entire talk)\",\"channel\":\"Entrepreneurship.org\",\"summary\":\"David Heinemeier Hansson, creator of Ruby on Rails and partner at 37signals, argues that planning is guessing and startups should focus on today. He says constraints are an advantage and that startups can bootstrap profitably in sizable markets.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as the classic counterpoint to Silicon Valley growth-at-all-costs startup thinking.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"bootstrapped startup philosophy\",\"categories\":[\"Startups & entrepreneurship\",\"Management, leadership & organizations\"],\"tags\":[\"dhh\",\"37signals\",\"bootstrapping\",\"startups\",\"mba critique\",\"profitability\"],\"quote\":\"His talk \\\"How to unlearn your MBA\\\" in particular was fantastic in providing a counterpoint to Silicon-valley style Startups\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"Mlrrc_vy79E\",\"title\":\"Diablo: Original Game Development\",\"channel\":\"jon d\",\"summary\":\"David Brevik's GDC 2016 post-mortem of the original Diablo, covering how it evolved from a turn-based claymation DOS game into the genre-defining action RPG. It includes development anecdotes and lessons learned.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN considers it one of the best GDC post-mortems, with funny anecdotes (such as turning down Hotmail equity) and multiplayer insights.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"game development postmortem\",\"categories\":[\"Game design\",\"Game history & retro gaming\",\"Indie game development\"],\"tags\":[\"diablo\",\"gdc\",\"blizzard\",\"postmortem\",\"game design\"],\"quote\":\"GDC post mortems are amazing, especially the ones on Diablo[0] and Fallout[1].\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"Mvse4Xhzwuk\",\"title\":\"The Long, Twisted and Slightly Ridiculous Story of Avgas Part 2\",\"channel\":\"AVweb\",\"summary\":\"AVweb's Paul Bertorelli explains in part two of a series why general aviation has not replaced leaded avgas. He covers industry inertia, bureaucratic delays and the exemption that lets the industry keep using lead.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends the series for explaining why unleaded avgas has been so hard to approve, usually in threads about FAA and EPA fuel decisions.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"leaded aviation fuel\",\"categories\":[\"Aviation & aerospace engineering\",\"Law, policing & justice\"],\"tags\":[\"avgas\",\"general aviation\",\"faa\",\"unleaded fuel\",\"regulation\"],\"quote\":\"AVWeb made 2 videos on the subject of leaded avgas and on how hard it is to get it approved.\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"N4UFC0y1tY0\",\"title\":\"Put Your Name on Your Game, a Talk by Bennett Foddy and Zach Gage\",\"channel\":\"GDC Festival of Gaming\",\"summary\":\"At GDC 2019, Bennett Foddy and Zach Gage ask why game developers rarely sign their work. They argue that indie developers could benefit from putting their names on their games.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it when people ask why games aren't marketed by creator like films, and for the value of attaching your name to your work.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"indie game branding\",\"categories\":[\"Indie game development\",\"Game design\"],\"tags\":[\"bennett foddy\",\"zach gage\",\"indie games\",\"authorship\",\"gdc\"],\"quote\":\"Great talk on that from the GettingOverIt/QWOP dev\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"N8elxpSu9pw\",\"title\":\"I wrote a Raytracer for DOS, 16 VGA colors\",\"channel\":\"Bisqwit\",\"summary\":\"Bisqwit writes a raytracer from scratch in C++ for DOS, rendering in the 16-color VGA palette at 640x480. The video also covers color quantization and ordered dithering.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN fans praise Bisqwit as a favorite programming YouTuber and this video as an excellent look at dithering.\",\"content_type\":\"tutorial\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"raytracing and dithering\",\"categories\":[\"Computer graphics & rendering\",\"Demoscene & retrocomputing\",\"C, C++, Rust, Go & systems languages\"],\"tags\":[\"raytracer\",\"dithering\",\"c++\",\"dos\",\"vga\"],\"quote\":\"Bisqwit's discussion of dithering is outstanding\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"N9RUqGYuGfw\",\"title\":\"JSON Parser 100% From Scratch in Haskell (only 111 lines)\",\"channel\":\"Tsoding\",\"summary\":\"Tsoding live-codes a JSON parser from scratch in Haskell in about 111 lines, using only the standard library. It is an edited Twitch stream that explains the intuition behind parser combinators, functors, applicatives and monads.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as a hands-on way to understand Haskell and parser combinators by watching a parser get built live.\",\"content_type\":\"tutorial\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"haskell parser combinators\",\"categories\":[\"Functional programming\",\"Compilers, interpreters & program analysis\"],\"tags\":[\"haskell\",\"json parser\",\"functional programming\",\"monads\",\"live coding\"],\"quote\":\"this guy writes a json parser live, giving his intuition along the way. It's helped me immensely.\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"NAh9oLs67Cw\",\"title\":\"07/27/1978\",\"channel\":\"lasagnacat\",\"summary\":\"An hour-long absurdist Lasagna Cat video in which John Blyth Barrymore muses philosophically on a single Garfield strip. It is set to the full score of Philip Glass's Kundun.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN cites it as the prime example of going absurdly deep on a tiny subject, and as a favorite among long YouTube videos.\",\"content_type\":\"comedy\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"garfield absurdist analysis\",\"categories\":[\"Arts & entertainment (other)\",\"Satire & parody\",\"Philosophy & ethics\"],\"tags\":[\"lasagna cat\",\"garfield\",\"philip glass\",\"absurdism\",\"long videos\"],\"quote\":\"I love this deep absurd analysis of a single Garfield comic.\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"NbpZCSf4_Yk\",\"title\":\"Exclusive: Metro Exodus Enhanced Edition Analysis - The First Triple-A Game Built Around Ray Tracing\",\"channel\":\"Digital Foundry\",\"summary\":\"Digital Foundry's Alex Battaglia analyzes Metro Exodus Enhanced Edition, the first AAA game built around ray tracing. It covers the new global illumination, emissive lighting, DLSS and performance on AMD and Nvidia hardware.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as a good overview of what ray-traced lighting changes for developers and players.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"real-time ray tracing\",\"categories\":[\"Computer graphics & rendering\",\"Game programming & engines\",\"Gaming culture & analysis\"],\"tags\":[\"ray tracing\",\"global illumination\",\"dlss\",\"game graphics\",\"metro exodus\"],\"quote\":\"is a good overview of why this is such a huge leap forward for developers and players alike.\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"NfizT369g60\",\"title\":\"Procedural World Generation of Ubisoft’s Far Cry 5 | Etienne Carrier | Houdini HIVE Utrecht\",\"channel\":\"Houdini\",\"summary\":\"Ubisoft procedural technical artist Etienne Carrier explains the Houdini-based tools used to build Far Cry 5's roughly 100 square km open world. It covers generating biomes, texturing terrain, freshwater networks, cliff rocks and vegetation placement.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN points people to it as a rare, concrete look at how a AAA studio uses procedural tools to fill huge worlds with natural-looking environments.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"procedural world generation\",\"categories\":[\"Computer graphics & rendering\",\"Game programming & engines\"],\"tags\":[\"houdini\",\"far cry 5\",\"procedural generation\",\"terrain\",\"game development\"],\"quote\":\"You may enjoy this talk, the devs for Far Cry 5 procedurally generated quite a lot of their world\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"O45LaFsaqMA\",\"title\":\"What if Current Foundations of Mathematics are Inconsistent? | Vladimir Voevodsky\",\"channel\":\"Institute for Advanced Study\",\"summary\":\"Vladimir Voevodsky's IAS lecture starts from Godel's second incompleteness theorem to explore whether first-order arithmetic could be inconsistent. It motivates his vision of univalent foundations and computer-checked proofs.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as a thought-provoking lecture from a Fields medalist on the foundations of mathematics and formal proof verification.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"advanced\",\"primary_topic\":\"foundations of mathematics\",\"categories\":[\"Logic & formal methods\",\"Pure mathematics\"],\"tags\":[\"voevodsky\",\"incompleteness\",\"univalent foundations\",\"proof assistants\",\"logic\"],\"quote\":\"I recommend this video\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"OBPpRqxY8Uw\",\"title\":\"Leonard Susskind | \\\"ER = EPR\\\" or \\\"What's Behind the Horizons of Black Holes?\\\" - 1 of 2\",\"channel\":\"Stanford Institute for Theoretical Physics\",\"summary\":\"Stanford physicist Leonard Susskind gives the first of two mini-lectures on \\\"ER = EPR\\\", the idea that quantum entanglement and wormholes are related. It covers what lies behind black hole horizons.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN people point to it as the more detailed follow-up when articles touch on entanglement, holography and black holes.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"advanced\",\"primary_topic\":\"black holes and entanglement\",\"categories\":[\"Quantum physics\",\"Cosmology & astrophysics\"],\"tags\":[\"er=epr\",\"black holes\",\"entanglement\",\"holography\",\"wormholes\"],\"quote\":\"I found these lectures by Leonard Susskind very insightful\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"OFgxAFdxYAQ\",\"title\":\"A Crash Course in Modern Hardware by Cliff Click\",\"channel\":\"Devoxx\",\"summary\":\"Cliff Click walks through a small performance example on a modern out-of-order CPU. He covers pipelining, ILP, CISC vs RISC, multicore, and why cache misses and the memory subsystem dominate performance.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as an accessible refresher on how modern hardware and caches affect software performance.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"modern cpu architecture and performance\",\"categories\":[\"Computer architecture & CPUs\",\"Software performance & optimization\"],\"tags\":[\"cpu caches\",\"out-of-order execution\",\"memory performance\",\"jvm\",\"multicore\"],\"quote\":\"it's a really accessible refresher if you're a bit outdated on how all these caches work and interact\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"OHPkdMGI6D4\",\"title\":\"VFX Artists React to DUNE Bad & Great CGi\",\"channel\":\"Corridor Crew\",\"summary\":\"Corridor Crew VFX artists react to the visual effects of the 2021 Dune and compare them with David Lynch's 1984 version. They discuss which effects were practical and which were CGI.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters cite it in debates about practical effects versus CGI, as a fun comparison of old and new Dune.\",\"content_type\":\"commentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"vfx practical vs cgi\",\"categories\":[\"Animation & visual effects\",\"Film analysis & criticism\"],\"tags\":[\"dune\",\"vfx\",\"cgi\",\"practical effects\",\"film\",\"corridor crew\"],\"quote\":\"a really good example of this is this comparison between the old and new Dune movies\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"OINAk2xl8Bc\",\"title\":\"Why Japan's Conviction Rate is 99% (Actually Explained)\",\"channel\":\"That Japanese Man Yuta\",\"summary\":\"Yuta explains what Japan's 99% conviction rate actually means, noting that