to argue that systems rather than bad apples drive cruelty, and for its section on how anonymity changes behavior.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"social psychology of evil\",\"categories\":[\"Psychology & human behavior\",\"Philosophy & ethics\"],\"tags\":[\"zimbardo\",\"stanford prison experiment\",\"abu ghraib\",\"anonymity\",\"situationism\",\"ted\"],\"quote\":\"Here's Zimbardo's TED talk on what makes people do evil\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"OyFJWRnt_AY\",\"title\":\"CS480/680 Lecture 19: Attention and Transformer Networks\",\"channel\":\"Pascal Poupart\",\"summary\":\"A university lecture from CS480/680 by Pascal Poupart on attention mechanisms and transformer networks, worked through on a chalkboard.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it to newcomers as a clear explanation of attention, including the query/key/value intuition.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"transformers and attention\",\"categories\":[\"LLMs & generative AI\",\"Machine learning & deep learning\",\"University lectures & courses\"],\"tags\":[\"attention\",\"transformers\",\"query key value\",\"deep learning\",\"nlp\"],\"quote\":\"I would also recommend Pascal Poupart's talk [1] on Attention and Transformers.\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"P1ww1IXRfTA\",\"title\":\"The complete FUN TO IMAGINE with Richard Feynman\",\"channel\":\"Christopher Sykes\",\"summary\":\"All six episodes of the 1983 BBC series Fun to Imagine, in which Richard Feynman muses on jiggling atoms, fire, rubber bands, magnets, electricity and more. He explains how to think about nature and why imagination is fun.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it for Feynman's intuitive explanations and his joy in understanding how things work.\",\"content_type\":\"interview\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"physics intuition\",\"categories\":[\"Fundamental physics\",\"Scientific method & science communication\",\"Tech pioneers & interviews\"],\"tags\":[\"richard feynman\",\"magnetism\",\"imagination\",\"physics explanations\",\"bbc\"],\"quote\":\"Pondering these questions and learning about them gives joy, excitement, peace of mind and is my great hobby.\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"PGv5BqNL164\",\"title\":\"The KGB, the Computer, and Me.\",\"channel\":\"DragonFartOutLoud\",\"summary\":\"A WGBH/NOVA re-enactment of Clifford Stoll's hunt for KGB-linked hacker Markus Hess, based on his book The Cuckoo's Egg. It starts from a 75-cent accounting discrepancy at Lawrence Berkeley Laboratory.\",\"why_hn\":\"Mentioned alongside The Cuckoo's Egg as the screen version of an early computer-security detective story.\",\"content_type\":\"documentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"early computer espionage\",\"categories\":[\"Security & hacking\",\"History of computing\",\"Documentaries & storytelling\"],\"tags\":[\"cuckoo's egg\",\"clifford stoll\",\"kgb\",\"hacking\",\"nova\"],\"quote\":\"Then check out the old Nova documentary about the story\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"PaCmpygFfXo\",\"title\":\"The spelled-out intro to language modeling: building makemore\",\"channel\":\"Andrej Karpathy\",\"summary\":\"Andrej Karpathy builds a bigram character-level language model from scratch, introducing torch.Tensor and the framework of training, sampling and loss evaluation. It is the first step toward a GPT-style Transformer in his makemore series.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as a long but worthwhile way to demystify how language models work technically.\",\"content_type\":\"tutorial\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"language modeling\",\"categories\":[\"Machine learning & deep learning\",\"LLMs & generative AI\"],\"tags\":[\"makemore\",\"bigram\",\"pytorch\",\"neural networks\",\"karpathy\",\"next-token prediction\"],\"quote\":\"It's long (2h), but well worth the time if anyone is curious about the technical workings of LLMs.\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"PbC4gqZGPSY\",\"title\":\"Ira Glass on the Creative Process (www.getoutthebox.org)\",\"channel\":\"Getoutthebox1\",\"summary\":\"A two-minute video of Ira Glass on the creative process, describing the gap between taste and skill when starting out and urging you to keep producing until your work matches your taste.\",\"why_hn\":\"Shared as encouragement for beginners learning design or craft, on persisting through the taste-skill gap.\",\"content_type\":\"interview\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"creative process and taste gap\",\"categories\":[\"Productivity & self-improvement\",\"Literature & writing craft\"],\"tags\":[\"ira glass\",\"creativity\",\"taste\",\"practice\",\"motivation\"],\"quote\":\"You learn how to differentiate between good and bad much faster than you learn how to create good.\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"PezlFNTGWv4\",\"title\":\"Charlie Brooker's Newswipe 25/03/09\",\"channel\":\"No Unicorns\",\"summary\":\"A clip from Charlie Brooker's Newswipe in which a forensic psychiatrist argues that intense news coverage of school shooters encourages copycat attacks, alongside Brooker's satire of formulaic news reporting.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN cites it in discussions about how media should report mass shootings, as an expert-backed argument for not glorifying perpetrators.\",\"content_type\":\"commentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"media coverage of mass shootings\",\"categories\":[\"Satire & parody\",\"Media, journalism & propaganda\"],\"tags\":[\"charlie brooker\",\"newswipe\",\"copycat effect\",\"news media\",\"mass shootings\"],\"quote\":\"Basically at the end the forensic psychiatrist says that giving this intense superficial coverage about the gunman only encourages more copycat killers.\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"Phl82D57P58\",\"title\":\"El Sendero Luminoso ft. Alex Honnold | The North Face\",\"channel\":\"The North Face\",\"summary\":\"A short film of Alex Honnold free soloing El Sendero Luminoso, a 2,500-foot route in El Potrero Chico, Mexico, in a little over three hours with no rope. It was made by The North Face with climbing footage and music.\",\"why_hn\":\"It is linked whenever Honnold or free soloing comes up, as a gripping and frightening example of extreme climbing.\",\"content_type\":\"documentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"free solo climbing\",\"categories\":[\"Sports & athletics\"],\"tags\":[\"alex honnold\",\"free soloing\",\"rock climbing\",\"el potrero chico\",\"extreme sports\"],\"quote\":\"one of the most frightening videos I have ever seen\",\"recommenders\":1,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"PnbOWi6f_IM\",\"title\":\"How Music Works 1 - Melody - Part 1\",\"channel\":\"timegrinder\",\"summary\":\"The first part of a four-part Channel 4 (UK) documentary series presented by Howard Goodall, this episode covers melody and why tunes affect us so deeply. It explains how melody works in Western music with historical background and music theory.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN people recommend the series as a good, approachable introduction to music theory with historical context.\",\"content_type\":\"documentary\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"music theory\",\"categories\":[\"Music theory, synthesis & production\",\"Documentaries & storytelling\"],\"tags\":[\"melody\",\"howard goodall\",\"music theory\",\"channel 4\",\"western music\"],\"quote\":\"There's also a great TV series from England called 'How Music Works' which gives more of a historical background.\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"PriwCi6SzLo\",\"title\":\"Should Knowledge Be Free?\",\"channel\":\"Medlife Crisis\",\"summary\":\"Medlife Crisis asks whether academic research should sit behind paywalls, covering publisher profit margins, unpaid reviewers, Sci-Hub and Aaron Swartz. It includes an interview with Sci-Hub founder Alexandra Elbakyan.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as an informative review of the academic publishing business and its monopoly-like structure.\",\"content_type\":\"commentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"academic publishing\",\"categories\":[\"Hacker culture & free software\",\"Free speech, censorship & civil liberties\",\"Scientific method & science communication\"],\"tags\":[\"sci-hub\",\"paywalls\",\"open access\",\"aaron swartz\",\"journals\"],\"quote\":\"I found this discussion really informative\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"PsPX0nElJ0k\",\"title\":\"Code Bubbles: Rethinking the User Interface Paradigm of Integrated Development Environments\",\"channel\":\"AndrewBragdon\",\"summary\":\"A short demo of Code Bubbles, an IDE interface built around editable code fragments in bubbles instead of files. It was a research prototype for Java development.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN keeps returning to it as the most convincing example of moving past file-based code editing, even though it never left prototype stage.\",\"content_type\":\"demo\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"ide user interfaces\",\"categories\":[\"Testing, debugging & developer tools\",\"GUI, HCI & interface history\"],\"tags\":[\"code bubbles\",\"ide\",\"code visualization\",\"user interface\",\"programming tools\"],\"quote\":\"Code Bubbles (from 2010) is the most convincing code visualization tool that I can recall\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"PtKhbbcc1Rc\",\"title\":\"Does \\\"land area\\\" assume a country is perfectly flat?\",\"channel\":\"Stand-up Maths\",\"summary\":\"Matt Parker of Stand-up Maths asks whether a country's reported land area accounts for hills and mountains, working through how surface area depends on the resolution of elevation data. It follows up on a question from a viewer and work by researchers who measured it.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it in map projection and coastline threads as a fun look at how measurement choices change area figures.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"measuring land area\",\"categories\":[\"Recreational & visual math\",\"Geology & earth science\"],\"tags\":[\"surface area\",\"topography\",\"maps\",\"measurement\",\"mathematics\"],\"quote\":\"Matt Parker from Standup Maths made a great video on exactly this subject\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"Q1ZeXnmDZMQ\",\"title\":\"James Kunstler: How bad architecture wrecked cities\",\"channel\":\"TED\",\"summary\":\"James Howard Kunstler's TED talk argues that American public spaces and architecture have become unworthy of care, damaging civic life. He presents suburban sprawl and car-first design as failures of the common good.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as an engaging introduction to the critique of car-centric design and bad architecture.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"urban design and architecture\",\"categories\":[\"Transit, cities & urban planning\",\"Visual arts, photography & architecture\"],\"tags\":[\"suburbia\",\"architecture\",\"car-centric design\",\"civic space\",\"ted\"],\"quote\":\"Here's a far more engaging critique of car-first design by James Kunstler\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"QAHi3lX3oGM\",\"title\":\"Peter McCullough, MD testifies to Texas Senate HHS Committee\",\"channel\":\"AAPS Video\",\"summary\":\"Testimony by Peter McCullough, MD, to the Texas Senate Health and Human Services Committee on March 10, 2021, about COVID-19 outpatient treatment. It was posted by the AAPS.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters cite it in pandemic-era threads about early treatments and ivermectin, though the claims are contested.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"covid-19 early treatment\",\"categories\":[\"Medicine & infectious disease\",\"US politics & elections\"],\"tags\":[\"covid-19\",\"senate testimony\",\"ivermectin\",\"outpatient treatment\",\"mccullough\"],\"quote\":null,\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"QEJ9HrZq7Ro\",\"title\":\"Tom Lehrer - Wernher von Braun\",\"channel\":\"The Tom Lehrer Wisdom Channel\",\"summary\":\"Tom Lehrer's satirical song about Wernher von Braun, the German rocket scientist who went from the Nazi missile program to the US space program. It includes the famous line about not caring where the rockets come down.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN quotes it constantly, mostly as a joke or reference, whenever rocketry, engineers' ethics or skill-versus-morality comes up.\",\"content_type\":\"music\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"satirical song\",\"categories\":[\"Music performance & songs\",\"Satire & parody\"],\"tags\":[\"tom lehrer\",\"satire\",\"von braun\",\"rockets\",\"engineering ethics\"],\"quote\":\"Tom Lehrer is indeed a national treasure, and his satire is top shelf.\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"QMiubC6LdTA\",\"title\":\"Console Hacking 2016 (33c3)\",\"channel\":\"media.ccc.de\",\"summary\":\"A 33c3 talk by marcan on reverse engineering the PS4 hardware to run Linux with 3D acceleration. It covers how the console differs from a PC and includes a story about tunneling PCIe over RS-232.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as an approachable, detailed look at console hardware architecture, FreeBSD in the PS4 and console hacking.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"console hacking\",\"categories\":[\"Security & hacking\",\"Speedrunning, glitches & game hacking\",\"Linux & open-source ecosystems\"],\"tags\":[\"ps4\",\"reverse engineering\",\"linux\",\"freebsd\",\"pcie\",\"ccc\"],\"quote\":\"This is a good watch, it goes into detail on the architecture of the PS4\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"QTJRwKOFddc\",\"title\":\"Daniel Henry Holmes Ingalls Jr.: The Live Web. Drag 'n drop in the cloud\",\"channel\":\"JSConf\",\"summary\":\"Smalltalk pioneer Dan Ingalls presents the Lively Kernel at JSConf US 2012, a live, direct-manipulation programming environment running in the browser. He shows drag-and-drop building of live objects on the web.\",\"why_hn\":\"Commenters link it as a demo of what a live, inspectable, Smalltalk-style environment in the browser can be, in threads about Alan Kay, Smalltalk and web limitations.\",\"content_type\":\"demo\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"live programming environments\",\"categories\":[\"GUI, HCI & interface history\",\"Web development\",\"Tech pioneers & interviews\"],\"tags\":[\"dan ingalls\",\"lively kernel\",\"smalltalk\",\"morphic\",\"live programming\",\"browser\"],\"quote\":\"Dan Ingalls himself presenting his continued work in the browser at JSConf US 2012\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"QVBlnCTu9Ms\",\"title\":\"#NoEstimates (Allen Holub)\",\"channel\":\"Allen Holub\",\"summary\":\"Allen Holub's keynote arguing that estimation is harmful, particularly in Agile, and presenting planning approaches based on data and flow instead. It covers the distinction between estimation and data-driven prediction.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended in estimation and sprint debates as a well-reasoned account of where waste is and isn't in agile.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"software estimation\",\"categories\":[\"Software practice, open source & careers\",\"Management, leadership & organizations\"],\"tags\":[\"noestimates\",\"agile\",\"cycle time\",\"planning\",\"cumulative flow\"],\"quote\":\"If you want a well-reasoned discussion of where the waste is and isn't, I highly recommend Allen Holub's talk #NoEstimates\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"QVuU2YCwHjw\",\"title\":\"Ptolemy and Homer (Simpson)\",\"channel\":\"Santiago Ginnobili\",\"summary\":\"A short animation reconstructing a planet's looping orbit using Ptolemy's epicycles and deferents, with the epicycles tracing out the face of Homer Simpson. It shows how enough circles can reproduce nearly any shape.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN uses it as a vivid demonstration that Fourier series and epicycles can fit anything, and as a warning about models with too much wiggle room.\",\"content_type\":\"demo\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"epicycles and fourier series\",\"categories\":[\"Recreational & visual math\",\"Information theory & signal processing\",\"Cosmology & astrophysics\"],\"tags\":[\"epicycles\",\"fourier series\",\"ptolemy\",\"overfitting\",\"visualization\"],\"quote\":\"This is my favourite visualisation of this fact\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"QpAhX-gsHMs\",\"title\":\"Modern C and What We Can Learn From It - Luca Sas [ ACCU 2021 ]\",\"channel\":\"ACCU Conference\",\"summary\":\"Luca Sas's ACCU 2021 talk on Modern C, a style of C and C-like C++ aligned with data-oriented design. It covers centralized memory management, zero-is-initialization, error handling and API design.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as a good overview of modern C patterns, including in threads about learning C.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"modern c programming\",\"categories\":[\"C, C++, Rust, Go & systems languages\",\"Software architecture & design\"],\"tags\":[\"c language\",\"data-oriented design\",\"memory management\",\"api design\",\"error handling\"],\"quote\":\"This video gives a good overview of some modern patterns in C\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"RCCigccBzIU\",\"title\":\"Edsger W. Dijkstra on Dutch TV\",\"channel\":\"Yin Wang\",\"summary\":\"A 2001 VPRO Noorderlicht documentary episode, filmed in Austin in 2000, visiting Edsger Dijkstra and his views on thinking as a discipline. It is in Dutch with English subtitles.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as a short documentary that shows the human side of Dijkstra.\",\"content_type\":\"documentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"edsger dijkstra\",\"categories\":[\"Tech pioneers & interviews\",\"History of computing\"],\"tags\":[\"dijkstra\",\"computer science history\",\"programming elegance\",\"documentary\"],\"quote\":\"For anyone interested in seeing the human side of Dijkstra, I recommend this documentary\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"RhdlBHHimeM\",\"title\":\"Software Art Thou: Glenn Vanderburg - Real Software Engineering\",\"channel\":\"Zendesk\",\"summary\":\"Glenn Vanderburg asks whether software development can be real engineering, surveying other engineering disciplines and decades of programming experience. He envisions what a software engineering worthy of the name might look like.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it in debates about software engineering rigor, agile, and formal methods.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"software engineering as engineering\",\"categories\":[\"Software practice, open source & careers\",\"Software architecture & design\"],\"tags\":[\"software engineering\",\"agile\",\"engineering disciplines\",\"cost of change\",\"glenn vanderburg\"],\"quote\":\"he explores the question of whether \\\"software engineering\\\" is or can really be engineering.\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"RtWbpyjJqrU\",\"title\":\"Slow Motion Flipping Cat Physics | Smarter Every Day 58\",\"channel\":\"SmarterEveryDay\",\"summary\":\"Destin of Smarter Every Day films a cat falling in high-speed slow motion to show how cats right themselves in midair, and introduces the physics behind it.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as a good visual explanation of how cats land on their feet.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"cat righting reflex\",\"categories\":[\"Everyday physics & science demos\",\"Animals & nature\"],\"tags\":[\"high speed video\",\"cats\",\"physics\",\"angular momentum\",\"slow motion\"],\"quote\":\"This video has a good explanation of the process\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0}",
    ",{\"vid\":\"RxabLA7UQ9k\",\"title\":\"Hans Zimmer - Time (Inception)\",\"channel\":\"Crisyta\",\"summary\":\"Hans Zimmer's 'Time', the closing piece from the Inception soundtrack, set to a static image.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as powerful soundtrack music, including for listening while coding.\",\"content_type\":\"music\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"film soundtrack\",\"categories\":[\"Music performance & songs\"],\"tags\":[\"hans zimmer\",\"inception\",\"soundtrack\",\"coding music\",\"orchestral\"],\"quote\":\"Hans Zimmer and James Newton Howard are also great\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"RzWB5jL5RX0\",\"title\":\"Cracking Enigma in 2021 - Computerphile\",\"channel\":\"Computerphile\",\"summary\":\"Dr Mike Pound of Computerphile shows how a modern laptop can crack Enigma, using a ciphertext-only attack based on an older paper. He compares it with the tooling in CrypTool.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as a good starting point on practical Enigma cryptanalysis with modern hardware.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"enigma cryptanalysis\",\"categories\":[\"Cryptography\",\"History of computing\"],\"tags\":[\"enigma\",\"cryptanalysis\",\"computerphile\",\"mike pound\",\"ciphers\"],\"quote\":\"would be a good place to start.\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"S-Xm7s9eGxU\",\"title\":\"Erik Satie - Gymnopédie No.1\",\"channel\":\"DistantMirrors\",\"summary\":\"A recording of Erik Satie's Gymnopedie No. 1, one of the best-known pieces of minimalist piano music by the French composer.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as an accessible entry to Satie and calm ambient classical music, and mentioned as a famous influence on game soundtracks.\",\"content_type\":\"music\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"minimalist classical piano\",\"categories\":[\"Music performance & songs\"],\"tags\":[\"erik satie\",\"gymnopedie\",\"piano\",\"ambient\",\"classical\"],\"quote\":\"perhaps putting a name to it will give you some appreciation for why his work is valued\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"S2Hp_1jqpY8\",\"title\":\"Differential Synchronization\",\"channel\":\"Google TechTalks\",\"summary\":\"Neil Fraser of Google presents Differential Synchronization, the algorithm behind MobWrite, for keeping multiple copies of a document in sync in real time. The talk argues the approach is robust, convergent and efficient.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN points to it as a simple diff-and-patch alternative to operational transforms and CRDTs for collaborative editing.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"real-time collaborative editing\",\"categories\":[\"Distributed systems\",\"Algorithms & data structures\"],\"tags\":[\"differential synchronization\",\"diff patch\",\"mobwrite\",\"operational transform\",\"convergence\"],\"quote\":\"The really nice bits, though, are that it is a simple algorithm\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"SAc0vQCC6UQ\",\"title\":\"Ryan Dahl - History of Node.js\",\"channel\":\"phxtagsoup\",\"summary\":\"Ryan Dahl tells the history of Node.js and why he created it at a Phoenix meetup. He explains the push for non-blocking IO on a clean slate rather than extending an existing ecosystem.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as the creator's own account of why Node was built and how he thinks about humility.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"history of node.js\",\"categories\":[\"Web development\",\"Tech pioneers & interviews\"],\"tags\":[\"node.js\",\"ryan dahl\",\"event loop\",\"non-blocking io\",\"javascript\"],\"quote\":\"He didn't want to use an ecosystem that had existing blocking code.\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"SDtAh9IwG-I\",\"title\":\"How big is a visible photon?\",\"channel\":\"Huygens Optics\",\"summary\":\"Huygens Optics shows through a laser interference experiment that the question of a photon's size is really about coherence length. It argues that photons exist as energy exchanges between radiation and matter, not as little particles in free space.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as an approachable, intuitive explanation of why visible photons can be surprisingly large, from a channel known for practical optics.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"photon size and coherence\",\"categories\":[\"Quantum physics\",\"Everyday physics & science demos\"],\"tags\":[\"photons\",\"coherence length\",\"interference\",\"optics\",\"quantum\"],\"quote\":\"I like this one for the approachable and intuitive explanation\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"SKhcan8pk2w\",\"title\":\"Homochirality: Why Nature Never Makes Mirror Molecules\",\"channel\":\"Steve Mould\",\"summary\":\"Steve Mould explains homochirality: why biological molecules like sugars and amino acids come in only one handedness. He covers ideas about how this asymmetry might have arisen, touching on meteorite samples and RNA.\",\"why_hn\":\"Cited as a clear, accessible explanation of why life uses only one version of chiral molecules.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"molecular chirality\",\"categories\":[\"Chemistry\",\"Biology & biotechnology\"],\"tags\":[\"homochirality\",\"chemistry\",\"origin of life\",\"amino acids\",\"biochemistry\"],\"quote\":\"Here is a video talking about molecular chirality and how nature really only produces one version of molecules\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"SKtQgFBRUvQ\",\"title\":\"RustConf 2016 - A Modern Editor Built in Rust by Raph Levien\",\"channel\":\"Confreaks\",\"summary\":\"Raph Levien presents Xi editor at RustConf 2016, a high-performance text editor built in Rust. The talk goes into technical details, building polished GUI apps, and running a community-focused open source project.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN lists it among good resources on building text editors and as a recommended lunch-and-learn talk.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"advanced\",\"primary_topic\":\"text editor architecture in rust\",\"categories\":[\"C, C++, Rust, Go & systems languages\",\"Testing, debugging & developer tools\",\"Software performance & optimization\"],\"tags\":[\"xi editor\",\"rust\",\"text editors\",\"raph levien\",\"gui\"],\"quote\":\"So far I've found and enjoyed reading/watching the resources below\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"SR1jwwagtaQ\",\"title\":\"The ascent of Alex Honnold\",\"channel\":\"CBS News\",\"summary\":\"A CBS News (60 Minutes) profile of free solo climber Alex Honnold, who scales sheer rock faces without ropes. Lara Logan reports on his climbing and his apparent lack of fear, including his Half Dome ascent.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters point to it as a nerve-wracking look at Honnold's free soloing, often when climbing, risk or van life comes up.\",\"content_type\":\"documentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"free solo climbing\",\"categories\":[\"Sports & athletics\",\"Documentaries & storytelling\"],\"tags\":[\"alex honnold\",\"free solo\",\"rock climbing\",\"risk\",\"60 minutes\"],\"quote\":\"The whole video is pretty interesting, but may make your hands sweat even more.\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"SYeeTvitvFU\",\"title\":\"Why this British crossroads was so dangerous\",\"channel\":\"Tom Scott\",\"summary\":\"Tom Scott explains why Ipley Cross in England's New Forest was one of Britain's most dangerous junctions. The angled crossroads lets a car's A-pillar hide approaching traffic and cyclists because there is no relative motion in the driver's view.\",\"why_hn\":\"Used as the go-to visual demonstration of A-pillar blind spots and badly designed junctions.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"road junction safety\",\"categories\":[\"Transit, cities & urban planning\",\"UX & UI design\"],\"tags\":[\"a-pillar blind spot\",\"road design\",\"cyclist safety\",\"intersections\",\"visibility\"],\"quote\":\"Tom Scott has a good video for anyone looking for a visual of this phenomenon\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"SrKj4hYic5A\",\"title\":\"\\\"Programming Should Eat Itself\\\" by Nada Amin\",\"channel\":\"Strange Loop Conference\",\"summary\":\"Nada Amin's Strange Loop talk on programming languages that build on themselves, centered on towers of interpreters and nestable interpretation in Scheme. She shows how interpreters can be stacked and collapsed.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as a quick, wonderful look at infinite towers of interpreters and what they make possible.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"advanced\",\"primary_topic\":\"towers of interpreters\",\"categories\":[\"Compilers, interpreters & program analysis\",\"Functional programming\",\"Programming language design\"],\"tags\":[\"nada amin\",\"scheme\",\"interpreters\",\"metacircular\",\"programming languages\"],\"quote\":\"Here's a wonderful demonstration of the power of nestability\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"SzA2YODtgK4\",\"title\":\"Getting Started With Org Mode\",\"channel\":\"thoughtbot\",\"summary\":\"Harry Schwartz gives a roughly hour-long tour of Emacs Org mode as an outliner, task manager, markup language and agenda tool. He demonstrates how he uses it for documents, presentations and todo lists.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as a thorough introduction showing what Org mode can do for personal organization.\",\"content_type\":\"tutorial\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"emacs org mode\",\"categories\":[\"Testing, debugging & developer tools\",\"Productivity & self-improvement\"],\"tags\":[\"org mode\",\"emacs\",\"task management\",\"plain text\",\"literate programming\"],\"quote\":\"which is a great demonstration on Emacs and Org Mode's potential as an organizational tool.\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"TPg4Vcr56F0\",\"title\":\"Dan Deacon - Bromst - In The Studio\",\"channel\":\"Pitchfork\",\"summary\":\"A Pitchfork TV documentary following Dan Deacon in Montana as he finishes his album Bromst. It shows the experimentation behind it, including pieces written for MIDI-driven player pianos.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN points to it in threads on black MIDI and player pianos as a look at an artist writing tuneful music beyond human playability.\",\"content_type\":\"documentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"experimental music production\",\"categories\":[\"Music theory, synthesis & production\",\"Music performance & songs\",\"Documentaries & storytelling\"],\"tags\":[\"dan deacon\",\"player piano\",\"midi\",\"bromst\",\"studio documentary\"],\"quote\":\"He talks about it quite in a bit in a Pitchfork TV video that documented him working on one of his albums, Bromst\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"TQB0KK2rxcw\",\"title\":\"GSMT - Dan Holohan, Author: \\\"The Lost Art of Steam Heating\\\"\",\"channel\":\"The General Society of Mechanics and Tradesmen\",\"summary\":\"Dan Holohan, author of The Lost Art of Steam Heating, talks about the old steam heating systems he learned about from veteran tradesmen. He shares stories and practical lessons on how these systems work, including running steam at low temperatures.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as an informative talk on steam heating whose advice to respect old working systems carries over to software.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"steam heating\",\"categories\":[\"Power grid, heating & buildings\",\"Home, DIY & practical skills\"],\"tags\":[\"steam heat\",\"dan holohan\",\"hvac\",\"old systems\",\"trades\"],\"quote\":\"When you see something that's old and it's been there for a long time and it's working, don't laugh at it.\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"TQMbvJNRpLE\",\"title\":\"How to Achieve Your Most Ambitious Goals | Stephen Duneier | TEDxTucson\",\"channel\":\"TEDx Talks\",\"summary\":\"Stephen Duneier describes a 