y\":\"The Thought Emporium builds a homemade multi-electrode array, grows human neurons on it and attempts to take readings. It is the first part of a series aimed at connecting living neurons to a computer.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN cites it as an impressive garage-science example of neuron-computer interfaces and hard-science DIY biology.\",\"content_type\":\"demo\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"neurons on electrode arrays\",\"categories\":[\"Neuroscience & the brain\",\"Biology & biotechnology\",\"Embedded systems & DIY electronics\"],\"tags\":[\"human neurons\",\"cell culture\",\"biocomputing\",\"diy biology\",\"electrode array\"],\"quote\":\"Seeing a dude grow human neurons directly connected to a computer chip to make a \\\"human AI\\\", all from his garage\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"VLxTHYGLKY0\",\"title\":\"The rigs we've created for chipsynth MD's emulation.\",\"channel\":\"plgDavid\",\"summary\":\"A Plogue developer shows the custom electronic test rigs built to measure the Sega Mega Drive/Genesis sound chip (YM2612) for the chipsynth MD emulation. It covers the years of testing and reverse engineering needed to reproduce the chip's quirks accurately.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended in threads on chip emulation as a look at how hard it is to emulate vintage sound hardware accurately.\",\"content_type\":\"demo\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"sound chip emulation\",\"categories\":[\"Demoscene & retrocomputing\",\"Electronics & circuit design\",\"Music theory, synthesis & production\"],\"tags\":[\"ym2612\",\"mega drive\",\"reverse engineering\",\"test rigs\",\"chipsynth\"],\"quote\":\"This video shows some insight into the development process\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"VVp8UGjECt4\",\"title\":\"Principles of economics, translated\",\"channel\":\"Yoram Bauman\",\"summary\":\"Economist-comedian Yoram Bauman delivers a humorous translation of Mankiw's ten principles of economics for the uninitiated. It