ebird\",\"david attenborough\",\"bird sounds\",\"mimicry\",\"bbc\"],\"quote\":\"The end of this BBC clip blew my mind the first time I saw it.\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"VkIJbpdTujE\",\"title\":\"3D Gaussian Splatting! - Computerphile\",\"channel\":\"Computerphile\",\"summary\":\"A Computerphile video in which Dr Mike Pound and PhD student Lewis Stuart explain 3D Gaussian splatting, a quick technique that turns photos of a scene into a 3D model without much compute. It includes a demo and compares the approach to NeRFs.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as an accessible introduction to Gaussian splats, made by people who understand the subject.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"gaussian splatting\",\"categories\":[\"Computer graphics & rendering\",\"Computer vision, speech & NLP\"],\"tags\":[\"3d gaussian splatting\",\"nerf\",\"3d reconstruction\",\"computer graphics\",\"computerphile\"],\"quote\":\"Here's a much better explainer from some people who seem to actually understand the subject matter and not just the buzzwords\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"W71BTkUbdqE\",\"title\":\"Why Google Stores Billions of Lines of Code in a Single Repository\",\"channel\":\"@Scale\",\"summary\":\"Rachel Potvin describes the scale of Google's codebase and its custom monolithic source repository. She explains the reasons for the monorepo model, the tooling that makes it workable, and its tradeoffs.\",\"why_hn\":\"The go-to reference in monorepo and trunk-based development discussions, recommended as a firsthand account of how Google manages source control.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"monorepos\",\"categories\":[\"Testing, debugging & developer tools\",\"Software architecture & design\"],\"tags\":[\"monorepo\",\"google\",\"source control\",\"trunk-based development\",\"dev tools\"],\"quote\":\"I'd recommend this video that describes it.\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"X9RYuvPCQUA\",\"title\":\"Everything is a Remix (2023 Edition)\",\"channel\":\"Kirby Ferguson\",\"summary\":\"Kirby Ferguson's updated four-part video essay arguing that creativity is built on copying, transforming and combining existing work. It covers examples from film, music and technology and touches on generative AI.\",\"why_hn\":\"Pointed to in copyright and AI debates as a compelling case that all culture is remix.\",\"content_type\":\"documentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"remix and creativity\",\"categories\":[\"Film analysis & criticism\",\"Law, policing & justice\",\"Futurism & technology and society\"],\"tags\":[\"copyright\",\"video essay\",\"creativity\",\"generative ai\",\"derivative work\"],\"quote\":\"It highlights how essentially everything in culture is a remix of some previous version of the culture\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"XH0F9r0siTI\",\"title\":\"DEF CON 26  - Christopher Domas - The Ring 0 Facade Awakening the Processors Inner Demons\",\"channel\":\"DEFCONConference\",\"summary\":\"Christopher Domas's DEF CON 26 talk on secret processor registers that vendors reserve for third parties holding trusted keys. He examines how users are locked out of parts of their own CPUs.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters call his x86 internals talks spectacular and mention them whenever low-level processor surprises come up.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"advanced\",\"primary_topic\":\"x86 processor internals\",\"categories\":[\"Security & hacking\",\"Computer architecture & CPUs\"],\"tags\":[\"x86\",\"hardware security\",\"cpu registers\",\"reverse engineering\",\"def con\"],\"quote\":\"I remember watching his Defcon talks on x86 shenanigans[^1][^2] and being amazed at what he's been able to discover\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"XJ97iXQrqzw\",\"title\":\"Emulating the DX7 the HARD way\",\"channel\":\"plgDavid\",\"summary\":\"An episode of Plogue's chipresearch series on emulating the Yamaha DX7 from the hardware up. It covers the sub CPU and main CPU, address map investigation, cartridges, firmware analysis and a skeleton MAME driver.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended in DX7 reverse-engineering threads as a look at the effort behind bit-accurate emulation.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"advanced\",\"primary_topic\":\"dx7 emulation\",\"categories\":[\"Music theory, synthesis & production\",\"Demoscene & retrocomputing\",\"Computer architecture & CPUs\"],\"tags\":[\"yamaha dx7\",\"reverse engineering\",\"mame\",\"firmware analysis\",\"chipsynth\"],\"quote\":\"a YouTube video about the reverse engineering process from David at Plogue\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"XJ9ckqCMiKk\",\"title\":\"Erlang Factory SF 2016 Keynote  Phoenix and Elm – Making the Web Functional\",\"channel\":\"Erlang Solutions\",\"summary\":\"A joint Erlang Factory 2016 keynote by Phoenix creator Chris McCord and Elm creator Evan Czaplicki on using the two together, including Phoenix.Presence design and PubSub optimizations.\",\"why_hn\":\"Pointed to as the best source on Phoenix Presence internals and for combining Phoenix with Elm.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"phoenix and elm\",\"categories\":[\"Functional programming\",\"Web development\",\"Distributed systems\"],\"tags\":[\"phoenix\",\"elm\",\"elixir\",\"crdt\",\"presence\",\"functional web\"],\"quote\":\"My Erlang Factory keynote also goes into the design considerations and PubSub layer optimizations that sits underneath Phoenix.Presence\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"XVmsfL5LG90\",\"title\":\"How to write cuneiform with Irving Finkel and @preservethings I Curator's Corner S4 Ep9\",\"channel\":\"The British Museum\",\"summary\":\"British Museum curator Irving Finkel teaches how to write cuneiform, with a colleague following along. It is a short, lively introduction to the script.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as the best short, funny and 