"why_hn\":\"Pointed to as the origin story of Hack and a real-world example of OCaml in production.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"advanced\",\"primary_topic\":\"static analysis of php\",\"categories\":[\"Compilers, interpreters & program analysis\",\"Functional programming\",\"Python, Ruby & scripting languages\"],\"tags\":[\"ocaml\",\"hack\",\"php\",\"type inference\",\"facebook\"],\"quote\":\"Another cool one from CUFP this year is how Facebook is using OCaml to add incremental type inference to their vast PHP code base.\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"gPlpphT7n9s\",\"title\":\"Shakespeare: Original pronunciation (The Open University)\",\"channel\":\"OpenLearn from The Open University\",\"summary\":\"David and Ben Crystal introduce the Original Pronunciation production of Shakespeare and show what it reveals about the history of English, including puns and rhymes lost in modern speech.\",\"why_hn\":\"Frequently linked as a short, accessible demonstration of how Shakespeare actually sounded.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"original pronunciation of shakespeare\",\"categories\":[\"Language & linguistics\",\"Literature & writing craft\"],\"tags\":[\"shakespeare\",\"linguistics\",\"history of english\",\"pronunciation\",\"ben crystal\"],\"quote\":\"I very much enjoyed this quick introduction to Shakespeare in Original Pronunciation\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"gQnmP7UD_zk\",\"title\":\"Etching small patterns in Glass\",\"channel\":\"Huygens Optics\",\"summary\":\"Huygens Optics experiments with etching tiny patterns in glass, covering hydrofluoric acid risks, buffered oxide etch, photoresist and chromium masks, and laser-induced etching. It ends with attempts at microscopic Tesla valves.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as a highly informative look at micro-etching glass and the smallest Tesla valves.\",\"content_type\":\"demo\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"glass micro-etching\",\"categories\":[\"Semiconductors & chip manufacturing\",\"Chemistry\",\"Materials science\"],\"tags\":[\"hydrofluoric acid\",\"tesla valve\",\"microfabrication\",\"optics\",\"laser etching\"],\"quote\":\"The video on micro etching glass with HF is highly informative on this post...\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"gSvUqhZcbVg\",\"title\":\"Hauntology, Lost Futures and 80s Nostalgia\",\"channel\":\"Jonas Čeika - CCK Philosophy\",\"summary\":\"A video essay explaining hauntology, Mark Fisher's idea of lost futures, and 80s nostalgia. It uses the Fallout games and other media as examples of a retro-futurism that mourns futures that never arrived.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as a clear introduction to hauntology and lost futures in discussions of Mark Fisher and cyberpunk.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"hauntology\",\"categories\":[\"Philosophy & ethics\",\"Gaming culture & analysis\",\"Futurism & technology and society\"],\"tags\":[\"hauntology\",\"mark fisher\",\"retro-futurism\",\"nostalgia\",\"philosophy\"],\"quote\":\"I think this guy does a great job of explaining it through the analogy of the Fallout videogame series\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0}",
    ",{\"vid\":\"gdK4y6O-llE\",\"title\":\"OODA Loop & Evolutionary Epistemology of John Boyd by Chuck Spinney\",\"channel\":\"52 Living Ideas\",\"summary\":\"Chuck Spinney, a long-time associate of John Boyd, presents Boyd's thinking on the OODA loop and evolutionary epistemology. The talk runs about two and a half hours and includes Q&A.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN readers recommend it as the way to learn what Boyd actually meant by OODA, straight from a collaborator, rather than from the popularized loop diagram.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"OODA loop and John Boyd\",\"categories\":[\"Military history & warfare\",\"Philosophy & ethics\",\"Management, leadership & organizations\"],\"tags\":[\"ooda loop\",\"john boyd\",\"military strategy\",\"decision making\",\"epistemology\"],\"quote\":\"It shows the thinking method behind the OODA Loop--Evolutionary Epistemology\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"ghtpJnrdgbo\",\"title\":\"Richard Hipp - Git: Just Say No\",\"channel\":\"southeastlinuxfest\",\"summary\":\"SQLite author Richard Hipp's talk at SouthEast LinuxFest criticizing Git's workflow and introducing Fossil as an alternative. He lists Git enhancements and argues for keeping history intact.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended for the SQLite author's reasoned case for a different approach to version control, and as an introduction to Fossil.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"version control and fossil\",\"categories\":[\"Testing, debugging & developer tools\",\"Databases & storage\",\"Linux & open-source ecosystems\"],\"tags\":[\"git\",\"fossil\",\"sqlite\",\"richard hipp\",\"version control\"],\"quote\":\"If the author of SQLite advances some arguments around the proper way of doing version management, I would advise to error on the side of caution and listen at least a second time.\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"gisdyTBMNyQ\",\"title\":\"I thought this rotating house was impossible.\",\"channel\":\"Tom Scott\",\"summary\":\"Tom Scott visits a rotating house near San Diego and explains how electricity, gas and water keep working on the rotating part. It includes a 3D render of the utility connection system.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN likes the clear animation of how slip rings and piping let a house rotate.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"rotating house engineering\",\"categories\":[\"Civil engineering & construction\",\"Mechanical & precision engineering\"],\"tags\":[\"rotating house\",\"slip ring\",\"utilities\",\"tom scott\",\"engineering\"],\"quote\":\"Tom Scott did a video last year that has a great 3D render of the system\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"goh2x_G0ct4\",\"title\":\"Gil Scott-Heron - Whitey On the Moon (Official Audio)\",\"channel\":\"Ace Records\",\"summary\":\"Gil Scott-Heron's 1970 spoken-word piece, in an official audio upload. It contrasts the Apollo moon program with poverty and neglect at home.\",\"why_hn\":\"Posted in space-spending threads as a classic critique of priorities, usually with a quip.\",\"content_type\":\"music\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"space spending critique\",\"categories\":[\"Music performance & songs\",\"Space exploration & spaceflight\",\"Social issues & culture\"],\"tags\":[\"gil scott-heron\",\"apollo\",\"spoken word\",\"inequality\",\"protest music\"],\"quote\":\"Only two minutes, worth a listen\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"gvZSpET11ZY\",\"title\":\"Retirement Plans: Last Week Tonight with John Oliver (HBO)\",\"channel\":\"LastWeekTonight\",\"summary\":\"A Last Week Tonight segment on retirement plans, high fees and bad financial advice, with comedic sketches. It explains how fees eat into retirement savings and favors low-cost passive investing.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN links it in personal finance threads as an entertaining explanation of how fees erode retirement savings.\",\"content_type\":\"commentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"retirement plan fees\",\"categories\":[\"Investing & personal finance\",\"Media, journalism & propaganda\"],\"tags\":[\"john oliver\",\"401k\",\"investment fees\",\"passive investing\",\"personal finance\"],\"quote\":\"John Oliver had a great bit on retirement plans [1] if you're interested in passive investing.\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"h3gwyHNo7MI\",\"title\":\"Pandora's Box, E1: The Engineers' Plot\",\"channel\":\"nagualdesign\",\"summary\":\"The first episode of Adam Curtis's 1992 BBC series Pandora's Box, on the engineers' plot, examining communism in the Soviet Union and the rise and limits of technocratic central planning.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as a fun, well-made documentary on Soviet planning, Gosplan, and why central control failed.\",\"content_type\":\"documentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"soviet central planning\",\"categories\":[\"World history\",\"Documentaries & storytelling\",\"Geopolitics & foreign policy\"],\"tags\":[\"adam curtis\",\"soviet union\",\"technocracy\",\"gosplan\",\"bbc documentary\"],\"quote\":\"I recommend watching Pandora's Box documentary. It is fun and pretty well made\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"h4Uu5eyN6VU\",\"title\":\"Is the EU Democratic? Does Your Vote Matter?\",\"channel\":\"Kurzgesagt – In a Nutshell\",\"summary\":\"Kurzgesagt explains how the European Union is governed, how its officials are elected or appointed, and whether a citizen's vote actually matters.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters point to it as a quick factual primer on EU institutions when threads get into EU politics and policy.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"european union governance\",\"categories\":[\"Geopolitics & foreign policy\",\"Politics & history (other)\"],\"tags\":[\"eu\",\"elections\",\"european parliament\",\"democracy\",\"institutions\"],\"quote\":\"Here is a quick video explaining how it works\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"hFL6qRIJZ_Y\",\"title\":\"ADHD & How Anyone Can Improve Their Focus\",\"channel\":\"Andrew Huberman\",\"summary\":\"Andrew Huberman discusses ADHD, its biology and myths, behavioral and pharmacologic treatments, and training protocols to improve focus for people with or without ADHD. It covers dopamine and the coordination of brain networks.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN people offer it as practical focus and ADHD advice from a credentialed neuroscientist, though some treat it as only a reference.\",\"content_type\":\"podcast\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"ADHD and focus\",\"categories\":[\"Mental health & neurodiversity\",\"Neuroscience & the brain\"],\"tags\":[\"adhd\",\"focus\",\"dopamine\",\"neuroscience\",\"huberman\"],\"quote\":\"He's pretty well credentialed though, so I trust on average the advice is good\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"hXgqik6HXc0\",\"title\":\"Consciousness is Not a Computation (Roger Penrose) | AI Podcast Clips\",\"channel\":\"Lex Fridman\",\"summary\":\"Clip from Lex Fridman's podcast with Roger Penrose in which Penrose argues that consciousness is not a computation. He discusses Godel-style arguments and the possible role of quantum effects in microtubules.\",\"why_hn\":\"Pointed to in consciousness and AI threads as the best-known statement of Penrose's view, which commenters find interesting even when they disagree.\",\"content_type\":\"podcast\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"consciousness and computation\",\"categories\":[\"Consciousness & cognition\",\"Quantum physics\",\"Tech pioneers & interviews\"],\"tags\":[\"penrose\",\"consciousness\",\"quantum mind\",\"microtubules\",\"ai\"],\"quote\":\"I don't take Roger's view about consciousness for granted but it is very interesting nevertheless.\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"ho7oagHeqNc\",\"title\":\"Category Theory in Life - Eugenia Cheng\",\"channel\":\"Lambda World\",\"summary\":\"Eugenia Cheng gives the opening keynote at Lambda World 2017, presenting category theory as very abstract algebra whose abstraction gives it broad applicability. She applies it to everyday questions including privilege and prejudice.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as an accessible, gentle introduction that makes category theory feel approachable and shows its analogy-making power.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"category theory\",\"categories\":[\"Pure mathematics\",\"Functional programming\",\"Recreational & visual math\"],\"tags\":[\"category theory\",\"eugenia cheng\",\"abstraction\",\"mathematics\",\"functional programming\"],\"quote\":\"To get a flavor of and gentle introduction to the analogy-making power of category theory, I recommend this talk by Eugenia Cheng\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"hzPd3umu78g\",\"title\":\"High-performance Tree Wrangling, the APL Way // Aaron Hsu // Dyalog '18\",\"channel\":\"Dyalog User Meetings\",\"summary\":\"Aaron Hsu shows how to manipulate and transform tree data in APL using arrays and inverted tables instead of pointers. He claims much smaller code, better performance and easier parallelisation.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as a striking example of high-performance tree handling with array programming.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"advanced\",\"primary_topic\":\"array programming trees\",\"categories\":[\"Programming language design\",\"Algorithms & data structures\",\"Software performance & optimization\"],\"tags\":[\"apl\",\"trees\",\"inverted tables\",\"array languages\",\"dyalog\"],\"quote\":\"He has one talk (available on YouTube) about his approach to using arrays to working with tree structures in a high-performance way in APL\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"i1vbvikDiI8\",\"title\":\"The Joy of Programming in Jai - Part 1: Hello World\",\"channel\":\"Nuno Afonso\",\"summary\":\"The first part of a series on programming in Jai, Jonathan Blow's in-development language. It covers compiling, writing hello world and using code from other files.\",\"why_hn\":\"Jai had no public release, so commenters point to this beta user's videos as a way to see what the language is actually like.\",\"content_type\":\"tutorial\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"jai programming language\",\"categories\":[\"Programming language design\",\"Game programming & engines\"],\"tags\":[\"jai\",\"jonathan blow\",\"game programming\",\"hello world\",\"beta\"],\"quote\":\"One of the beta testers has published some more up to date videos with his language introduction and initial experience\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"iRleB034EC8\",\"title\":\"Peter Thiel on “The Straussian Moment”\",\"channel\":\"Hoover Institution\",\"summary\":\"Peter Robinson interviews Peter Thiel at the Hoover Institution about Thiel's essay The Straussian Moment. Thiel discusses how people trust the will more than the intellect and reason, and how the question of human nature has been abandoned.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN points to it as a primer on Thiel's thinking and as an example of Peter Robinson's skill as an interviewer.\",\"content_type\":\"interview\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"peter thiel political philosophy\",\"categories\":[\"Philosophy & ethics\",\"Tech pioneers & interviews\",\"US politics & elections\"],\"tags\":[\"peter thiel\",\"straussian moment\",\"political philosophy\",\"hoover institution\",\"peter robinson\"],\"quote\":\"There is a Peter Robinson interview about the essay that can serve as a good primer[1].\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"iRsSjh5TTqI\",\"title\":\"Microtonal Guitar (Fixed Fret) - Tolgahan Çoğulu\",\"channel\":\"Microtonal Guitar - Tolgahan Çoğulu\",\"summary\":\"Tolgahan Cogulu demonstrates a fixed-fret microtonal guitar, playing music that uses notes between the usual twelve semitones. The channel is dedicated to his microtonal guitar designs.\",\"why_hn\":\"Linked in music theory and tuning threads as a listenable example of microtonal guitar that sounds good.\",\"content_type\":\"demo\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"microtonal guitar\",\"categories\":[\"Music theory, synthesis & production\",\"Music performance & songs\"],\"tags\":[\"microtonal\",\"guitar\",\"tuning\",\"turkish music\",\"frets\"],\"quote\":\"Tolgahan Çoğulu and his awesome microtonal guitar creations\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"ihkRwArnc1k\",\"title\":\"Birth of The Transistor: A video history of Japan's electronic industry. (Part 1)\",\"channel\":\"RC286\",\"summary\":\"First part of a four-part video history of Japan's electronics industry, covering the birth of the transistor and the rise of Japanese semiconductors. It appears to draw on NHK documentary material.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended in documentary and semiconductor-history threads as a detailed look at Japan's chip industry.\",\"content_type\":\"documentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"history of japanese electronics\",\"categories\":[\"History of computing\",\"Semiconductors & chip manufacturing\"],\"tags\":[\"transistor\",\"semiconductors\",\"japan\",\"nhk\",\"history\"],\"quote\":\"There is a four-part documentary I saw on YouTube that went into much more (fascinating) detail.\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"imhUmLtlZpw\",\"title\":\"Understanding the rise of China | Martin Jacques\",\"channel\":\"TED\",\"summary\":\"Economist Martin Jacques offers three building blocks for understanding China's rise, including seeing China as a civilization-state rather than a nation-state. He also covers how Chinese people view the state.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it in discussions of China's surveillance and social-credit systems for explaining Chinese attitudes toward the state.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"understanding China\",\"categories\":[\"Geopolitics & foreign policy\",\"Macroeconomics & financial crises\"],\"tags\":[\"china\",\"civilization state\",\"ted talk\",\"geopolitics\",\"martin jacques\"],\"quote\":\"I highly recommend Martin Jacques' Ted talk [0] on this very subject from over 7 years ago.\",\"recommenders\":1,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"ipzR9bhei_o\",\"title\":\"Bach, Toccata and Fugue in D minor, organ\",\"channel\":\"smalin\",\"summary\":\"Bach's Toccata and Fugue in D minor on organ, shown with a Music Animation Machine bar-graph score instead of traditional notation.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as a music visualization that makes the structure easier to follow than sheet music.\",\"content_type\":\"music\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"music visualization\",\"categories\":[\"Music performance & songs\",\"Music theory, synthesis & production\"],\"tags\":[\"bach\",\"organ\",\"toccata and fugue\",\"animated score\",\"classical\"],\"quote\":\"videos are eye-opening\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"j02b8Fuz73A\",\"title\":\"NeXTSTEP Release 3 Demo\",\"channel\":\"blatherskyte\",\"summary\":\"Steve Jobs demos NeXTSTEP Release 3, showing the object-oriented development environment and UI that later became the basis of Mac OS X. Midway through he builds a database-backed GUI app with Interface Builder and Enterprise Objects Framework with almost no code.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN uses it as the classic example of rapid visual app building and of a polished early Jobs presentation, often with timestamps pointing to the database demo.\",\"content_type\":\"demo\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"nextstep operating system demo\",\"categories\":[\"GUI, HCI & interface history\",\"Tech pioneers & interviews\",\"Operating systems & kernels\"],\"tags\":[\"nextstep\",\"steve jobs\",\"interface builder\",\"enterprise objects framework\",\"rapid app development\",\"os x history\"],\"quote\":\"There's a video of steve jobs building a fairly complex DB app without a single line of code, including joins.\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"j2dHFC31VtQ\",\"title\":\"How American Fire Departments are Getting People Killed\",\"channel\":\"Not Just Bikes\",\"summary\":\"Not Just Bikes argues that oversized American fire trucks and fire department requirements push cities toward wide roads and dangerous street design. He compares this with the Dutch approach to emergency response.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN people link it in threads on big vehicles and road safety as a comparison of US and Dutch fire department approaches.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"urban planning and road safety\",\"categories\":[\"Transit, cities & urban planning\"],\"tags\":[\"fire trucks\",\"street design\",\"road safety\",\"netherlands\",\"urbanism\"],\"quote\":\"shows quite well the major difference in approach between fire departments in the USA vs the Netherlands\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"j4H9LL7A-nQ\",\"title\":\"Renting vs. Buying a Home: What People Get Wrong\",\"channel\":\"Ben Felix\",\"summary\":\"Ben Felix argues that common beliefs about renting versus buying are wrong: homes are not great investments, mortgages are not a wealth hack, and ownership does not reliably bring happiness.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as detailed math on rent versus buy, showing the choice is often roughly a wash.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"rent versus buy\",\"categories\":[\"Investing & personal finance\"],\"tags\":[\"personal finance\",\"housing\",\"mortgages\",\"investing\",\"ben felix\"],\"quote\":null,\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"jGWFg7EDnyY\",\"title\":\"Pulling Clear Images Directly Off Satellites | GOES-15,16,17 and Himawari 8 HRIT\",\"channel\":\"The Thought Emporium\",\"summary\":\"The Thought Emporium shows how to receive clear images directly from GOES-15, 16 and 17 and Himawari 8 geostationary weather satellites with software-defined radio. It covers the extra effort needed to receive GOES data.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters credit it for inspiring their own satellite-reception hobby and praise the channel's high-quality content.\",\"content_type\":\"tutorial\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"satellite image reception\",\"categories\":[\"Space exploration & spaceflight\",\"Electronics & circuit design\",\"Embedded systems & DIY electronics\"],\"tags\":[\"goes-16\",\"sdr\",\"weather satellites\",\"hrit\",\"radio\"],\"quote\":\"The Thought Emporium makes really high quality content.\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"jPhJbKBuNnA\",\"title\":\"I tried using AI. It scared me.\",\"channel\":\"Tom Scott\",\"summary\":\"Tom Scott describes trying ChatGPT to fix his Gmail labels and being unsettled by how capable it was. He argues that we cannot know where we are on the technology curve, comparing it to Napster.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN cites it for the argument that we cannot tell whether AI is early or late on its sigmoid curve until hindsight.\",\"content_type\":\"commentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"ai impact\",\"categories\":[\"LLMs & generative AI\",\"AI safety, AGI & impact\"],\"tags\":[\"chatgpt\",\"tom scott\",\"technology curve\",\"ai progress\",\"napster\"],\"quote\":\"Tom Scott's video[0] makes a very good argument: we don't know where we are on the technology curve.\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"jSFsrmLhC00\",\"title\":\"Transport Tycoon 2014 Soundtrack (remaster with real instrument from original creator)\",\"channel\":\"Arif Nur Rahman\",\"summary\":\"A complete remastered soundtrack for the Transport Tycoon 2014 mobile game, re-recorded with real instruments rather than the original MIDI. The music was composed by John Broomhall for the classic Transport Tycoon.\",\"why_hn\":\"Fans of Transport Tycoon and OpenTTD point to it as a nostalgic, live-instrument version of the game's music.\",\"content_type\":\"music\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"video game soundtrack\",\"categories\":[\"Music performance & songs\",\"Game history & retro gaming\"],\"tags\":[\"transport tycoon\",\"openttd\",\"john broomhall\",\"remaster\",\"retro gaming\"],\"quote\":\"The composer performed it with real instruments sometime in the past few years and it really brought on those nostalgic feelings\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"jSo5v5t4OQM\",\"title\":\"Jordan Peterson - Controversial Facts about IQ\",\"channel\":\"Bite-sized Philosophy\",\"summary\":\"A clip of psychologist Jordan Peterson lecturing on intelligence and IQ, covering how it is defined and its correlation with academic and work success.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters use it as supporting material in arguments about IQ and the g-factor view of intelligence.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"iq and intelligence\",\"categories\":[\"Psychology & human behavior\",\"Consciousness & cognition\"],\"tags\":[\"iq\",\"g factor\",\"jordan peterson\",\"psychology\",\"intelligence testing\"],\"quote\":\"IQ has an amazingly high correlation with academic success and work success\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"jyQwgBAaBag\",\"title\":\"Risking My Life To Settle A Physics Debate\",\"channel\":\"Veritasium\",\"summary\":\"Veritasium investigates whether a wind-powered vehicle can travel directly downwind faster than the wind, driving the Blackbird craft himself. The video explains the physics behind a claim some physics professors dispute.\",\"why_hn\":\"Frequently linked whenever wind-powered vehicles come up, as the clearest visual explanation of how the craft works.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"downwind faster than the wind\",\"categories\":[\"Fundamental physics\",\"Everyday physics & science demos\",\"Renewables, batteries & storage\"],\"tags\":[\"physics\",\"blackbird\",\"wind power\",\"sailing\",\"veritasium\"],\"quote\":\"The visual that made it click for me was at 18:54 in the original video\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"k1luKAS_Xcg\",\"title\":\"Find your primal posture and sit without back pain: Esther Gokhale at TEDxStanford\",\"channel\":\"TEDx Talks\",\"summary\":\"Esther Gokhale's short TEDx talk argues that the natural human spine is curved rather than straight and teaches a 'primal posture' for sitting without back pain.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended in back pain and posture threads as a short, practical idea that helped some commenters.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"posture and back pain\",\"categories\":[\"Fitness, sleep & longevity\",\"Product & industrial design\"],\"tags\":[\"posture\",\"back pain\",\"sitting\",\"ergonomics\",\"gokhale method\"],\"quote\":\"I found this Ted talk helped me with some lower back and leg issues.\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"kb-m2fasdDY\",\"title\":\"What I Wish I Had Known Before Scaling Uber to 1000 Services • Matt Ranney • GOTO 2016\",\"channel\":\"GOTO Conferences\",\"summary\":\"Matt Ranney of Uber talks at GOTO Chicago 2016 about what Uber learned from growing past 1,000 production microservices. He covers regrets, sprawl and organizational effects.\",\"why_hn\":\"Often brought up as an honest account of microservice sprawl, including the fact that nobody at Uber knew exactly how many services existed.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"microservices at scale\",\"categories\":[\"Software architecture & design\",\"Distributed systems\",\"Management, leadership & organizations\"],\"tags\":[\"microservices\",\"uber\",\"architecture\",\"service sprawl\",\"lessons learned\"],\"quote\":\"I enjoyed that video\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"kfM-yu0iQBk\",\"title\":\"Shader Basics, Blending & Textures • Shaders for Game Devs [Part 1]\",\"channel\":\"Freya Holmér\",\"summary\":\"The first part of Freya Holmer's three-part, nearly four-hour lecture on shader coding for game devs in Unity. It covers what shaders are, vertex and fragment shaders, interpolators, data types, blending and textures.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as a good first-steps shader tutorial that also teaches broad graphics knowledge.\",\"content_type\":\"course\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"shader programming\",\"categories\":[\"Computer graphics & rendering\",\"Game programming & engines\"],\"tags\":[\"shaders\",\"unity\",\"vertex shader\",\"fragment shader\",\"game graphics\"],\"quote\":\"I liked this tutorial series as a \\\"first steps\\\" by Freya Holmér\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"kixAljyfdqU\",\"title\":\"The Higgs Mechanism Explained | Space Time | PBS Digital Studios\",\"channel\":\"PBS Space Time\",\"summary\":\"PBS Space Time explains the Higgs field and how the Higgs mechanism gives particles mass within quantum field theory. It is a ten-minute overview for a lay audience.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as a clear explanation of how electrons gain mass, and the channel itself gets binge-watch praise.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"higgs mechanism\",\"categories\":[\"Quantum physics\"],\"tags\":[\"higgs field\",\"quantum field theory\",\"particle physics\",\"mass\",\"pbs space time\"],\"quote\":\"I think this video does a good job explaining it, much better than I could\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"klpDHn8viX8\",\"title\":\"The Holographic Universe Explained\",\"channel\":\"PBS Space Time\",\"summary\":\"A PBS Space Time episode explaining the holographic principle: the idea that our 3+1 dimensional universe may be described by information on a lower-dimensional boundary. It walks through black hole entropy and the AdS/CFT correspondence.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters point to it as an accessible way to get a grip on AdS/CFT and the holographic principle whenever black holes or information physics come up.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"holographic principle\",\"categories\":[\"Quantum physics\",\"Cosmology & astrophysics\"],\"tags\":[\"holographic principle\",\"ads/cft\",\"black holes\",\"theoretical physics\",\"pbs space time\"],\"quote\":\"Here's a video that made me feel like I almost half-way understood one of the main underlying principles (AdS/CFT correspondence)\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"lI7T650RTT8\",\"title\":\"No, The Tank Is Not Dead.\",\"channel\":\"The Chieftain\",\"summary\":\"Armor expert The Chieftain explains why tanks still have a place on the modern battlefield despite drones and guided weapons. He responds to the 'tank is dead' debate, noting that tanks have been declared dead repeatedly since 1919.\",\"why_hn\":\"Pointed to as an authoritative answer, from a former Abrams commander and tank historian, to claims that tanks are obsolete.\",\"content_type\":\"commentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"future of tanks\",\"categories\":[\"Military history & warfare\"],\"tags\":[\"tanks\",\"armored warfare\",\"drones\",\"military history\",\"combined arms\"],\"quote\":\"I'd recommend this video by a former Abrams commander and tank historian who presents this argument far better than I can.\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"lIFE7h3m40U\",\"title\":\"The Art of the Bodge: How I Made The Emoji Keyboard\",\"channel\":\"Tom Scott\",\"summary\":\"Tom Scott explains how he built his emoji keyboard, which turned out to be a series of bodged-together tools including AutoHotkey and LuaMacros. He tells stories about the value of quick-and-dirty solutions.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN links it as a charming defense of bodging and of AutoHotkey, and as the go-to example when custom keyboards or hotkey hacks come up.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"bodging and custom keyboards\",\"categories\":[\"Embedded systems & DIY electronics\",\"Software practice, open source & careers\",\"Consumer devices & gadgets\"],\"tags\":[\"autohotkey\",\"bodge\",\"emoji keyboard\",\"luamacros\",\"tom scott\"],\"quote\":\"I saw a good talk on it from Tom Scott some time ago[0] which I think is relevant.\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"lKQ0yaEJjok\",\"title\":\"Joscha: From Computation to Consciousness (31c3)\",\"channel\":\"Joscha Bach\",\"summary\":\"Joscha Bach's 31c3 talk argues that the mind should be understood as an information-processing system and that even the physical universe can be seen as computational. It tours the philosophy underlying artificial intelligence and cognitive science.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as an inspiring summary of a computational model of mind and a first lecture in a series on the brain as a computer.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"computational theory of mind\",\"categories\":[\"Consciousness & cognition\",\"Philosophy & ethics\",\"Machine learning & deep learning\"],\"tags\":[\"consciousness\",\"artificial intelligence\",\"philosophy of mind\",\"cognitive science\",\"joscha bach\"],\"quote\":\"stuff like talks by Joscha Bach on C3\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"lWZ2Bz0tS-s\",\"title\":\"MIT Godel Escher Bach Lecture 1\",\"channel\":\"jasonofthel33t\",\"summary\":\"The first lecture of an MIT course on Douglas Hofstadter's Godel, Escher, Bach, covering themes like self-reference, recursion and incompleteness. The full lecture series is on YouTube.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as a companion to reading the dense book.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"godel escher bach\",\"categories\":[\"University lectures & courses\",\"Computability & complexity theory\",\"Logic & formal methods\"],\"tags\":[\"godel escher bach\",\"self-reference\",\"recursion\",\"incompleteness\",\"mit course\"],\"quote\":\"They’re great to watch as you work through the book\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"lXfEK8G8CUI\",\"title\":\"How The Immune System ACTUALLY Works – IMMUNE\",\"channel\":\"Kurzgesagt – In a Nutshell\",\"summary\":\"A Kurzgesagt animation giving a big-picture tour of the human immune system, its organs, transport network, and the cells it produces every day. It accompanies their IMMUNE book.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters suggest it as a simple, well-made introduction to how immune responses work when discussing infection, T cells or chronic illness.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"immune system\",\"categories\":[\"Medicine & infectious disease\",\"Biology & biotechnology\"],\"tags\":[\"immune system\",\"biology\",\"kurzgesagt\",\"t cells\",\"animation\"],\"quote\":\"I like the series that Kurzgesagt made on the immune system as a way to explain this complexity a little more simply.\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"leX541Dr2rU\",\"title\":\"There is No Algorithm for Truth - with Tom Scott\",\"channel\":\"The Royal Institution\",\"summary\":\"Tom Scott's Royal Institution discourse on science communication in the age of social media, how recommendation algorithms shape popularity, and why view counts can matter more than truth. It includes a segment on parasocial relationships.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN returns to it in discussions of algorithmic feeds, misinformation and parasocial relationships, as a clear and thoughtful talk.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"algorithms and truth online\",\"categories\":[\"Scientific method & science communication\",\"Futurism & technology and society\",\"Media, journalism & propaganda\"],\"tags\":[\"social media\",\"algorithms\",\"science communication\",\"parasocial relationships\",\"tom scott\"],\"quote\":\"did a really good segment on the parasocial relationship as part of his talk to the Royal Institution\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"lgivCGdmFrw\",\"title\":\"Introducing a Memory-Safe Successor Language in Large C++ Code Bases - John McCall - CppNow 2023\",\"channel\":\"CppNow\",\"summary\":\"John McCall of Apple's Swift team presents at CppNow 2023 on introducing a memory-safe successor language into large C++ code bases. He argues incremental improvement of C++ hits hard limits and explains Swift's C++ interoperability for gradual migration.\",\"why_hn\":\"Used as the primary source for Apple's stated plan to make Swift its successor to C++ and for how interop supports gradual transition.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"advanced\",\"primary_topic\":\"swift c++ interoperability\",\"categories\":[\"C, C++, Rust, Go & systems languages\",\"Programming language design\",\"Security & hacking\"],\"tags\":[\"swift\",\"c++\",\"memory safety\",\"language interop\",\"apple\"],\"quote\":\"Finaly a recent gem from CppNow 2023\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"lhe286ky-9A\",\"title\":\"How to Fix America's Corrupt Political System\",\"channel\":\"RepresentUs\",\"summary\":\"A short RepresentUs explainer on how money corrupts American politics and what an anti-corruption act could do about it. It covers lobbying, super PACs and campaign finance, and pitches the group's proposed reforms. The video was later superseded by an updated version.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters cite it as a brief summary of the problem and the proposed Anti-Corruption Act solution when arguing for getting money out of politics.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"political corruption reform\",\"categories\":[\"US politics & elections\",\"Free speech, censorship & civil liberties\"],\"tags\":[\"campaign finance\",\"lobbying\",\"super pacs\",\"anti-corruption act\",\"us politics\"],\"quote\":\"I find these videos to be helpful in briefly understanding the problem and their proposed solution\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"lnA9DMvHtfI\",\"title\":\"Large Language Models from scratch\",\"channel\":\"Graphics in 5 Minutes\",\"summary\":\"A short cartoon explaining how language models like ChatGPT work, from autocomplete and conditional probabilities to neural networks, gradient descent and backpropagation. It shows how networks act as universal function approximators.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as a quick, non-dumbed-down way to get up to speed on how LLMs are built.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"large language models\",\"categories\":[\"LLMs & generative AI\",\"Machine learning & deep learning\"],\"tags\":[\"llm\",\"neural networks\",\"gradient descent\",\"transformers\",\"language modeling\"],\"quote\":\"I highly recommend this video by Steve Seitz\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"loCBvaj4eSg\",\"title\":\"The Hyperbolic Geometry of DMT Experiences (@Harvard Science of Psychedelics Club)\",\"channel\":\"Qualia Research Institute\",\"summary\":\"Andres Gomez Emilsson of the Qualia Research Institute presents a model of DMT experiences using hyperbolic geometry and dynamical systems theory. He describes how different intoxication levels correspond to changing curvature and information content of experience.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters point to it as an interesting lecture related to psychedelics, brain geometry and hyperbolic space, with a good intro to hyperbolic geometry.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"psychedelic phenomenology\",\"categories\":[\"Consciousness & cognition\",\"Neuroscience & the brain\",\"Pure mathematics\"],\"tags\":[\"dmt\",\"hyperbolic geometry\",\"consciousness\",\"qualia research institute\",\"psychedelics\"],\"quote\":\"has a good intro in the beginning.\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"lqzJewjZUkk\",\"title\":\"Turning the Sun Into a Giant Telescope with Dr. Slava Turyshev\",\"channel\":\"Fraser Cain\",\"summary\":\"Fraser Cain interviews JPL physicist Slava Turyshev about using the Sun's gravity as a lens to image distant exoplanets. They cover how the lens works, how a spacecraft would align with it, and mission design.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN points to it as the best accessible explanation of the solar gravitational lens mission plan.\",\"content_type\":\"interview\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"solar gravitational lens\",\"categories\":[\"Cosmology & astrophysics\",\"Space exploration & spaceflight\"],\"tags\":[\"gravitational lensing\",\"exoplanets\",\"jpl\",\"space telescope\",\"solar sail\"],\"quote\":\"It is the most interesting interview that I have seen in years, possibly ever.\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"lr4MmmWQtZM\",\"title\":\"Why is it so Easy to be Thin in Japan?\",\"channel\":\"Joseph Everett - WIL\",\"summary\":\"Joseph Everett compares the food environment of the US, with roughly 30% obesity, to Japan's, around 3.5%. He argues that what is available and normal to eat explains much of the gap.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters recommend it as an accurate account of everyday Japanese eating and of why the US food environment is so much worse.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"food environment and obesity\",\"categories\":[\"Nutrition & diet science\",\"Social issues & culture\"],\"tags\":[\"japan\",\"obesity\",\"diet\",\"food culture\",\"united states\"],\"quote\":\"This youtube video goes into more detail about the US problem compared to japan\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"lvTqbM5Dq4Q\",\"title\":\"How Quantum Computers Break Encryption | Shor's Algorithm Explained\",\"channel\":\"minutephysics\",\"summary\":\"MinutePhysics explains Shor's algorithm, showing how a quantum computer factors large integers by finding the period of a number modulo N. It walks through the modular arithmetic and number theory that give the exponential speedup over classical factoring, and why this threatens encryption.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters point to it as a clear, approachable explanation of Shor's algorithm and what quantum computers actually do.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"quantum computing\",\"categories\":[\"Quantum computing\",\"Cryptography\"],\"tags\":[\"shor's algorithm\",\"quantum factoring\",\"cryptography\",\"modular arithmetic\"],\"quote\":\"Minute Physics has a really good explanation of Shor's algorithm, and by proxy, Quantum computing.\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"m05abdGSOxY\",\"title\":\"Using ground relays with Starlink\",\"channel\":\"Mark Handley\",\"summary\":\"Mark Handley of UCL explains, with simulation visualizations, how early Starlink satellites without inter-satellite links could still give low-latency wide-area communication by hopping via ground relays. He shows it can beat fibre on many long paths.\",\"why_hn\":\"Cited in Starlink latency debates as a clear visual analysis showing sub-100ms latency is plausible even without inter-satellite links.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"starlink network latency\",\"categories\":[\"Networking & internet infrastructure\",\"Space exploration & spaceflight\"],\"tags\":[\"starlink\",\"satellite networking\",\"latency\",\"ground relays\",\"simulation\"],\"quote\":\"as Mark Handley has elegantly pointed out in his incredible video\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"mHtdZgou0qU\",\"title\":\"Speed Up Your JavaScript\",\"channel\":\"Google TechTalks\",\"summary\":\"A 2009 Google Tech Talk by Nicholas Zakas on the hidden performance pitfalls of JavaScript. It covers what is fast and slow in the language and how to avoid common bottlenecks.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as a classic JavaScript performance talk whose fundamentals about reflow and efficient code still apply.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"javascript performance\",\"categories\":[\"Software performance & optimization\",\"Web development\"],\"tags\":[\"javascript\",\"performance\",\"web performance\",\"reflow\",\"google tech talk\"],\"quote\":\"the fundamental ideas are still the same.\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"mSB71jNq-yQ\",\"title\":\"Attenborough: the amazing Lyre Bird sings like a chainsaw! Now in high quality | BBC Earth\",\"channel\":\"BBC Earth\",\"summary\":\"A short BBC Earth clip from David Attenborough's Life of Birds showing a lyrebird imitating sounds, including a chainsaw and camera shutter, so accurately it is hard to tell from the real thing.\",\"why_hn\":\"Shared whenever birds mimic sounds come up, as a mind-blowing example of vocal mimicry.\",\"content_type\":\"documentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"bird mimicry\",\"categories\":[\"Animals & nature\"],\"tags\":[\"lyrebird\",\"david attenborough\",\"vocal mimicry\",\"birdsong\",\"nature\"],\"quote\":\"Here's the one that blows my mind\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"muCPjK4nGY4\",\"title\":\"Speaking Piano - Now with (somewhat decent) captions!\",\"channel\":\"TheMcphearson\",\"summary\":\"A player piano is made to reproduce human speech, here reciting a European Environmental Criminal Court proclamation in German at the 2009 World Venice Forum. This upload adds subtitles so the words become understandable.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as the canonical talking-piano example, uncanny and impressive once you read the captions.\",\"content_type\":\"demo\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"speech on piano\",\"categories\":[\"Music performance & songs\",\"Computer vision, speech & NLP\",\"Magic, puzzles & curiosities\"],\"tags\":[\"talking piano\",\"speech synthesis\",\"auditory perception\",\"player piano\",\"music\"],\"quote\":\"This is a other great example\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"n068fel-W9I\",\"title\":\"Special Lecture: F-22 Flight Controls\",\"channel\":\"MIT OpenCourseWare\",\"summary\":\"A special lecture from MIT's Private Pilot Ground School in which Lt. Col. Randy Gordon, a fighter and test pilot, describes F-22 flight controls. Topics include thrust vectoring, the pilot command system and how software handles failures.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as a fascinating insider account of how the F-22's software makes it easier to fly than a Cessna.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"fighter jet flight controls\",\"categories\":[\"Aviation & aerospace engineering\",\"University lectures & courses\"],\"tags\":[\"f-22\",\"fly-by-wire\",\"thrust vectoring\",\"test pilot\",\"aviation\"],\"quote\":\"the talk is that good!\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"nIKhUizkXxA\",\"title\":\"DIY mass spectrometer measures potassium in dietary salt substitute\",\"channel\":\"Applied Science\",\"summary\":\"Ben Krasnow of Applied Science builds and operates a simple mass spectrometer, using it to measure potassium isotopes in a salt substitute. He explains the design choices and the theory behind it.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as an expert-made DIY build that explains why the apparatus is designed as it is, though one commenter notes a missing baseline run.\",\"content_type\":\"demo\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"diy mass spectrometry\",\"categories\":[\"Chemistry\",\"Everyday physics & science demos\",\"Embedded systems & DIY electronics\"],\"tags\":[\"mass spectrometer\",\"applied science\",\"ion beam\",\"isotopes\",\"diy science\"],\"quote\":\"he tries to explain why it's built the way it is and give you a good basis of the theory of operation.\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"nJslrTT-Yhc\",\"title\":\"Carbon Capture Isn't Real\",\"channel\":\"Adam Something\",\"summary\":\"Adam Something gives a short, sarcastic argument that carbon capture does not live up to its promises. He says the energy it uses would be better spent replacing fossil sources directly.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters cite it to argue carbon capture is a distraction from stopping emissions at the source.\",\"content_type\":\"commentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"carbon capture\",\"categories\":[\"Climate policy, solutions & debate\"],\"tags\":[\"carbon capture\",\"climate\",\"emissions\",\"energy\",\"electrification\"],\"quote\":\"100 times out of 100, you would rather just stop the emissions at the source by making the emitter use the green energy.\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"ncPyMUfNyVM\",\"title\":\"Andreas Antonopoulos - 51% Bitcoin Attack\",\"channel\":\"Rodolfo Díaz\",\"summary\":\"A two-minute clip of Andreas Antonopoulos explaining why a 51% attack on Bitcoin would not destroy it and what such an attack would cost. It is a succinct take on mining security.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters link it as a short, favorite explanation of Bitcoin's security and the sustainability of a 51% attack.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"bitcoin 51% attack\",\"categories\":[\"Cryptography\"],\"tags\":[\"bitcoin\",\"51% attack\",\"andreas antonopoulos\",\"mining security\",\"blockchain\"],\"quote\":\"Andreas Antonopoulos has a good take on this for Bitcoin\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"noQcWra6sbU\",\"title\":\"Netanel Rubin: The Perl Jam: Exploiting a 20 Year-old Vulnerability\",\"channel\":\"media.ccc.de\",\"summary\":\"Netanel Rubin's 31C3 talk chains Perl's syntax quirks into a powerful remote code execution attack, taking down popular Perl-based projects.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as a funny, engaging look at how Perl's quirks create real security vulnerabilities.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"perl security vulnerabilities\",\"categories\":[\"Security & hacking\",\"Python, Ruby & scripting languages\"],\"tags\":[\"perl\",\"rce\",\"web security\",\"language quirks\",\"31c3\"],\"quote\":\"try this great Perl presentation from Netanel Rubin\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"nsJZ_kKjXcE\",\"title\":\"(405) Choosing a High Security Lock\",\"channel\":\"Bill Johnson\",\"summary\":\"Lock-picking YouTuber Bill Johnson (BosnianBill) explains how to choose a high-security lock and why, showing examples and comparing designs. It covers what makes some padlocks more pick-resistant than others.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN users recommend it as an amusing, practical overview of padlocks from cheap to high security.\",\"content_type\":\"tutorial\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"high security locks\",\"categories\":[\"Security & hacking\",\"Home, DIY & practical skills\"],\"tags\":[\"lockpicking\",\"padlocks\",\"physical security\",\"pick resistance\"],\"quote\":\"Here's an amusing overview of available padlocks, starting with crap Master Locks and moving upward.\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"o2HBHckrdQc\",\"title\":\"Netdev 0x16 - Keynote: It's Time to Replace TCP in the Datacenter\",\"channel\":\"netdevconf\",\"summary\":\"John Ousterhout's Netdev 0x16 keynote arguing that nearly every aspect of TCP is wrong for the datacenter, from connections and streams to reliable delivery and congestion control. He presents the case for a replacement protocol.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as the thorough case for replacing TCP in datacenters and for Ousterhout's Homa protocol.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"advanced\",\"primary_topic\":\"datacenter transport protocols\",\"categories\":[\"Networking & internet infrastructure\",\"Distributed systems\"],\"tags\":[\"tcp\",\"homa\",\"john ousterhout\",\"rpc\",\"datacenter networking\",\"congestion control\"],\"quote\":\"The Netdev talk I've linked below is really good\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":3.0}",
    ",{\"vid\":\"o6L6XeNdd_k\",\"title\":\"Category Theory by Tom LaGatta\",\"channel\":\"AI Council\",\"summary\":\"Tom LaGatta's talk on category theory at a 2014 LispNYC meetup, covering objects, morphisms and the basic ideas in an informal, conversational style. It runs about 97 minutes.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as a more approachable introduction to category theory than the more formal lecture series people often start with.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"category theory\",\"categories\":[\"Pure mathematics\",\"Functional programming\",\"University lectures & courses\"],\"tags\":[\"category theory\",\"morphisms\",\"functional programming\",\"mathematics\",\"lisp meetup\"],\"quote\":\"Milewski's YouTube lectures gets mentioned a lot, but I couldn't make much sense of them before watching Tom LaGatta's more informal and conversational presentation\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"oFrTqQw0_3c\",\"title\":\"Brian Warner - Magic Wormhole- Simple Secure File Transfer - PyCon 2016.mp4\",\"channel\":\"PyCon 2016\",\"summary\":\"Brian Warner, author of magic-wormhole, presents the tool at PyCon 2016. He explains how a few secret words let two computers securely transfer files, covering the cryptography (NaCl, SPAKE2) and library use.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as the author's own explanation of the design and security of magic-wormhole.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"secure file transfer\",\"categories\":[\"Cryptography\",\"Python, Ruby & scripting languages\",\"Security & hacking\"],\"tags\":[\"magic-wormhole\",\"spake2\",\"cryptography\",\"python\",\"pycon\"],\"quote\":\"Author has a very good video on it at pycon-2016\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"oM6S7FEUfkU\",\"title\":\"LibreSSL: The first 30 days, and what the Future Holds\",\"channel\":\"Andrea Ross\",\"summary\":\"Bob Beck of OpenBSD gives a BSDCan 2014 talk on the first month of LibreSSL, the OpenSSL fork. He explains why they forked, what they removed, and examples of the problems found in the OpenSSL code base.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as a funny, fascinating tour of how bad the OpenSSL code was and what cleanup looks like.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"libressl and openssl\",\"categories\":[\"Cryptography\",\"Security & hacking\",\"Linux & open-source ecosystems\"],\"tags\":[\"libressl\",\"openssl\",\"openbsd\",\"code cleanup\",\"security\"],\"quote\":\"There is a fascinating and funny talk by Bob Beck from OpenBSD on the first 30 days after forking OpenSSL.\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"oOlDewpCfZQ\",\"title\":\"4 Chords | Music Videos | The Axis Of Awesome\",\"channel\":\"The Axis of Awesome\",\"summary\":\"A comedy-music medley from the Australian band The Axis of Awesome showing that dozens of famous pop songs use the same four-chord progression. The performers cycle through a long list of hits over the same chords.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters use it as the canonical demonstration that popular songs reuse one chord progression, especially in threads on music analysis and AI-generated music.\",\"content_type\":\"comedy\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"pop chord progressions\",\"categories\":[\"Music theory, synthesis & production\",\"Stand-up & sketch comedy\",\"Music performance & songs\"],\"tags\":[\"four chords\",\"pop music\",\"music theory\",\"medley\",\"comedy\"],\"quote\":\"the \\\"4 Chords\\\" medley by Axis of Awesome is a great demonstration of its ubiquity\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"oQbei5JGiT8\",\"title\":\"Tea Consent\",\"channel\":\"Blue Seat Studios\",\"summary\":\"A three-minute animated video that explains sexual consent through the analogy of offering someone a cup of tea. It is based on a blog post by Emmeline May.\",\"why_hn\":\"Used as the classic analogy for consent, applied to opt-out data collection and privacy practices.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"consent\",\"categories\":[\"Social issues & culture\"],\"tags\":[\"consent\",\"analogy\",\"animation\",\"opt-in vs opt-out\"],\"quote\":\"Here’s a very educational video on how giving consent works\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"oSmqbnhHp1c\",\"title\":\"RacketCon 2013: Dan Liebgold - Racket on the Playstation 3? It's Not What you Think!\",\"channel\":\"Racket\",\"summary\":\"Dan Liebgold of Naughty Dog gives a RacketCon 2013 keynote on using Racket in PlayStation 3 game development. He explains the Racket-based DSLs used for game data and scripting, which replaced the studio's earlier GOAL-based toolchain.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN cites it whenever Lisp in games, Naughty Dog or Racket in production comes up, as the main first-hand account.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"racket in game development\",\"categories\":[\"Functional programming\",\"Game programming & engines\"],\"tags\":[\"racket\",\"naughty dog\",\"lisp\",\"dsl\",\"game scripting\",\"playstation 3\"],\"quote\":\"There's a talk by one of the engine devs at RacketCon from a few years ago on youtube about how they use these DSLs\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"oTpA5jt1g60\",\"title\":\"Windows \\\"SUCKS\\\": How I'd Fix it by a retired Microsoft Windows engineer\",\"channel\":\"Dave's Garage\",\"summary\":\"Dave Plummer, a retired Microsoft engineer who wrote Task Manager, responds to the claim that Windows sucks. He describes how he would fix common annoyances, in what he calls a love letter to Windows wrapped in a rant.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN people share it in Windows 11 backlash threads as an even-handed insider's view of what is wrong with Windows.\",\"content_type\":\"commentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"windows usability\",\"categories\":[\"Operating systems & kernels\",\"Tech company stories & documentaries\",\"UX & UI design\"],\"tags\":[\"windows 11\",\"dave plummer\",\"microsoft\",\"operating systems\",\"task manager\"],\"quote\":\"I find this take (from the guy who wrote the Windows task manager while at Microsoft) to be evenhanded.\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"ooNzRrHA9VY\",\"title\":\"Why I Cook With Induction\",\"channel\":\"Jon Kung\",\"summary\":\"Chef Jon Kung explains why he cooks almost exclusively on induction, citing fast heating, cleanliness and precise temperature control. He shows a rounded induction wok burner.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN points to it in gas-versus-induction threads as a professional chef making the positive case, including for wok cooking.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"induction cooking\",\"categories\":[\"Cooking & food\",\"Power grid, heating & buildings\"],\"tags\":[\"induction\",\"wok\",\"cooking\",\"gas stoves\",\"kitchen\"],\"quote\":\"Jon Kung makes the positive case for an induction wok better than I can\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"orMtwOz6Db0\",\"title\":\"Roger Penrose: Physics of Consciousness and the Infinite Universe | Lex Fridman Podcast #85\",\"channel\":\"Lex Fridman\",\"summary\":\"Lex Fridman interviews physicist Roger Penrose on consciousness, the limits of computation, quantum mechanics and his cyclic cosmology. It covers his views on AI and the big bang.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as an accessible walkthrough of Penrose's ideas on consciousness and cosmology, interesting even if debatable.\",\"content_type\":\"podcast\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"physics of consciousness\",\"categories\":[\"Consciousness & cognition\",\"Fundamental physics\",\"Tech pioneers & interviews\"],\"tags\":[\"roger penrose\",\"consciousness\",\"cosmology\",\"quantum mechanics\",\"artificial intelligence\"],\"quote\":\"Penrose delivered a very accessible walkthrough of his historical and current thoughts on the topic.\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"osSMJRyxG0k\",\"title\":\"Intel - 