omy.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters recommend it for a more cynical view of central bank power, though some caveat it as conspiracy-leaning.\",\"content_type\":\"documentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"central banking\",\"categories\":[\"Macroeconomics & financial crises\",\"Documentaries & storytelling\"],\"tags\":[\"bank of japan\",\"richard werner\",\"japanese economy\",\"asset bubble\",\"monetary policy\"],\"quote\":\"did a good job of illustrating the dangers of having such high powered unaccountable cabals\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"pIgMTsQXg3Q\",\"title\":\"The Constitution of Athens\",\"channel\":\"Historia Civilis\",\"summary\":\"Historia Civilis explains the constitution of ancient Athens, including its assembly, offices and use of sortition to choose leaders. It draws on Aristotle and Plutarch.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended in governance threads for showing how randomness and short terms prevented power concentration.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"athenian democracy\",\"categories\":[\"World history\"],\"tags\":[\"ancient athens\",\"sortition\",\"constitution\",\"history\",\"democracy\"],\"quote\":\"This is a good video on this\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"pL3Yzjk5R4M\",\"title\":\"The Original Skunk Works – Nickolas Means | The Lead Developer UK 2017\",\"channel\":\"LeadDev\",\"summary\":\"Nickolas Means tells the story of Lockheed's Skunk Works and how its engineers built the U-2, SR-71 and F-117 under tight deadlines and budgets. He draws lessons for modern software teams.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as a well-done history of Skunk Works, popping up in SR-71 threads.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"skunk works engineering history\",\"categories\":[\"Software practice, open source & careers\",\"Aviation & aerospace engineering\",\"Management, leadership & organizations\"],\"tags\":[\"skunk works\",\"sr-71\",\"lockheed\",\"engineering management\",\"aerospace\"],\"quote\":\"I would also recommend this conference talk on the subject by Nickolas Means, it was very well done\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"pUF5esTscZI\",\"title\":\"A4 Paper Is Too Perfect\",\"channel\":\"CGP Grey\",\"summary\":\"CGP Grey explains the elegant ratio of A4 and the metric paper sizes, then zooms out across scales from the Planck length to the observable universe.\",\"why_hn\":\"Loved for its zoom-out tour of physical scales and recommended alongside other paper-size and scale-of-the-universe discussions.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"a4 paper sizes\",\"categories\":[\"Recreational & visual math\",\"Physics & space (other)\"],\"tags\":[\"a4 paper\",\"metric system\",\"scale of the universe\",\"iso 216\",\"cgp grey\"],\"quote\":\"the most psychedelic experience one can have without doing drugs\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"pdJQ8iVTwj8\",\"title\":\"Chris Lattner: Future of Programming and AI | Lex Fridman Podcast #381\",\"channel\":\"Lex Fridman\",\"summary\":\"Lex Fridman's long podcast conversation with Chris Lattner, creator of Swift, LLVM and MLIR and co-founder of Modular. Topics include the Mojo language, the future of programming and AI, and hardware.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN points to it as the main place to hear Lattner's motivations and design ideas for Mojo, often in Mojo threads.\",\"content_type\":\"podcast\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"programming languages and ai\",\"categories\":[\"Compilers, interpreters & program analysis\",\"Programming language design\",\"LLMs & generative AI\"],\"tags\":[\"chris lattner\",\"mojo\",\"llvm\",\"swift\",\"compilers\",\"lex fridman\"],\"quote\":\"further emboldened my stance that if Mojo can succeed, Lattner is one of the best-positioned engineers to see it through\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"pdmODVYPDLA\",\"title\":\"I Built a COMPUTER in Magic: The Gathering\",\"channel\":\"Because Science\",\"summary\":\"Kyle Hill of Because Science explains and demonstrates, using real cards, how Magic: The Gathering can be used to build a Turing machine, a computer inside the card game.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN links it as the accessible, hands-on demo behind the claim that Magic is Turing complete.\",\"content_type\":\"demo\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"magic the gathering turing completeness\",\"categories\":[\"Computability & complexity theory\",\"Chess, board games & puzzles\"],\"tags\":[\"turing machine\",\"magic the gathering\",\"computation\",\"card games\",\"emergent complexity\"],\"quote\":\"Because Science did a thorough breakdown and demonstration, using actual cards\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"pnv5iKB2hl4\",\"title\":\"Why city flags may be the worst-designed thing you've never noticed | Roman Mars\",\"channel\":\"TED\",\"summary\":\"Roman Mars gives a funny TED talk on vexillology, laying out five basic principles of good flag design and why many city flags fail them.\",\"why_hn\":\"The canonical reference on flag and logo design principles, cited in branding and design threads.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"flag design\",\"categories\":[\"Graphic design & typography\",\"Transit, cities & urban planning\"],\"tags\":[\"vexillology\",\"design principles\",\"roman mars\",\"city flags\",\"branding\"],\"quote\":\"one must watch the short talk on state and country flags\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"pyX8kQ-JzHI\",\"title\":\"Lecture 1 | Modern Physics: Classical Mechanics (Stanford)\",\"channel\":\"Stanford\",\"summary\":\"The first lecture of Leonard Susskind's Stanford Continuing Studies course on modern physics, beginning with classical mechanics. It is the start of a six-quarter sequence on the theoretical foundations of physics.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends Susskind as a very good explainer who uses real math at an accessible level, and links this lecture series for self-learners.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"classical mechanics\",\"categories\":[\"Fundamental physics\",\"University lectures & courses\"],\"tags\":[\"susskind\",\"physics\",\"stanford\",\"theoretical minimum\",\"lecture series\"],\"quote\":\"He is a very good explainer.\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"qL0BB2PRY7k\",\"title\":\"Was Roman Concrete Better?\",\"channel\":\"Practical Engineering\",\"summary\":\"Practical Engineering compares modern concrete with that of the Roman Empire, covering modern techniques like roller compacted concrete and water-reducing admixtures. It explains the tradeoffs of Roman construction versus reinforced concrete.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as a clear, balanced answer to claims that Roman concrete was better than ours.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"roman vs modern concrete\",\"categories\":[\"Civil engineering & construction\",\"Materials science\"],\"tags\":[\"concrete\",\"roman engineering\",\"civil engineering\",\"rebar\",\"materials\"],\"quote\":\"Here's a good video on the use and tradeoffs of Roman concrete engineering\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"qMkYlIA7mgw\",\"title\":\"The US-Canada Border Is 5,500 Miles of Pure Nonsense\",\"channel\":\"CGP Grey\",\"summary\":\"CGP Grey walks through the odd history and quirks of the US-Canada border, including how it was surveyed and its irregularities such as exclaves.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as an entertaining overview in any thread about border oddities.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"us-canada border\",\"categories\":[\"World history\",\"Geopolitics & foreign policy\"],\"tags\":[\"borders\",\"geography\",\"canada\",\"exclaves\",\"history\"],\"quote\":\"There's a great CGP Grey video on the Canada-US border well worth watching if this interests you\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"qQTjLWIHN74\",\"title\":\"The One Tiny Law That Keeps Amtrak Terrible\",\"channel\":\"Wendover Productions\",\"summary\":\"Wendover Productions explains why Amtrak trains run late: most Amtrak routes run on freight-owned track, and the law giving passenger trains priority is rarely enforced. The video covers rail ownership versus control and how freight carriers hold the leverage.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters cite it whenever passenger rail delays or expansion come up, as the clearest explanation of freight priority over Amtrak.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"US passenger rail\",\"categories\":[\"Transit, cities & urban planning\",\"Law, policing & justice\"],\"tags\":[\"amtrak\",\"freight rail\",\"rail policy\",\"infrastructure\",\"right of way\"],\"quote\":\"Wendover Productions made a great video [0] explaining this situation.\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"qS34oV-jv_A\",\"title\":\"that dark matter video aged like milk\",\"channel\":\"Angela Collier \",\"summary\":\"Angela Collier, a physicist, revisits her earlier dark matter video, explaining dark matter as a set of observations rather than a theory and discussing MOND comments.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN points to her videos as a counterpoint to popular dark matter discussion, framing it as the discrepancy that needs explaining.\",\"content_type\":\"commentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"dark matter\",\"categories\":[\"Cosmology & astrophysics\",\"Scientific method & science communication\"],\"tags\":[\"dark matter\",\"mond\",\"astrophysics\",\"angela collier\",\"rotation curves\"],\"quote\":\"these YouTube videos are a nice counterpoint to a lot of popular communication and discussion on dark matter\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"qbKGw8MQ0i8\",\"title\":\"\\\"NTFS really isn't that bad\\\" - Robert Collins (LCA 2020)\",\"channel\":\"linux.conf.au\",\"summary\":\"Robert Collins explains why rustup installs were painfully slow on Windows and how changes to rustup and Windows cut install time from 3m30s to 14s. The talk covers NTFS, small files and the cost of antivirus hooks on file close.\",\"why_hn\":\"The go-to reference for why file operations are slow on Windows and how antivirus filters, not NTFS itself, are the cause.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"advanced\",\"primary_topic\":\"windows filesystem performance\",\"categories\":[\"Software performance & optimization\",\"Operating systems & kernels\",\"C, C++, Rust, Go & systems languages\"],\"tags\":[\"ntfs\",\"rustup\",\"antivirus\",\"file io\",\"performance\"],\"quote\":\"This is a great talk on the topic\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"r1ZLGqL1FMo\",\"title\":\"Why Japan Arrests Foreigners\",\"channel\":\"Paolo fromTOKYO\",\"summary\":\"Paolo fromTOKYO explains why foreign tourists get arrested in Japan, covering strict laws and the justice system's very high conviction rate.\",\"why_hn\":\"Commenters mostly use it as a lead-in to recommending interviews with Japanese lawyers about detention and the justice system, and one says they learned from it.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"japanese law for foreigners\",\"categories\":[\"Law, policing & justice\",\"Social issues & culture\"],\"tags\":[\"japan\",\"criminal justice\",\"police\",\"travel\",\"conviction rate\"],\"quote\":\"I enjoyed learning from this video years ago\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"r9rGX91rq5I\",\"title\":\"The UK Election Results Were the Worst in History\",\"channel\":\"CGP Grey\",\"summary\":\"CGP Grey explains how the 2015 UK general election showed the flaws of first-past-the-post voting, with parties like UKIP winning many votes but few seats.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as a short, clear illustration of how first-past-the-post misrepresents voters.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"first past the post voting\",\"categories\":[\"US politics & elections\",\"Politics & history (other)\"],\"tags\":[\"uk elections\",\"first past the post\",\"electoral systems\",\"proportional representation\",\"cgp grey\"],\"quote\":\"Great CGP Grey video on the 2015 General Election in the UK\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"rAl-9HwD858\",\"title\":\"Crust of Rust: Lifetime Annotations\",\"channel\":\"Jon Gjengset\",\"summary\":\"In this first Crust of Rust episode, Jon Gjengset live-codes an example that needs multiple explicit lifetime annotations. Along the way he covers string types and generics over a custom trait.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as a slow-paced, detailed live-coding session that makes lifetimes click once you are past Rust basics.\",\"content_type\":\"tutorial\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"rust lifetimes\",\"categories\":[\"C, C++, Rust, Go & systems languages\",\"Programming language design\"],\"tags\":[\"rust\",\"lifetimes\",\"live coding\",\"strings\",\"generics\"],\"quote\":\"each one is actually writing carefully chosen code that runs and talking through what's going on\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"rHP-OPXK2ig\",\"title\":\"How I Upgraded My iPhone Memory 800% - in Shenzhen, China\",\"channel\":\"Strange Parts\",\"summary\":\"Scotty Allen of Strange Parts travels through Shenzhen's Huaqiangbei markets and learns to upgrade an iPhone 6s from 16GB to 128GB by swapping its flash chip. It is part 3 of his custom iPhone series and shows the difficulty of the hands-on repair work.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN people recommend it for its vivid look at Shenzhen's repair culture and for the flash chip upgrade, and cite it in discussions of hardware modding.\",\"content_type\":\"documentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"iphone hardware modding\",\"categories\":[\"Repair, teardowns & right to repair\",\"Consumer devices & gadgets\"],\"tags\":[\"shenzhen\",\"iphone\",\"flash memory\",\"repair\",\"electronics\"],\"quote\":\"it gives such a cool sense of Shenzhen as a place to make and modify things\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"rURRYI66E54\",\"title\":\"AI Language Models & Transformers - Computerphile\",\"channel\":\"Computerphile\",\"summary\":\"Rob Miles explains on Computerphile how language models generate plausible text and how transformers improved on recurrent networks, with a look at what comes next.\",\"why_hn\":\"Pointed to as an accessible early explanation of transformers and attention for newcomers.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"transformers and language models\",\"categories\":[\"LLMs & generative AI\",\"Machine learning & deep learning\",\"Computer vision, speech & NLP\"],\"tags\":[\"transformers\",\"language models\",\"attention\",\"rob miles\",\"computerphile\"],\"quote\":\"You can check-out his other videos on NLP as well they are all excellent.\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"ra_9zU-mnl8\",\"title\":\"Gridfinity: Your Ultimate Modular Workshop is FREE!\",\"channel\":\"Zack Freedman\",\"summary\":\"Zack Freedman introduces Gridfinity, his free, open-source modular workshop storage system of 3D-printable bins and baseplates. The video walks through the design, the parametric templates and the many module types that fit parts, tools and accessories.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN points people to it as the original explanation of what Gridfinity is and why it exists, in threads about the project and its catalogs.\",\"content_type\":\"demo\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"3d printed storage\",\"categories\":[\"3D printing, CNC & maker projects\",\"Woodworking, metalworking & workshop skills\"],\"tags\":[\"gridfinity\",\"3d printing\",\"workshop organization\",\"open source hardware\",\"modular storage\"],\"quote\":\"Watching Zack's original video on the concept really helps to cement why this exists.\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"rgGmKD87U3M\",\"title\":\"Lotus Improv for NeXT 1990\",\"channel\":\"Thierry Hubert\",\"summary\":\"A 1990 Lotus sales demo of Improv, a spreadsheet built for the NeXT computer that rethought the grid with named categories and multiple views. The uploader, who tested it at Price Waterhouse, presents it as an early, innovative vision of the spreadsheet.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN links it in spreadsheet-reinvention threads as the clearest look at what Improv did differently, with a distinctive NeXTstep aesthetic.\",\"content_type\":\"demo\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"lotus improv spreadsheet\",\"categories\":[\"GUI, HCI & interface history\",\"History of computing\",\"UX & UI design\"],\"tags\":[\"lotus improv\",\"next\",\"spreadsheets\",\"retro software\",\"ui design\"],\"quote\":\"for anyone who hasn't seen Lotus Improv before and is both curious about what it did differently\",\"recommenders\":1,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"rmueBVrLKcY\",\"title\":\"Computer Science - A Guide for the Perplexed • Joe Armstrong • GOTO 2018\",\"channel\":\"GOTO Conferences\",\"summary\":\"Joe Armstrong's GOTO 2018 talk contrasts the problems computer scientists study with the problems users have. He revisits forgotten great ideas such as tuple spaces, flow-based programming, Xanadu and Unix pipes, with anecdotes.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN calls it a superb, funny and engaging talk, and links it often after Armstrong's death as one of his best.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"history of computer science\",\"categories\":[\"History of computing\",\"Functional programming\",\"Software (other)\"],\"tags\":[\"joe armstrong\",\"erlang\",\"forgotten ideas\",\"tuple spaces\",\"unix pipes\"],\"quote\":\"One of my favorite talks by Joe Armstrong\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"s0ukXDnWlTY\",\"title\":\"The Smartphone CPU Review You NEED to Watch!\",\"channel\":\"Geekerwan\",\"summary\":\"Geekerwan reviews smartphone CPUs by power efficiency rather than benchmark scores, with test methodology and chapters on Qualcomm, Apple, Samsung and Google chips. It explains why high benchmark scores can coexist with heat and poor battery life.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN people cite it as detailed data on mobile SoC efficiency, sustained performance and ARM core efficiency curves.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"smartphone cpu power efficiency\",\"categories\":[\"Computer architecture & CPUs\",\"Consumer devices & gadgets\"],\"tags\":[\"mobile soc\",\"power efficiency\",\"arm cores\",\"thermal throttling\",\"benchmarks\"],\"quote\":\"explores the power efficiency graphs and that's probably what most phone users really want\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"s9aCwCKgkLo\",\"title\":\"What's It Really like Working at Valve?\",\"channel\":\"People Make Games\",\"summary\":\"People Make Games interviewed 16 current and former Valve employees to examine how the company's no-bosses, no-titles structure works in practice, and where it falls short.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as well-researched, interview-based coverage of the Valve model and its failures, often linked in threads about Valve and flat organizations.\",\"content_type\":\"documentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"valve company culture\",\"categories\":[\"Management, leadership & organizations\",\"Tech company stories & documentaries\",\"Gaming culture & analysis\"],\"tags\":[\"valve\",\"flat organization\",\"game industry\",\"employee interviews\",\"management\"],\"quote\":\"People Make Games made an enlightening documentary on the Valve model and where it fails\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"s9fByRmAHgU\",\"title\":\"Jacob Appelbaum (Part 2/2) Digital Anti-Repression Workshop - April 26 2012\",\"channel\":\"NewYorkRawVideos\",\"summary\":\"Part 2 of a Q&A workshop with security researcher Jacob Appelbaum from April 2012, covering mobile phone security, cellphone forensics, state repression and surveillance. Topics include booting Tails from USB and surveillance self-defense resources.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as a practical talk on privacy and anonymity options, including diskless laptops running Tails.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"digital privacy and surveillance\",\"categories\":[\"Privacy, surveillance & cybercrime\",\"Security & hacking\"],\"tags\":[\"tails\",\"tor\",\"jacob appelbaum\",\"cellphone forensics\",\"anti-surveillance\"],\"quote\":\"Worth listening to Jake Appelbaum's 'digital anti repression workshop' [1].\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"sCZJblyT_XM\",\"title\":\"The Unix Chainsaw - Gary Bernhardt\",\"channel\":\"timmonta\",\"summary\":\"Gary Bernhardt's Cascadia Ruby Conference 2011 talk on using the Unix shell for practical problem solving. He builds pipelines interactively by iterative refinement, using small tools as levers.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as a brilliant demonstration of how to think in Unix pipelines.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"unix shell\",\"categories\":[\"Testing, debugging & developer tools\",\"Operating systems & kernels\"],\"tags\":[\"unix\",\"pipelines\",\"command line\",\"gary bernhardt\",\"shell workflow\"],\"quote\":\"gave a great talk about practical problem solving with the unix shell\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":3.0},{\"vid\":\"sI1C9DyIi_8\",\"title\":\"Arithmetic, Population and Energy - FULL LENGTH\",\"channel\":\"kip399\",\"summary\":\"Physicist Albert Bartlett's full-length lecture on how steady exponential growth in population and energy use collides with finite resources. It uses examples like bacteria in a bottle and covers peak oil.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN repeatedly quotes its thesis that humanity's greatest shortcoming is failing to understand the exponential function.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"exponential growth and sustainability\",\"categories\":[\"Climate policy, solutions & debate\",