which hand-drawn strokes become boxes, connections and dataflow-style programs.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN points to it as proof that the 1960s already had modeless, sketch-based interfaces that remain impressive today.\",\"content_type\":\"demo\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"early graphical programming\",\"categories\":[\"GUI, HCI & interface history\",\"History of computing\"],\"tags\":[\"alan kay\",\"grail\",\"visual programming\",\"computing history\",\"user interface\"],\"quote\":\"Pretty amazing stuff, even today.\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.5},{\"vid\":\"QUjYy4Ksy1E\",\"title\":\"Why Gifted Kids Are Actually Special Needs\",\"channel\":\"HealthyGamerGG\",\"summary\":\"Dr. K of HealthyGamerGG argues that gifted kids can be seen as special needs, tracing a path from the gifted label to burnout and perfectionism.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters call it enlightening for understanding the emotional challenges of gifted kids, especially those who struggle later in school or college.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"gifted children psychology\",\"categories\":[\"Mental health & neurodiversity\",\"Psychology & human behavior\",\"Education & learning\"],\"tags\":[\"gifted kids\",\"burnout\",\"perfectionism\",\"mental health\",\"education\"],\"quote\":\"It is a truly excellent video for helping to understand some of the psychological/emotional challenges of being\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.5},{\"vid\":\"QeNmydIk8Yo\",\"title\":\"The Default Mode Network & End of Suffering - Gary Weber\",\"channel\":\"Non-Dual Couch\",\"summary\":\"A dialogue with Gary Weber on the default mode network, meditation and non-dual awakening, with discussion of psychedelics, thought and time. It is part of the Non-Dual Couch series.\",\"why_hn\":\"Cited to explain the default mode network and how meditation may quiet it.\",\"content_type\":\"interview\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"default mode network and meditation\",\"categories\":[\"Neuroscience & the brain\",\"Religion, belief & skepticism\",\"Consciousness & cognition\"],\"tags\":[\"default mode network\",\"meditation\",\"non-duality\",\"neuroscience\",\"psychedelics\"],\"quote\":\"I highly recommend this talk given by a physicist exploring what spirituality does to the brain\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.5},{\"vid\":\"QfuK8la0y6s\",\"title\":\"Fermi Paradox: The Dyson Dilemma v2.0\",\"channel\":\"Isaac Arthur\",\"summary\":\"Isaac Arthur examines the Dyson Dilemma, the puzzle of why we see no Dyson spheres or megastructures if growth-oriented civilizations should build them. He covers detectability and whether such structures could hide.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended in Fermi paradox threads as a thorough and reasoned treatment of Dyson sphere detectability.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"fermi paradox\",\"categories\":[\"Cosmology & astrophysics\",\"Futurism & technology and society\"],\"tags\":[\"dyson sphere\",\"fermi paradox\",\"kardashev scale\",\"seti\",\"megastructures\",\"isaac arthur\"],\"quote\":\"He describes the Fermi Paradox and related concepts 100x better than anyone else.\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.5},{\"vid\":\"QspuCt1FM9M\",\"title\":\"Justin Bieber 800% Slower\",\"channel\":\"turinpt2\",\"summary\":\"A Justin Bieber song slowed down 800% with the PaulStretch program, turning a pop track into a long ambient drone.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN points to it as the canonical example of extreme audio stretching making pop music into something entirely different and relaxing.\",\"content_type\":\"music\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"extreme audio time stretching\",\"categories\":[\"Music theory, synthesis & production\",\"Information theory & signal processing\",\"Music performance & songs\"],\"tags\":[\"paulstretch\",\"ambient\",\"drone\",\"time stretching\",\"audio experiment\"],\"quote\":\"I'm not even kidding. It's superb.\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.5},{\"vid\":\"QtxVdC7pBQM\",\"title\":\"The Ridiculous Engineering Of Jet Engines\",\"channel\":\"Veritasium\",\"summary\":\"Veritasium explains how jet engines run hotter than their components' melting points, using cooling air and single-crystal superalloy turbine blades. It covers how the blades are grown as aligned crystals.\",\"why_hn\":\"Commenters send it to people asking how engines survive such temperatures, and call it fascinating and worth watching.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"jet engine turbine blades\",\"categories\":[\"Aviation & aerospace engineering\",\"Materials science\",\"Mechanical & precision engineering\"],\"tags\":[\"jet engines\",\"superalloys\",\"single crystal blades\",\"turbine cooling\",\"materials science\",\"veritasium\"],\"quote\":\"is absolutely fascinating & well worth a watch\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.5},{\"vid\":\"R37zkizucPU\",\"title\":\"Louis CK honors George Carlin\",\"channel\":\"Bromista5\",\"summary\":\"Louis CK speaks at a 2010 NYPL tribute to George Carlin about how Carlin's habit of discarding material and writing a new hour each year changed his own career. He becomes emotional describing it.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN finds the story inspiring beyond comedy: getting good by throwing out your old work and starting over.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"creative process in comedy\",\"categories\":[\"Stand-up & sketch comedy\",\"Tech pioneers & interviews\"],\"tags\":[\"louis ck\",\"george carlin\",\"stand-up\",\"creativity\",\"new material\"],\"quote\":\"His story is not only inspiring, it transcends comedy.\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.5},{\"vid\":\"R3ZJKN_5M44\",\"title\":\"Don't Buy Stuff - Saturday Night Live\",\"channel\":\"Saturday Night Live\",\"summary\":\"A 2006 Saturday Night Live fake infomercial with Steve Martin and Amy Poehler selling the sure-fire way out of debt: don't buy stuff.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN posts it as a favorite comic summary of debt avoidance and spending discipline, and as a joke in buy-now-pay-later threads.\",\"content_type\":\"comedy\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"debt and consumer spending\",\"categories\":[\"Satire & parody\",\"Stand-up & sketch comedy\",\"Investing & personal finance\"],\"tags\":[\"snl\",\"debt\",\"personal finance\",\"sketch comedy\",\"credit cards\"],\"quote\":\"This is my favorite video that explains it all\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.5},{\"vid\":\"RNsEsZbXE-4\",\"title\":\"RustConf 2020 - Closing Keynote by Siân Griffin\",\"channel\":\"Rust Programming Language\",\"summary\":\"RustConf 2020 closing keynote by Sian Griffin that dives into Pokemon Blue glitches, notably Missingno and arbitrary-code-execution tricks. It uses them to explain how memory unsafety works and how memory-safe languages like Rust prevent such bugs.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN points to it as a fun, technically deep talk that makes memory-safety bugs vivid through a beloved game.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"memory safety and game glitches\",\"categories\":[\"C, C++, Rust, Go & systems languages\",\"Speedrunning, glitches & game hacking\",\"Security & hacking\"],\"tags\":[\"pokemon\",\"missingno\",\"rust\",\"memory safety\",\"glitches\",\"keynote\"],\"quote\":\"I really enjoyed the RustConf 2020 Closing Keynote\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.5},{\"vid\":\"Rqfkuc5mawg\",\"title\":\"Nathan Barley - Episode 1\",\"channel\":\"genuinelyfunnynazi\",\"summary\":\"The first episode of Nathan Barley, Chris Morris and Charlie Brooker's British sitcom satirizing hip media and startup scenesters in mid-2000s Shoreditch. Cynical writer Dan Ashcroft finds himself idolized by the moronic Nathan Barley.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as a savage, ahead-of-its-time parody of startup and tech culture.\",\"content_type\":\"comedy\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"tech culture satire\",\"categories\":[\"Satire & parody\",\"Film & TV clips\",\"Computing culture (other)\"],\"tags\":[\"nathan barley\",\"british comedy\",\"chris morris\",\"shoreditch\",\"satire\"],\"quote\":\"I can highly, highly , recommend Nathan Barley which was ahead of its time\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.5},{\"vid\":\"S0mviKhVmBI\",\"title\":\"Bringing the Unix Philosophy to Big Data by Bryan Cantrill: FutureStack13\",\"channel\":\"New Relic\",\"summary\":\"Bryan Cantrill presents Manta, an object store with in-situ compute that lets tools like grep, awk, and sed run map-reduce style over huge data. He frames it as bringing the Unix philosophy to big data.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as an elegant argument for simple Unix tools replacing heavyweight Hadoop-style pipelines.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"unix philosophy for big data\",\"categories\":[\"Databases & storage\",\"Operating systems & kernels\",\"Data science & statistics\"],\"tags\":[\"bryan cantrill\",\"manta\",\"unix\",\"map-reduce\",\"object storage\"],\"quote\":\"The Bryan Cantrill talk is definitely worth your time\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.5},{\"vid\":\"S4gU2Tsv6hY\",\"title\":\"The Iroquois Confederacy\",\"channel\":\"Historia Civilis\",\"summary\":\"Historia Civilis explains the origins and governing system of the Iroquois (Haudenosaunee) Confederacy. It covers the Great Law and the league's surprisingly intricate legal and political structure.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it for anyone interested in legal systems, governance, or history.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"iroquois confederacy governance\",\"categories\":[\"World history\",\"Law, policing & justice\"],\"tags\":[\"iroquois\",\"haudenosaunee\",\"legal systems\",\"indigenous history\",\"great law of peace\"],\"quote\":\"Highly recommend for anyone interested in legal systems or history in general\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.5},{\"vid\":\"SEDuVF7kiPU\",\"title\":\"2017/08/08: James Damore and his Google Memo on Diversity  (complete)\",\"channel\":\"Jordan B Peterson\",\"summary\":\"Jordan Peterson's roughly 50-minute interview with James Damore, the fired Google engineer, going through his diversity memo nearly line by line. The description links the scientific literature behind the claims.\",\"why_hn\":\"During the 2017 memo controversy, HN commenters pointed to it as Damore's own account and a detailed walk-through of the memo's sources.\",\"content_type\":\"interview\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"google diversity memo\",\"categories\":[\"Free speech, censorship & civil liberties\",\"Social issues & culture\"],\"tags\":[\"james damore\",\"jordan peterson\",\"google memo\",\"diversity\",\"gender differences\",\"free speech\"],\"quote\":\"See Jordan Peterson's interview with James Damore, where he goes through the memo almost line-by-line.\",\"recommenders\":8,\"score\":2.5},{\"vid\":\"SEwTjZvy8vw\",\"title\":\"2023 LLVM Dev Mtg - Mojo 🔥: A system programming language for heterogenous computing\",\"channel\":\"LLVM\",\"summary\":\"A 2023 LLVM Developers' Meeting keynote by Abdul Dakkak, Chris Lattner and Jeff Niu introducing Mojo, a Python-family systems language built on MLIR and LLVM for heterogeneous computing.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN points to it as the best deep dive into Mojo's design, including MLIR use and SIMD-first scalars.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"advanced\",\"primary_topic\":\"mojo programming language\",\"categories\":[\"Compilers, interpreters & program analysis\",\"C, C++, Rust, Go & systems languages\",\"Programming language design\"],\"tags\":[\"mojo\",\"mlir\",\"llvm\",\"compilers\",\"chris lattner\"],\"quote\":\"this video totally changed my view\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.5},{\"vid\":\"SGUCcjHTmGY\",\"title\":\"OpenAI Codex Live Demo\",\"channel\":\"OpenAI\",\"summary\":\"OpenAI's 2021 live demo of Codex translating natural language into code, from Hello World to building a game and a Microsoft Word plugin.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN points to it as an early, impressive demonstration that LLMs could write working code.\",\"content_type\":\"demo\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"ai code generation\",\"categories\":[\"LLMs & generative AI\"],\"tags\":[\"openai codex\",\"code generation\",\"llm\",\"live demo\",\"programming\"],\"quote\":\"Kind of changed my whole view about programming and it's future.\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.5},{\"vid\":\"SK4ezQrTqFw\",\"title\":\"Chris Sacca of Lowercase Capital Pt. 2- TWiST #295\",\"channel\":\"This Week in Startups\",\"summary\":\"Part two of Jason Calacanis's long interview with investor Chris Sacca on This Week in Startups. Sacca discusses his investments in Facebook, Twitter and Uber, and how he hustled his way to the top.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN calls it a favorite startup video because it is packed with candid stories about Facebook, Twitter and Google.\",\"content_type\":\"interview\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"startup investing stories\",\"categories\":[\"Startups & entrepreneurship\",\"Investing & personal finance\",\"Tech pioneers & interviews\"],\"tags\":[\"chris sacca\",\"venture capital\",\"twitter\",\"facebook\",\"angel investing\"],\"quote\":\"My favorite, because it's so full of great stories, is the Chris Sacca interview with Jason Calacanis\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.5},{\"vid\":\"Sg4U4r_AgJU\",\"title\":\"Computer Science - Brian Kernighan on successful language design\",\"channel\":\"University of Nottingham\",\"summary\":\"Brian Kernighan gives a talk at the University of Nottingham on how to succeed in language design without really trying. He draws on his experience with awk and other languages.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN cites it as a practical talk on language design and for background on awk from one of its authors.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"programming language design\",\"categories\":[\"Programming language design\",\"Tech pioneers & interviews\"],\"tags\":[\"brian kernighan\",\"awk\",\"language design\",\"domain specific languages\",\"unix\"],\"quote\":\"This is an excellent talk on designing successful programming language by Brian Kernighan\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":2.5},{\"vid\":\"T6JFTmQCFHg\",\"title\":\"They're Made Out Of Meat\",\"channel\":\"Stephen O'Regan\",\"summary\":\"A 2004 short film adaptation of Terry Bisson's story \\\"They're Made Out of Meat\\\", in which aliens discuss the astonishing discovery of meat-based intelligent life. It stars Tom Noonan and Ben Bailey.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN points to it as the best filmed version of the story whenever aliens or the nature of intelligence come up.\",\"content_type\":\"other\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"science fiction short film\",\"categories\":[\"Literature & writing craft\",\"Film & TV clips\",\"Stand-up & sketch comedy\"],\"tags\":[\"terry bisson\",\"aliens\",\"short film\",\"sci-fi\",\"comedy\"],\"quote\":\"O'Reagan's short is the best take on it.\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.5},{\"vid\":\"TM9h89Vo_Qo\",\"title\":\"Scale By The Bay 2019: Bryan Cantrill, Was He Wright All Along? Software After Moore's Law\",\"channel\":\"FunctionalTV\",\"summary\":\"Bryan Cantrill's Scale By The Bay 2019 talk on software after Moore's Law. He covers the end of easy scaling and the move toward specialized, emerging architectures.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as a history and analysis of the end of scaling from a well-regarded systems speaker, and lists it among the best talks of 2019.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"end of moore's law\",\"categories\":[\"Computer architecture & CPUs\",\"Software performance & optimization\"],\"tags\":[\"bryan cantrill\",\"moore's law\",\"hardware architecture\",\"systems software\",\"scaling\"],\"quote\":\"Bryan Cantrill has a great talk on this and the end of scaling\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.5},{\"vid\":\"TcEWRykSgwE\",\"title\":\"Jordan Peterson - Pareto Distributions\",\"channel\":\"Bite-sized Philosophy\",\"summary\":\"A short clip of Jordan Peterson explaining Pareto distributions, using Soviet farm collectivization and the resulting famine as context. It also covers how outcomes concentrate in a few people.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN points to it for an accessible explanation of why wealth and creative output are so unevenly distributed.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"pareto distributions\",\"categories\":[\"Psychology & human behavior\",\"Labor, work & inequality\",\"Probability & game theory\"],\"tags\":[\"jordan peterson\",\"pareto distribution\",\"inequality\",\"power laws\",\"psychology\"],\"quote\":\"Jordan Peterson has an excellent lecture on describing this phenomenon.\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.5},{\"vid\":\"Tml94je2edk\",\"title\":\"Why Static Typing Came Back • Richard Feldman • GOTO 2022\",\"channel\":\"GOTO Conferences\",\"summary\":\"Richard Feldman's GOTO Copenhagen 2022 talk on why dynamic languages like Python, Ruby, PHP and JavaScript rose in the 1990s and why each now has a popular static type checker. It argues that modern static typing recovers the advantages that made dynamic languages attractive.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters cite it as a clear account of the trade-offs between static and dynamic typing and why the industry is swinging back to types.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"static vs dynamic typing\",\"categories\":[\"Programming language design\",\"Python, Ruby & scripting languages\",\"Web development\"],\"tags\":[\"static typing\",\"dynamic languages\",\"gradual typing\",\"programming language history\",\"type checkers\"],\"quote\":\"It gives the major reasons why one would prefer one over the other\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.5},{\"vid\":\"TneLO5TdW_M\",\"title\":\"Rick Reed - WhatsApp: Half a billion unsuspecting FreeBSD users\",\"channel\":\"iX\",\"summary\":\"Rick Reed of WhatsApp speaks at MeetBSD about running a messaging service on FreeBSD and Erlang at huge scale. He covers the stack, tuning and the challenges of millions of connections per host.\",\"why_hn\":\"Pointed to as a rare look at how a tiny engineering team ran WhatsApp's massive scale on FreeBSD and Erlang.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"advanced\",\"primary_topic\":\"scaling messaging infrastructure\",\"categories\":[\"Distributed systems\",\"Linux & open-source ecosystems\",\"Cloud, DevOps & reliability\"],\"tags\":[\"whatsapp\",\"freebsd\",\"erlang\",\"scalability\",\"rick reed\"],\"quote\":\"Their technology stack (FreeBSD/Erlang) is pretty interesting, more info here: 2014 talks by Rick Reed\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.5},{\"vid\":\"TrfD3pC0VSs\",\"title\":\"illumos day 2014: The Dream is Alive! Running Linux containers on an illumos kernel\",\"channel\":\"illumos\",\"summary\":\"Bryan Cantrill's illumos Day 2014 talk on running Linux containers on an illumos kernel via LX-branded zones. He covers emulating Linux syscalls on SmartOS and compares Linux and Solaris-derived systems.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN points to it as an entertaining deep dive on Linux emulation on SmartOS and on how the Solaris kernel and libc are tightly coupled.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"advanced\",\"primary_topic\":\"linux emulation on illumos\",\"categories\":[\"Operating systems & kernels\",\"Cloud, DevOps & reliability\",\"Linux & open-source ecosystems\"],\"tags\":[\"bryan cantrill\",\"smartos\",\"lx zones\",\"containers\",\"syscalls\"],\"quote\":\"I really enjoyed this talk[1] by Bryan Cantrill\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.5}",
    ",{\"vid\":\"U6qZPx4uD0g\",\"title\":\"EEVblog #555 - 555 Timer Kit\",\"channel\":\"EEVblog\",\"summary\":\"Dave Jones builds the Evil Mad Scientist discrete-component 555 timer kit, then tests it and explains the internal circuitry of the 555 at transistor level. He finishes with scope waveforms.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as a deep, hands-on explanation of how a 555 timer works inside.\",\"content_type\":\"tutorial\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"555 timer circuits\",\"categories\":[\"Electronics & circuit design\",\"Embedded systems & DIY electronics\"],\"tags\":[\"555 timer\",\"electronics\",\"soldering\",\"analog circuits\",\"eevblog\"],\"quote\":\"Please watch this clip as Dave dives deep into how a simple 555 chip works at the transistor level\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.5},{\"vid\":\"UDtlvZGmHYk\",\"title\":\"Spider With Three Super Powers | The Hunt | BBC Earth\",\"channel\":\"BBC Earth\",\"summary\":\"A BBC Earth clip from The Hunt showing Portia jumping spiders, which leap many times their body length and ambush prey larger than themselves.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as striking footage of how cleverly jumping spiders plan their attacks.\",\"content_type\":\"documentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"jumping spiders\",\"categories\":[\"Animals & nature\"],\"tags\":[\"portia spider\",\"bbc earth\",\"animal behavior\",\"spider intelligence\",\"nature\"],\"quote\":\"Well worth the watch if it’s new to you! Quite the perspective shifter\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":2.5},{\"vid\":\"UN8bJb8biZU\",\"title\":\"SLAPP Suits: Last Week Tonight with John Oliver (HBO)\",\"channel\":\"LastWeekTonight\",\"summary\":\"John Oliver explains SLAPP suits, lawsuits designed to silence critics through legal costs, after his show's legal fight with coal executive Bob Murray. It covers how such suits work and why state anti-SLAPP laws matter.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it whenever legal threats against critics or open-source authors come up, often as the best accessible primer on SLAPP suits.\",\"content_type\":\"commentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"slapp lawsuits\",\"categories\":[\"Law, policing & justice\",\"Free speech, censorship & civil liberties\",\"Media, journalism & propaganda\"],\"tags\":[\"slapp\",\"free speech\",\"john oliver\",\"defamation\",\"legal threats\"],\"quote\":\"In my opinion, it's the best one they've ever done with an absolutely magnificent ending\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.5},{\"vid\":\"UOHURuAf5iY\",\"title\":\"What is Concrete?\",\"channel\":\"Practical Engineering\",\"summary\":\"Practical Engineering gives a short introduction to concrete, covering the difference between concrete and cement, the ingredients, and reinforcement. It includes a hydraulic press demonstration of compressive strength.\",\"why_hn\":\"Commenters link it as an approachable explanation of concrete basics in threads about Roman concrete, construction and sand supply.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"concrete and civil engineering\",\"categories\":[\"Civil engineering & construction\",\"Materials science\"],\"tags\":[\"concrete\",\"cement\",\"reinforced concrete\",\"construction materials\",\"practical engineering\"],\"quote\":\"You're going to love this playlist from Practical Engineering [1].\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.5},{\"vid\":\"UQuIVsNzqDk\",\"title\":\"This Invention Made Disney MILLIONS, but Then They LOST It!\",\"channel\":\"Corridor Crew\",\"summary\":\"Corridor Crew tracks down Disney's lost sodium vapor prism process, used for Mary Poppins-era transparency mattes. They explain how a narrow sodium wavelength and a beam splitter enabled better compositing than green screen.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as a fun, informative rediscovery of a lost film-effects technique.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"sodium vapor compositing\",\"categories\":[\"Animation & visual effects\",\"Tech company stories & documentaries\"],\"tags\":[\"vfx\",\"disney\",\"green screen\",\"mary poppins\",\"beam splitter\"],\"quote\":\"is a fantastic watch.\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.5},{\"vid\":\"UmIgNfp-MdI\",\"title\":\"Microsoft \\\"Courier\\\" secret tablet\",\"channel\":\"Neowin\",\"summary\":\"A short concept video of Microsoft's unreleased Courier, a dual-screen tablet designed as a digital notebook with clippings and annotation. It was first leaked by Gizmodo in 2009.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN treats it as a gold-standard concept demo and a what-might-have-been for dual-screen and digital notebook devices.\",\"content_type\":\"demo\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"tablet design concepts\",\"categories\":[\"Consumer devices & gadgets\",\"UX & UI design\",\"Tech company stories & documentaries\"],\"tags\":[\"microsoft courier\",\"dual screen\",\"tablet\",\"digital notebook\",\"concept demo\"],\"quote\":\"It has also been my personal gold standard of a superb video demo.\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.5},{\"vid\":\"UyFF4cpMVag\",\"title\":\"Soyuz Launch Escape Tower System\",\"channel\":\"Bruno Leal\",\"summary\":\"Short footage of the 1983 Soyuz T-10-1 pad fire, in which the launch escape tower pulled the crew away seconds before the rocket exploded.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN cites it as the one dramatic real-world proof that a launch escape system works.\",\"content_type\":\"news\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"launch escape systems\",\"categories\":[\"Space exploration & spaceflight\",\"Military history & warfare\"],\"tags\":[\"soyuz t-10-1\",\"launch abort\",\"rocket explosion\",\"spaceflight safety\",\"escape tower\"],\"quote\":\"here is a neat video of a launch escape system working as intended during Soyuz T-10-1\",\"recommenders\":1,\"score\":2.5},{\"vid\":\"V6VP-2aIcSc\",\"title\":\"Object-Oriented Programming is Garbage: 3800 SLOC example\",\"channel\":\"Brian Will\",\"summary\":\"Brian Will critiques object-oriented programming and rewrites a roughly 3800-line OO program in a procedural style, cutting code and complexity. It is part of a series of OOP critiques.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as a concrete, worked example of the case against heavy OOP rather than abstract argument.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"object-oriented programming critique\",\"categories\":[\"Software architecture & design\",\"Software practice, open source & careers\"],\"tags\":[\"oop\",\"procedural programming\",\"refactoring\",\"software design\",\"code complexity\"],\"quote\":\"I recommend the whole series\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.5},{\"vid\":\"VHWUCX6osgM\",\"title\":\"Steve Jobs Stanford Commencement Speech 2005 high definition.flv\",\"channel\":\"Jed Joe\",\"summary\":\"Steve Jobs's 2005 Stanford commencement address, in which he tells three stories about connecting the dots, love and loss, and death, including his remarks on typography.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN people recommend it as personal inspiration for perseverance and for keeping perspective by remembering mortality.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"commencement speech\",\"categories\":[\"Productivity & self-improvement\",\"Tech pioneers & interviews\",\"Graphic design & typography\"],\"tags\":[\"steve jobs\",\"stanford\",\"motivation\",\"career advice\",\"mortality\"],\"quote\":\"I suggest you see Steve Jobs' Stanford Commencement Speech (2005)\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.5},{\"vid\":\"VPjODKUxV5g\",\"title\":\"The Vegas Loop Is Getting Progressively More Stupid\",\"channel\":\"Ray Delahanty | CityNerd\",\"summary\":\"CityNerd critiques the Las Vegas Loop, the Boring Company's tunnel transit system, arguing its design and operation are poor. The video covers how the system actually runs.\",\"why_hn\":\"Used as the go-to critical, first-hand overview of the Vegas Loop in threads about the Boring Company.\",\"content_type\":\"commentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"las vegas loop transit\",\"categories\":[\"Transit, cities & urban planning\",\"Civil engineering & construction\"],\"tags\":[\"boring company\",\"urbanism\",\"transit\",\"las vegas\",\"tunnels\"],\"quote\":\"This video gives a first hand experience that seems to cover everything\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.5},{\"vid\":\"VdqhHKjepiE\",\"title\":\"RimWorld: Contrarian, Ridiculous, and Impossible Game Design Methods\",\"channel\":\"GDC Festival of Gaming\",\"summary\":\"RimWorld creator Tynan Sylvester explains at GDC 2017 how Ludeon framed the game as a story generator rather than a game. He covers the design methods that came out of that frame.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as an accessible talk on emergent narrative and on how RimWorld was designed, and cited in threads on simulation games.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"game design\",\"categories\":[\"Game design\"],\"tags\":[\"rimworld\",\"emergent narrative\",\"story generator\",\"gdc\",\"simulation games\"],\"quote\":\"Strong recommend, if only to understand how RimWorld was designed and built.\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.5},{\"vid\":\"W4SxKWwFhA0\",\"title\":\"Browser hacking: Let's make \\\"Cookie Clicker\\\" playable in Ladybird!\",\"channel\":\"Andreas Kling\",\"summary\":\"In a long live-coding session, Andreas Kling works on making the Cookie Clicker game playable in the Ladybird browser from SerenityOS. He tracks down bugs in the browser engine and fixes them.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN loves the Browser hacking series for its clear debugging method of reducing bugs to minimal reproductions, which people say influenced their own testing.\",\"content_type\":\"tutorial\",\"level\":\"advanced\",\"primary_topic\":\"browser engine debugging\",\"categories\":[\"Web development\",\"Software practice, open source & careers\",\"Compilers, interpreters & program analysis\"],\"tags\":[\"ladybird\",\"serenityos\",\"c++\",\"browser engine\",\"live coding\"],\"quote\":\"At one point, he deletes half the HTML file to isolate where in the site the problematic code is.\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.5},{\"vid\":\"W88Sact1kws\",\"title\":\"How Wolves Change Rivers [REMASTERED HD] - George Monbiot\",\"channel\":\"Sustainable Human\",\"summary\":\"A short narrated video describing how reintroducing wolves to Yellowstone triggered a trophic cascade that changed wildlife, vegetation and even river courses. This is a remastered HD version of George Monbiot's viral piece.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN cites it as a compelling example of unforeseen ecosystem effects and rewilding.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"trophic cascades\",\"categories\":[\"Animals & nature\",\"Environment, agriculture & food systems\"],\"tags\":[\"yellowstone\",\"wolves\",\"rewilding\",\"ecosystems\",\"keystone species\"],\"quote\":\"probably one of the most fascinating videos on YouTube\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.5},{\"vid\":\"WB2-2j9e4co\",\"title\":\"Extreme transformer home in Hong Kong: Gary Chang's 24 rooms in 1\",\"channel\":\"Kirsten Dirksen\",\"summary\":\"Kirsten Dirksen visits architect Gary Chang's 32 square meter Hong Kong apartment, which he has redesigned over decades. Sliding walls and transformable furniture turn it into 24 different room configurations.\",\"why_hn\":\"Held up as the ultimate example of small-space design and a beautiful compact home.\",\"content_type\":\"documentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"small space design\",\"categories\":[\"Visual arts, photography & architecture\",\"Home, DIY & practical skills\",\"Product & industrial design\"],\"tags\":[\"tiny apartment\",\"hong kong\",\"transformable furniture\",\"architecture\",\"gary chang\"],\"quote\":\"I can't recommend Kirsten Dirksen's channel enough to people interested in architecture, energy efficiency, tiny homes\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.5},{\"vid\":\"WRdJCFEqFTU\",\"title\":\"Joscha: Computational Meta-Psychology\",\"channel\":\"media.ccc.de\",\"summary\":\"Joscha Bach's 32C3 talk applies computational theories of mind to questions of meaning, emotion, and love, asking how computation can explain irrational-seeming human experience.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as one of the best bridges between mind and computation, and as a sample of Bach's thinking on AI and philosophy.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"computational theories of mind\",\"categories\":[\"Consciousness & cognition\",\"Psychology & human behavior\"],\"tags\":[\"joscha bach\",\"consciousness\",\"ai\",\"cognitive science\",\"ccc\"],\"quote\":\"Recommend watching this video on Computational Meta-Psychology\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.5},{\"vid\":\"WVTWCPoUt8w\",\"title\":\"The Origins of Linux—Linus Torvalds\",\"channel\":\"Computer History Museum\",\"summary\":\"Linus Torvalds tells the story of Linux in a 2001 talk at the Computer History Museum, from writing code as a graduate student in Helsinki in the early 1990s to becoming an icon of open source. It covers the project's origins, its growth, and his views on licensing and kernel design.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN points to it as the long first-person account of Linux history, including Linus on the GPL's role and on microkernels.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"linux history\",\"categories\":[\"Linux & open-source ecosystems\",\"Tech pioneers & interviews\",\"Operating systems & kernels\"],\"tags\":[\"linus torvalds\",\"linux\",\"open source\",\"gpl\",\"kernel design\"],\"quote\":\"There's a great (long) video about Linus and Linux history from 2001. Watch the whole thing if you can\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.5},{\"vid\":\"Wi_nFz1CJSI\",\"title\":\"Iowa Swine Day 2022: Agriculture at the End of the World\",\"channel\":\"Iowa Pork Industry Center\",\"summary\":\"Peter Zeihan speaks at Iowa Swine Day 2022 on geopolitics, the end of globalization and agriculture. He argues that supply chains, energy and farm inputs are all about to be disrupted.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended for its bleak take on globalization, energy and food supply, though some commenters question how objective it is.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"geopolitics and deglobalization\",\"categories\":[\"Geopolitics & foreign policy\",\"Environment, agriculture & food systems\",\"Business & economics (other)\"],\"tags\":[\"peter zeihan\",\"globalization\",\"agriculture\",\"energy crisis\",\"supply chains\"],\"quote\":\"The audio is a little rough, but it's worth the watch (in my opinion).\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.5},{\"vid\":\"Wp7GAKLSGnI\",\"title\":\"Oral History of Donald Knuth Part 1\",\"channel\":\"Computer History Museum\",\"summary\":\"A roughly three-hour Computer History Museum oral history interview of Donald Knuth by Edward Feigenbaum, recorded in 2007. Knuth covers his family, music, early programming, his thesis, The Art of Computer Programming, and the detour into TeX and METAFONT.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as a superb, personal long-form look at Knuth's life and work habits.\",\"content_type\":\"interview\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"donald knuth biography\",\"categories\":[\"Tech pioneers & interviews\",\"History of computing\"],\"tags\":[\"knuth\",\"oral history\",\"tex\",\"art of computer programming\",\"computing history\"],\"quote\":\"It's a _really_ long interview, +7 hours. But it's also superb.\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.5},{\"vid\":\"X1T3IQ4N-3g\",\"title\":\"CppCon 2014: Jeff Preshing \\\"How Ubisoft Develops Games for Multicore - Before and After C++11\\\"\",\"channel\":\"CppCon\",\"summary\":\"CppCon 2014 talk by Jeff Preshing on how Ubisoft Montreal developed games for multicore hardware, before and after C++11 concurrency support. It goes into atomics and lock-free programming in practice.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as a clear, practical introduction to atomics and lock-free programming in C++.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"lock-free programming\",\"categories\":[\"C, C++, Rust, Go & systems languages\",\"Game programming & engines\",\"Software performance & optimization\"],\"tags\":[\"c++11\",\"atomics\",\"multicore\",\"game engines\",\"concurrency\"],\"quote\":\"Jeff Preshing gave a really good introduction to atomics at CppCon 2014.[1]\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.5},{\"vid\":\"X29lF43mUlo\",\"title\":\"George Carlin - Germs, Immune System\",\"channel\":\"Geen Naam\",\"summary\":\"A clip from George Carlin's \\\"You Are All Diseased\\\" special in which he riffs on germs, the immune system and white blood cells. It argues that modern obsession with cleanliness is misplaced.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN brings it up as comic relief and as a funny take when immunity, dirt and germs come up.\",\"content_type\":\"comedy\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"germs and immunity comedy\",\"categories\":[\"Stand-up & sketch comedy\"],\"tags\":[\"george carlin\",\"stand-up\",\"germs\",\"immune system\"],\"quote\":\"You've reminded me of this George Carlin rant\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.5},{\"vid\":\"XtMmeAjQTXc\",\"title\":\"Episode 1: Introduction - The Mechanical Universe\",\"channel\":\"caltech\",\"summary\":\"The first episode of Caltech's The Mechanical Universe, a 52-part introductory university physics series. David Goodstein introduces ideas from Copernicus to Newton, linking the physics of the heavens and the earth with computer animation.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN rates the series as one of the best physics lecture courses on YouTube, with an engaging format and historical context.\",\"content_type\":\"course\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"introductory physics\",\"categories\":[\"Fundamental physics\",\"University lectures & courses\"],\"tags\":[\"mechanical universe\",\"david goodstein\",\"caltech\",\"newtonian mechanics\",\"physics lectures\"],\"quote\":\"Hands down best: David Goodstein, CalTech,\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.5},{\"vid\":\"YNTARSM-Fjc\",\"title\":\"Yacht Rock 1 HD\",\"channel\":\"Hunter Stair\",\"summary\":\"The first episode of the Yacht Rock web series, a comedic parody about the smooth soft-rock musicians of the late 1970s and early 80s. It is a short, scripted sketch set at Koko's Boat House.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters point the uninitiated to it as a beloved parody series, often in threads that mention yacht rock.\",\"content_type\":\"comedy\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"yacht rock parody\",\"categories\":[\"Satire & parody\",\"Music performance & songs\"],\"tags\":[\"yacht rock\",\"parody\",\"web series\",\"soft rock\",\"music comedy\"],\"quote\":\"Also don't miss this wonderful parody series\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.5},{\"vid\":\"YXylqtEQ0tk\",\"title\":\"How Google's Self-Driving Car Works\",\"channel\":\"IEEE Spectrum\",\"summary\":\"Part 2 of the IROS 2011 keynote by Sebastian Thrun and Chris Urmson on Google's self-driving car. It shows how the car perceives its surroundings with a lidar point cloud and recognizes traffic lights and pedestrians. The audio is poor because it was recorded on a pocket camcorder.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as a technical talk aimed at a real engineering audience, with live demos of perception and pre-mapped roads.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"self-driving cars\",\"categories\":[\"Self-driving & autonomous vehicles\",\"Computer vision, speech & NLP\"],\"tags\":[\"google self-driving car\",\"lidar\",\"sebastian thrun\",\"autonomous vehicles\",\"perception\"],\"quote\":\"The whole talk is awesome and a presentation for an actual technical audience, too.\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.5},{\"vid\":\"YcUycQoz0zg\",\"title\":\"How We Diagnosed and Fixed the 2023 Voyager 1 Anomaly from 15 Billion Miles Away\",\"channel\":\"Flight Software Workshop\",\"summary\":\"JPL's David Cummings presents at the 2025 Flight Software Workshop how the team diagnosed and fixed the 2023 Voyager 1 anomaly from billions of miles away. It walks through the flight computer and the memory fix.\",\"why_hn\":\"Commenters recommend it for the remarkable remote reverse engineering done on decades-old hardware with no simulator.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"advanced\",\"primary_topic\":\"spacecraft flight software\",\"categories\":[\"Space exploration & spaceflight\",\"Testing, debugging & developer tools\"],\"tags\":[\"voyager 1\",\"jpl\",\"flight software\",\"reverse engineering\",\"debugging\"],\"quote\":\"Blows my mind of the reverse engineering done to achieve this\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.5},{\"vid\":\"Ygty9HxhFK4\",\"title\":\"Diatoms: Tiny Factories You Can See From Space\",\"channel\":\"Journey to the Microcosmos\",\"summary\":\"An episode of Journey to the Microcosmos, hosted by Hank Green, about diatoms, single-celled algae with glass shells. It covers their role in producing a large share of Earth's oxygen and their uses in beer filtering, paint and kitty litter.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters recommend the series as an excellent, accessible introduction to microscopic life, using this episode as the diatom primer.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"diatoms and microbiology\",\"categories\":[\"Biology & biotechnology\",\"Animals & nature\"],\"tags\":[\"diatoms\",\"microscopy\",\"microorganisms\",\"algae\",\"oxygen\"],\"quote\":\"I recently learned about them from an excellent Journey to the Microcosmos video\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.5},{\"vid\":\"Yze1YAz_LYM\",\"title\":\"Dan Britt - Orbits and Ice Ages: The History of Climate\",\"channel\":\"TheIHMC\",\"summary\":\"Dan Britt lectures on the history of climate, covering orbital cycles, ice ages and conditions millions of years ago. It frames current climate debate with deep-time context.\",\"why_hn\":\"Strongly recommended as a clear explanation of 50-60 million years of climate history that clears up confusion.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"climate history\",\"categories\":[\"Climate science & climate change\",\"Geology & earth science\"],\"tags\":[\"ice ages\",\"orbital cycles\",\"paleoclimate\",\"geologic time\",\"climate\"],\"quote\":\"For anyone interested in finding out more about the last 50-60 million years of climate history I would strongly recommend this talk\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":2.5},{\"vid\":\"Z0oFJ2jbkDI\",\"title\":\"Earth-cooled, shipping container underground CA home for 30K\",\"channel\":\"Kirsten Dirksen\",\"summary\":\"Kirsten Dirksen profiles Steve Rees, who buried two shipping containers to build an earth-cooled, off-grid 640 sq ft home in Northern California for about \$30,000, solar included.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as an example of earth-sheltered housing and as part of a channel HN loves for architecture and efficiency.\",\"content_type\":\"documentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"earth-sheltered housing\",\"categories\":[\"Home, DIY & practical skills\",\"Power grid, heating & buildings\",\"Civil engineering & construction\"],\"tags\":[\"shipping container home\",\"off grid\",\"underground home\",\"passive cooling\",\"tiny homes\"],\"quote\":\"I can't recommend Kirsten Dirksen's channel enough to people interested in architecture, energy efficiency, tiny homes\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.5},{\"vid\":\"Z2o_Sp2-aBo\",\"title\":\"Build an electroluminescent glass panel display -- an Apollo DSKY\",\"channel\":\"Applied Science\",\"summary\":\"Applied Science builds a replica Apollo DSKY using an electroluminescent driver circuit and screen-printed conductive ink, with source and PCB files published.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN people recommend it as a fantastic maker project among Apollo anniversary builds.\",\"content_type\":\"tutorial\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"electroluminescent displays\",\"categories\":[\"Electronics & circuit design\",\"Embedded systems & DIY electronics\",\"Space exploration & spaceflight\"],\"tags\":[\"apollo dsky\",\"electroluminescent\",\"screen printing\",\"diy electronics\",\"high voltage\"],\"quote\":\"This video of building a custom Apollo electroluminescent glass panel DSKY display is also fantastic!\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.5},{\"vid\":\"ZlfIVEy_YOA\",\"title\":\"Stanford Professor Andrei Linde celebrates physics breakthrough\",\"channel\":\"Stanford\",\"summary\":\"A short Stanford video in which Chao-Lin Kuo surprises Professor Andrei Linde with BICEP2 evidence supporting cosmic inflation, the first apparent images of primordial gravitational waves.\",\"why_hn\":\"Shared for its moving moment of scientific discovery, and later cited as a cautionary example after the BICEP2 result was retracted.\",\"content_type\":\"news\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"cosmic inflation discovery\",\"categories\":[\"Cosmology & astrophysics\"],\"tags\":[\"bicep2\",\"andrei linde\",\"gravitational waves\",\"cosmic inflation\",\"stanford\"],\"quote\":\"They made a nice video of the researcher surprising Prof Linde with the news\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.5},{\"vid\":\"_BNIkw4Ao9w\",\"title\":\"Here's Why Your Static Website Needs HTTPS\",\"channel\":\"Troy Hunt\",\"summary\":\"Troy Hunt argues that even static, non-secret websites need HTTPS, demonstrating attacks such as injected content and China's Great Cannon. It accompanies his blog post of the same name.\",\"why_hn\":\"Cited as a must-watch persuasive case for HTTPS everywhere.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"https for static sites\",\"categories\":[\"Security & hacking\",\"Cryptography\",\"Networking & internet infrastructure\"],\"tags\":[\"https\",\"web security\",\"tls\",\"great cannon\",\"man-in-the-middle\",\"troy hunt\"],\"quote\":\"The linked video is a must watch to those who still doubt HTTPS\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.5},{\"vid\":\"_Cm6EFYktRQ\",\"title\":\"BBS The.Documentary Part 4 - Fidonet\",\"channel\":\"bbsers\",\"summary\":\"Part 4 of the BBS: The Documentary series, covering FidoNet, the amateur network that linked dial-up bulletin board systems together to relay messages and files worldwide.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as a great documentary on FidoNet's rise, with interviews with figures like Tom Jennings.\",\"content_type\":\"documentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"fidonet and bbs history\",\"categories\":[\"History of computing\",\"Networking & internet infrastructure\"],\"tags\":[\"fidonet\",\"bbs\",\"tom jennings\",\"dial-up\",\"computing history\"],\"quote\":\"A great documentary on FidoNet's rise with superb music and universal themes\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.5},{\"vid\":\"__wn9zXFiy8\",\"title\":\"Tutorial: New and Updated - Everything You Always Wanted to Know About Optical Networking\",\"channel\":\"NANOG\",\"summary\":\"A two-hour NANOG tutorial by Richard Steenbergen aimed at network engineers, covering optical networking technologies. This is the updated 2017 edition of a recurring talk.\",\"why_hn\":\"Commenters call it an invaluable, approachable way to learn how optical networking actually works.\",\"content_type\":\"tutorial\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"optical networking\",\"categories\":[\"Networking & internet infrastructure\"],\"tags\":[\"optical networking\",\"dwdm\",\"fiber\",\"network engineering\",\"nanog\"],\"quote\":\"proved invaluable to me in learning optical networking\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.5},{\"vid\":\"_gXiVOmaVSo\",\"title\":\"Shawn Douglas - Nanoscale Instruments for Visualizing Small Proteins & Bret Victor - Dynamicland\",\"channel\":\"Foresight Institute\",\"summary\":\"A Foresight Institute workshop video with two talks: Shawn Douglas on nanoscale instruments for visualizing small proteins, and Bret Victor on Dynamicland. Victor's part shows biomolecular design built in his Realtalk system with physical objects.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN points to Bret Victor's segment as a recent, concrete demonstration of Dynamicland's ideas applied to a specific scientific domain.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"dynamicland\",\"categories\":[\"GUI, HCI & interface history\",\"Biology & biotechnology\",\"Tech pioneers & interviews\"],\"tags\":[\"bret victor\",\"dynamicland\",\"realtalk\",\"protein visualization\",\"physical computing\"],\"quote\":\"It shows just how far the Dynamicland concept can be pushed into a hyperspecific feature set customized to fit a single domain\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.5},{\"vid\":\"_zSjpIyMt0k\",\"title\":\"Mars DOS demo by Tim Clarke (1993)\",\"channel\":\"Sean Barrett\",\"summary\":\"A one-minute capture of Tim Clarke's 1993 'Mars' DOS demo, a roughly 5 KB program that renders a navigable terrain landscape in real time.\",\"why_hn\":\"Remembered fondly as a tiny, efficient demo that inspired graphics programmers and is a benchmark for modern terrain rendering.\",\"content_type\":\"demo\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"demoscene terrain rendering\",\"categories\":[\"Demoscene & retrocomputing\",\"Computer graphics & rendering\"],\"tags\":[\"demoscene\",\"dos\",\"voxel terrain\",\"size coding\",\"graphics\"],\"quote\":\"I saw Mars demo which hooked me forever on the path of graphics programming.\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.5},{\"vid\":\"_zfN9wnPvU0\",\"title\":\"AI Slop Is Destroying The Internet\",\"channel\":\"Kurzgesagt – In a Nutshell\",\"summary\":\"Kurzgesagt examines how AI-generated content is flooding the internet, from video and music to news and books. It looks at how this makes verifying what is real harder.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN uses it as a reference for AI slop, LLMs citing LLM output, and the growing difficulty of fact checking.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"ai slop\",\"categories\":[\"LLMs & generative AI\",\"Media, journalism & propaganda\",\"Futurism & technology and society\"],\"tags\":[\"ai slop\",\"misinformation\",\"llms\",\"fact checking\",\"internet\"],\"quote\":\"Kurzgesagt made a great video on how this stuff gets generated\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":2.5},{\"vid\":\"a1PtQ67urG4\",\"title\":\"Cambridge Ideas - Professor Risk\",\"channel\":\"Cambridge University\",\"summary\":\"David Spiegelhalter, Professor of the Public Understanding of Risk, uses statistics to weigh everyday risks such as bacon sandwiches and cycling. The six-minute piece introduces micromorts as a way to compare the risk of dying.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as a short, fun introduction to micromorts and thinking about risk.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"risk and statistics\",\"categories\":[\"Data science & statistics\",\"Probability & game theory\",\"Scientific method & science communication\"],\"tags\":[\"micromorts\",\"risk\",\"probability\",\"statistics\",\"uncertainty\"],\"quote\":\"Here is great video by Cambridge Ideas about the risk of dying from various activities (measured in micromorts)\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.5},{\"vid\":\"aNAtbYSxzuA\",\"title\":\"Hacker Interviews: Larry Wall\",\"channel\":\"The BXI\",\"summary\":\"Jonathan Schiefer interviews Larry Wall, creator of Perl, in a 33-minute conversation. Wall discusses his self-designed linguistics degree and how language and programming overlap.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as an enjoyable look at the hacker mindset and a thoughtful interview worth rewatching.\",\"content_type\":\"interview\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"larry wall and perl\",\"categories\":[\"Python, Ruby & scripting languages\",\"Tech pioneers & interviews\",\"Language & linguistics\"],\"tags\":[\"larry wall\",\"perl\",\"linguistics\",\"hacker culture\",\"programming languages\"],\"quote\":\"I really enjoyed this interview with Larry Wall (of Perl fame)\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.5},{\"vid\":\"aXvDEmo6uS4\",