exceptional whirlwind tour of the foundations of computer science.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"propositions as types\",\"categories\":[\"Logic & formal methods\",\"Programming language design\",\"Functional programming\"],\"tags\":[\"curry-howard\",\"type theory\",\"lambda calculus\",\"functional programming\",\"logic\"],\"quote\":\"This is a very good lecture series[4].\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.5},{\"vid\":\"agw-wlHGi0E\",\"title\":\"Dynamic Languages Wizards Series - Panel on Language Design\",\"channel\":\"Eduardo Cavazos\",\"summary\":\"A 2001 panel with Paul Graham, John Maeda, Jonathan Rees and Guy Steele on programming language design, part of the Dynamic Languages Wizards Series.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN users mine it for design wisdom and history from luminaries, and often quote Guy Steele's remarks.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"programming language design\",\"categories\":[\"Programming language design\",\"Functional programming\"],\"tags\":[\"language design\",\"lisp\",\"guy steele\",\"paul graham\",\"dynamic languages\"],\"quote\":\"I'd also highly recommend that anyone interested in this kind of thing listen to all three of the Dynamic Languages Wizards Series panels\",\"recommenders\":1,\"score\":2.5},{\"vid\":\"b9IkpUYlOx8\",\"title\":\"Super Mario World Ending Theme sight-read by Tom Brier\",\"channel\":\"Keeper1st\",\"summary\":\"Pianist Tom Brier sight-reads a piano arrangement of the Super Mario World ending theme, working from a first-draft score by Keeper1st. He takes some liberties with the score as he plays.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters recommend it as a showcase of remarkable sight-reading and a favorite when sight-reading or notation comes up.\",\"content_type\":\"music\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"piano sight-reading\",\"categories\":[\"Music performance & songs\",\"Game history & retro gaming\"],\"tags\":[\"video game music\",\"super mario\",\"piano\",\"sight-reading\",\"sheet music\"],\"quote\":\"He's a rare gem and a treat to the ears.\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.5},{\"vid\":\"bB340S0tGf8\",\"title\":\"Interview with an Agile Coach - Sprint1\",\"channel\":\"Kai Lentit\",\"summary\":\"Kai Lentit's comedy sketch parodying an interview with an agile coach, lampooning scrum, sprints and kanban jargon. It is a few minutes of programmer humor.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as a funny parody that resembles agile as practiced at large companies.\",\"content_type\":\"comedy\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"agile satire\",\"categories\":[\"Satire & parody\",\"Software practice, open source & careers\"],\"tags\":[\"agile\",\"scrum\",\"parody\",\"programmer humor\"],\"quote\":\"A funny enough YouTube video (comedy)\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.5},{\"vid\":\"bM0PmwOlifE\",\"title\":\"Black Hat 2013 - OPSEC Failures of Spies\",\"channel\":\"HackersOnBoard\",\"summary\":\"Matthew Cole's Black Hat 2013 talk on operational security failures by CIA operatives. It covers the Abu Omar case and how sloppy tradecraft with cell phones exposed officers through call records and metadata.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters cite it as a compelling demonstration that metadata identifies people, and as a great watch on sloppy CIA tradecraft.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"operational security\",\"categories\":[\"Security & hacking\",\"Privacy, surveillance & cybercrime\"],\"tags\":[\"opsec\",\"metadata\",\"cia\",\"surveillance\",\"black hat\"],\"quote\":\"This is one of the best videos I have ever seen countering this argument.\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.5},{\"vid\":\"baFaEvBywGc\",\"title\":\"Secret Life Of Machines 104 The Washing Machine\",\"channel\":\"Retrotv\",\"summary\":\"An episode of Tim Hunkin's The Secret Life of Machines that explains how a washing machine works, using models, cut-aways and animations. It was first aired in 1988.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as a humorous and clear explanation of everyday machines, including why washing machine balancing works.\",\"content_type\":\"documentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"how washing machines work\",\"categories\":[\"Mechanical & precision engineering\",\"Documentaries & storytelling\"],\"tags\":[\"tim hunkin\",\"secret life of machines\",\"appliances\",\"mechanical engineering\",\"tv series\"],\"quote\":\"Cartoonist Tim Hunkin explains how machines work, with lots and lots of models and cut-aways of real life devices.\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.5},{\"vid\":\"bnoUBfLxZz0\",\"title\":\"The Super-Fast Logistics of Delivering Blood By Drone\",\"channel\":\"Wendover Productions\",\"summary\":\"A Wendover Productions documentary-style video on how Zipline delivers blood and medical supplies by drone in Rwanda. It covers the catapult launch, parachute drop and the logistics behind it.\",\"why_hn\":\"Offered as a clear, well-made look at a real, working drone-delivery system, often in answer to skepticism about delivery drones.\",\"content_type\":\"documentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"drone delivery logistics\",\"categories\":[\"Robotics\",\"Business & economics (other)\",\"Medicine & infectious disease\"],\"tags\":[\"zipline\",\"rwanda\",\"drones\",\"medical logistics\",\"last-mile delivery\"],\"quote\":\"Wendover Productions did a great video detailing this a few years back.\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.5},{\"vid\":\"bwDrHqNZ9lo\",\"title\":\"Geographical limit between Balkan and Central Europe\",\"channel\":\"kunc\",\"summary\":\"A one-minute clip of Slavoj Zizek showing what he calls the official geographical limit between the Balkans and Central Europe, standing at a river in Ljubljana. It is a short, absurd and funny piece of cultural geography.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters recommend it as one of the best short clips on YouTube for illustrating Balkan versus Central European identity.\",\"content_type\":\"commentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"balkan identity\",\"categories\":[\"Magic, puzzles & curiosities\",\"Philosophy & ethics\",\"Arts & entertainment (other)\"],\"tags\":[\"zizek\",\"balkans\",\"central europe\",\"slovenia\",\"geography\"],\"quote\":\"this short clip [1] of Zizek explaining the geography that separates Central Europe from the Balkans is one of the best things on YT\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.5},{\"vid\":\"c52QhiXsmyI\",\"title\":\"REBL - Stuart Halloway\",\"channel\":\"ClojureTV\",\"summary\":\"Stuart Halloway introduces REBL, a graphical, interactive tool for browsing Clojure data, extracted from Datomic tools. The talk is from Clojure/conj 2018.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as a must-watch Conj talk and as the intro for people who haven't seen REBL, often compared to Jupyter-style tooling.\",\"content_type\":\"demo\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"clojure data browsing tools\",\"categories\":[\"Functional programming\",\"Testing, debugging & developer tools\"],\"tags\":[\"clojure\",\"rebl\",\"datomic\",\"repl\",\"developer tools\"],\"quote\":\"People who have not heard of the REBL, can check out this video\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.5},{\"vid\":\"cBDJDZAo41c\",\"title\":\"What Makes This Song Great? \\\"Into You\\\" MAX MARTIN\",\"channel\":\"Rick Beato\",\"summary\":\"Rick Beato analyzes the music and production of Max Martin through Ariana Grande's Into You, breaking down its layering and songwriting craft.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN points to it as an explanation of how Max Martin builds hit songs.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"pop music production\",\"categories\":[\"Music theory, synthesis & production\",\"Music performance & songs\"],\"tags\":[\"max martin\",\"songwriting\",\"music production\",\"rick beato\",\"pop music\"],\"quote\":\"My favourite video of his is about the most accomplished songwriter most people have never heard of - Max Martin\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":2.5},{\"vid\":\"cQWlnTyOSig\",\"title\":\"The Chinese Student Crisis\",\"channel\":\"PolyMatter\",\"summary\":\"PolyMatter examines how universities, especially in the West, depend on foreign and Chinese student tuition and how rankings and incentives distort institutions around that money.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters recommend it in college-ranking and higher-ed threads as an explanation of why rankings are a hustle for foreign student revenue.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"university rankings\",\"categories\":[\"Education & learning\",\"Geopolitics & foreign policy\",\"Business & economics (other)\"],\"tags\":[\"higher education\",\"international students\",\"rankings\",\"china\",\"tuition\"],\"quote\":\"Polymatter recently had a great video on the way the college ranking system is a hustle for foreign student money\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.5},{\"vid\":\"cVaTc15plVs\",\"title\":\"Hjernevask - Brainwashing (Eng Sub) Part 1 - The Gender Equality Paradox\",\"channel\":\"The Bookman\",\"summary\":\"First episode of the Norwegian documentary series Hjernevask, in which Harald Eia interviews researchers on the nature versus nurture debate. It focuses on the gender equality paradox and gender differences in career choice.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters cite it, especially during the Google memo debate, as expert testimony on biological contributions to gender differences.\",\"content_type\":\"documentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"gender differences\",\"categories\":[\"Psychology & human behavior\",\"Social issues & culture\",\"Evolution & genetics\"],\"tags\":[\"nature vs nurture\",\"gender equality paradox\",\"norwegian\",\"sociology\",\"hjernevask\"],\"quote\":\"\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":2.5},{\"vid\":\"d6iQrh2TK98\",\"title\":\"Why is this number everywhere?\",\"channel\":\"Veritasium\",\"summary\":\"Veritasium explores why the number 37 turns up so often, drawing on Tom Magliery's collection of 37 sightings and a poll of about 200,000 people asked to pick a random number. It covers how humans choose numbers and why 37 stands out.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN cites it in LLM randomness and number-picking threads as evidence that humans are also biased and predictable when asked for random numbers.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"human randomness bias\",\"categories\":[\"Probability & game theory\",\"Psychology & human behavior\",\"Recreational & visual math\"],\"tags\":[\"number 37\",\"randomness\",\"probability\",\"veritasium\",\"psychology\"],\"quote\":\"humans are fairly predictable when trying to come up with random numbers, for example, have a look at this Veritasium video\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.5},{\"vid\":\"dPK6t7echuA\",\"title\":\"\\\"How to Think about Parallel Programming: Not!\\\" - Guy L. Steele Jr. (Strange Loop 2010)\",\"channel\":\"Strange Loop Conference\",\"summary\":\"Guy Steele's Strange Loop 2010 talk argues that programmers should not have to think about parallelism, and that languages should support algorithms built on independence and divide-and-conquer. He also recounts fitting a program onto a single punch card.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as a fantastic talk, valued both for its parallelism ideas and for the punch-card story.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"parallel programming\",\"categories\":[\"Programming language design\",\"Functional programming\",\"History of computing\"],\"tags\":[\"guy steele\",\"parallelism\",\"functional programming\",\"language design\",\"punch cards\"],\"quote\":\"It's a fantastic talk in its own right\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.5},{\"vid\":\"dT9y-KQbqi4\",\"title\":\"How I hacked a hardware crypto wallet and recovered \$2 million\",\"channel\":\"Joe Grand\",\"summary\":\"Joe Grand recounts being hired to hack a Trezor One hardware wallet and recover \$2 million of cryptocurrency. He describes three months of experimentation, voltage glitching, failures and high-stakes attempts with only one chance to get it right.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as a gripping real-world hardware hacking story and a showcase of Joe Grand's skill.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"hardware wallet hacking\",\"categories\":[\"Security & hacking\",\"Cryptography\",\"Embedded systems & DIY electronics\"],\"tags\":[\"trezor\",\"fault injection\",\"voltage glitching\",\"joe grand\",\"cryptocurrency recovery\"],\"quote\":\"a cool video about someone hacking a Trezor One. Very enjoyable watch.\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.5},{\"vid\":\"dX649lnKAU0\",\"title\":\"How Analog Color TV Works:  The Beginnings\",\"channel\":\"Technology Connections\",\"summary\":\"Technology Connections explains the early history of analog color television, from the 1954 start of modern color broadcasting, and how shadow masks work in CRTs. It is the first in a series on how analog color TV works.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters recommend it as an especially good explanation of analog TV and CRT shadow masks.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"analog color television\",\"categories\":[\"Audio & video engineering\",\"Electronics & circuit design\"],\"tags\":[\"color tv\",\"crt\",\"shadow mask\",\"ntsc\",\"technology connections\"],\"quote\":\"This one on \\\"how analog tv works\\\" I thought was especially good.\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.5}",
